All artifacts verified on disk. Emitting structured blocks.

---

**STEP 1 — DELTA**

[NEW] `gateway.sumup.com` is the SumUp **hosted-fields / card-entry iframe** — 546 B shell titled `hostedfields`, `HostedForm.init(window, window.parent || window.top)`, plus `hosted.js` (26,839 B, sha256 `1302f1d6…0873`) and the full Web SDK at `/sdk.js` (291,877 B). KB recorded it only as "non-Next 404" one cycle ago. It is a PCI-scoped payment surface the program had never analysed.

[NEW] **No framing protection** on the card-entry frame: no `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy` (no `frame-ancestors`), no `Referrer-Policy` on `/` or `/hosted.js`. Framable by any origin.

[NEW] **The defect** — the `postMessage` handler never reads `event.origin`. It destructures `{data, source}` only, gates on `data.type==="SumUpCard"` (an attacker-controlled JSON string) plus a self-echo `source!==toWindow` filter that short-circuits to `false` when `toWindow` is unset. `sendTo` posts with hardcoded targetOrigin `"*"`.

[NEW] Full message protocol recovered: `input--initialize`, `input--change`, `input--recognize`, `input--focus`, `input--blur`, `form--submit`, `form--submit-sent`, `form--on-result`, `form--on-error`, `form--invalid`, `form--invalid-input`, `aux--metric-track`, `messenger--unmount`.

[NEW] **Live PoC 1** — cross-origin dispatch. Attacker page on `https://127.0.0.1:8443` framing the shell, one `postMessage {type:"SumUpCard",message:"form--submit"}`. SumUp's own iframe console (`source: https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`): `Error while receiving message:  - form--submit Error: Unable to process checkout without a valid checkoutId` + `TAG: messenger_id`.

[NEW] **Live PoC 2** — live state-changing API call. `checkoutId` is read from `payment.checkoutId` (not top level). Chrome netlog: `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 HTTP/1.1`, `initiator: https://gateway.sumup.com`, `network_isolation_key: "https://127.0.0.1 https://sumup.com"`, `cors_preflight_policy: consider_preflight`, headers `X-Sumup-Correlation-Id: <attacker id>`, `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: poc-invalid-session-0000`, `Sumup-Product-Origin: undefined`. API base hardcoded `https://api.sumup.com`.

[CHANGED] `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` = 403 (Vercel WAF) externally, so the real checkout page cannot be framed; `gateway.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` = 404.

[CHANGED] Response direction **not** exploitable as implemented — `send()` does `t.postMessage(e, n||"*")` with `n = document.referrer` (a full URL), which throws. Tested with and without referrer suppression; nothing delivered. Exfiltration is not claimed.

[CHANGED] `web.sumup.com` still connection-timeout on 443, A 77.246.42.130, no CNAME — the 21-day-old Rackspace takeover lead is unchanged and dormant.

[CHANGED] `api.sumup.com` / `auth.sam-app.ro` not re-probed; prior verification stands per the 27-cycle zero-yield lesson.

**STEP 2 — PRIORITIZE**

[PRIO] gateway.sumup.com hosted-fields messenger + frame,8.60,attack_surface:9 business_value:10 tech_exposure:9 gate_ease:10 cloud_surface:7 freshness:10
[PRIO] mcp.sumup.com/mcp,8.15,attack_surface:7 business_value:8 tech_exposure:9 gate_ease:9 cloud_surface:7 freshness:10
[PRIO] auth.sam-app.ro/oauth2/register,6.90,attack_surface:7 business_value:6 tech_exposure:9 gate_ease:10 cloud_surface:5 freshness:3

**STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES**

[HYP] gateway.sumup.com hosted-fields frame accepts and acts on `form--submit` from any web origin, issuing an authenticated `PUT` to the production payments API with attacker-supplied checkout id, session id and payment payload
class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (hosted.js postMessage API + `/`, `/hosted.js`)
confidence: 88
reasoning: handler `const p=r=>{const{data:n,source:i}=r; ...}` never reads `event.origin`; only gates are `data.type==="SumUpCard"` and self-echo `source!==toWindow`. No `X-Frame-Options`/`frame-ancestors`/`Referrer-Policy` on `/` or `/hosted.js`. `form--submit` handler destructures `{payment, sessionId, product}` from the attacker's value and calls `processCheckout({checkoutId: a.checkoutId, payload: u})` → `endpoint("checkouts/"+id,{method:"PUT"})` against hardcoded `https://api.sumup.com`, with `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` taken from the attacker's `sessionId`. Live: netlog `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-…`, initiator `https://gateway.sumup.com`, isolation key `https://127.0.0.1 https://sumup.com`.
evidence_needed: nothing further — the defect, the dispatch and the outbound `PUT` are all reproduced. The only open question is exploitability with a *valid* widget session.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/` (no XFO/CSP) → `curl -sS -o hosted.js https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`; then from any origin, frame `/` and `postMessage({type:"SumUpCard",message:"form--submit",value:{sessionId:"<x>",product:{appId:"x"},payment:{checkoutId:"11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555",card:{number:"4111111111111111",expiryMonth:"12",expiryYear:"2030",cvv:"111"}}}}, "*")` and observe the `PUT` in netlog.
impact: any web origin gains unauthenticated control of a first-party `sumup.com` payment frame's command channel and can make it issue state-changing calls to the production payments API. Bounded by widget-session validity; `input--initialize` additionally rewrites the card inputs' placeholder/type/autocomplete/maxLength/disabled cross-origin, enabling attacker-controlled UI redress over card entry. Medium, escalating if a session id is ever exposed cross-origin.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] A widget session id that reaches a third-party origin is immediately replayable against the payments API, because the frame supplies it as the credential and never checks who the parent is
class: AUTH
asset: gateway.sumup.com → api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}
confidence: 55
reasoning: `Te()` attaches `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: r` to every endpoint call, where `r` is the `sessionId` from the parent's `postMessage`. Proven that an arbitrary origin can choose this value and cause the `PUT`. The session is the *only* control that rejected my test requests. Any path that discloses it cross-origin (XSS on a merchant page, a leak in a page that legitimately embeds the frame, Referer/log exposure) converts directly into payment-API calls.
evidence_needed: one observation of a real `sessionId` value reaching a non-SumUp origin, or a merchant page that reflects it. Not obtainable passively; the frame never emitted a session id in any of my probes.
verify_steps: with a merchant test checkout, load the legitimate embed from a controlled page and log every `message` event plus `document.referrer` handling; check whether the session id appears in any cross-origin-readable position.
impact: session replay from any origin against the production payments API — unauthenticated payment initiation in the merchant's context. High if reachable; currently unproven.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

[HYP] Prod MCP scope binding is satisfiable by the stock `dashboard` client, so the agent surface may be reachable from an ordinary merchant session with no developer OAuth app
class: OATH
asset: mcp.sumup.com/mcp
confidence: 62
reasoning: RFC 9728 metadata (200, prod) declares `authorization_servers:["https://auth.sumup.com"]`, `scopes_supported:["offline_access","email"]`. The measured `dashboard` consent set is `{openid, classic, offline, readers.read, terminals.read, email}` — it accepts `email` (302 `login_challenge`) and rejects `offline_access` (303 `invalid_scope`). Prod verifier enforces `{RS256, EdDSA}`, refuses `none`/HS256, and its trust store is exactly the 8 published kids (30-candidate sweep, 0 undeclared). Open question is purely the aud/scope binding between `dashboard` tokens and the MCP resource server.
evidence_needed: one completed `authorization_code` exchange as `client_id=dashboard` with `scope=email`, presented to `mcp.sumup.com/mcp`; a non-401 JSON-RPC `initialize` reply proves reachability.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -X POST -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H "Accept: application/json, text/event-stream" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{}}' https://mcp.sumup.com/mcp` (own merchant, ≤1 rps).
impact: the agent surface documented as a developer integration becomes reachable by any authenticated merchant via the stock dashboard client — privilege-boundary erosion across the OAuth trust graph. Medium if confirmed.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

**STEP 4 — SELF-CRITIQUE**

[PARKED] Response exfiltration from the hosted-fields frame: two candidate outbound channels (`aux--metric-track` ack, `form--invalid-input` → `input--blur`) were tested directly and neither delivered. `send()` throws because `document.referrer` is a full URL, not an origin. Recorded as not-claimed rather than assumed, so the report cannot overstate.
[PARKED] `api.sam-app.ro` gateway bearer discrimination and `api.sumup.com` byte-stable quartet: 27+ cycles, no reproducible discriminator without a live bearer. Re-probing is zero-yield waste.
[PARKED] `web.sumup.com` takeover (Rackspace `77.246.42.130`): 21 days open, still connection-timeout, no CNAME, no claimable service state. Genuinely dormant, not fileable.
[PARKED] `mcp.sam-app.ro` scope-claim ordering: real but staging-only, prod rejects staging keys, low value against a stronger finding.
[FINAL] 1) gateway.sumup.com cross-origin payment-frame control — 88, PASSIVE, **complete, on disk, Medium**; 2) prod MCP scope binding via `dashboard` — 62, AUTH_HELPED; 3) widget-session replay once disclosed — 55, AUTH_HELPED; 4) auth.sam-app.ro dynamic registration — 85, PASSIVE, still unsubmitted.

**STEP 5 — NEXT ACTION**

[NEXT] HUMAN: submit `reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md` to bugs.olivermaicher.eu. Evidence is complete, self-contained and already on disk with two PoC pages, the netlog and the served bundle — it is the program's first payment-surface finding and needs no further probing. Second priority is a test-mode merchant API key, which unblocks the MCP scope-binding and apple-pay-session SSRF legs in one step.

**STEP 6 — LEARNING**

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ gateway.sumup.com: hosted-fields postMessage API performs no `event.origin` validation and the frame ships no `X-Frame-Options`/`frame-ancestors`; a cross-origin `form--submit` causes the frame to issue `PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{attacker-chosen-id}` with attacker-supplied session id and payment payload. Proven live via netlog + iframe console. Bounded by widget-session validity; no exfiltration path exists as implemented.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ gateway.sumup.com: the asset the KB dismissed as a "non-Next 404" one cycle earlier is the program's highest-value surface — a PCI card-entry frame. A negative observation recorded from a single path is not a characterisation of a host: `/hosted.js` and `/sdk.js` were both 200 and had never been fetched. This closes nemotron3's confidence-35 lead at 88.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ gateway.sumup.com: response exfiltration via the postMessage API. Two candidate outbound channels tested directly, neither delivered; `send()` throws because `document.referrer` is a full URL rather than an origin. Not claimed.

**STEP 7 — RISK**

[RISK] sumup: 55. Up 15 from 40. This cycle produced the program's first payment-surface finding: a real, live, reproducible cross-origin control of a PCI card-entry frame's command channel, with a `PUT` against the production payments API captured in netlog. It also demonstrates the failure mode that caused 27 wasted cycles — a host characterised from one path — and shows the reverse: breadth-first path enumeration on a single host found more than 27 cycles of re-verification did. The finding is capped at Medium because the widget session is the binding control and no exfiltration path exists as implemented, so program risk remains dominated by the unfiled, evidence-complete deliverables: this one plus auth.sam-app.ro, now unsubmitted for the 16th cycle.
