class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (`/`, `/hosted.js` messenger) → api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}
confidence: 92
reasoning: handler `const p=r=>{const{data:n,source:i}=r; …}` never reads `r.origin`; only gates are `data.type==="SumUpCard"` and self-echo `t&&i!==t`. No `X-Frame-Options`/CSP `frame-ancestors`/`Referrer-Policy` on `/` or `/hosted.js` (re-verified 200, 07:33Z). `Te` attaches `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: r` and `Sumup-Product-Origin: t.origin` straight from the attacker message, and the same values also enter the body via `n.session_id` / `n.product_origin`. The frame-collection gate is conditional on `"card"===a.payment_type`, so omitting it skips `Ce()`/`if(!d.length) return` entirely and takes `card:{...a.card,...o}` from the attacker. `processCheckout` rejects only a missing or non-UUID (`le`/`ue.test`) checkoutId. `sdk.js` independently shows two of three messengers deliver via `fromWindow.parent.postMessage(e,"*")`, so the wildcard-sink pattern is systemic, not incidental. Live: netlog `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555`, `initiator: https://gateway.sumup.com`, isolation key `"https://127.0.0.1 https://sumup.com"`.
evidence_needed: none further. Defect, cross-origin dispatch and the outbound `PUT` are all reproduced; `payment_type`-bypass is a static read of the served, sha256-pinned bundle.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/` (assert no XFO/CSP) → `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum` (expect `1302f1d6…f220873`) → serve a page from any origin that frames `/` and, on iframe load, `postMessage({type:"SumUpCard",message:"form--submit",value:{sessionId:"<invalid>",product:{appId:"poc"},payment:{checkoutId:"11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555",card:{number:"4111111111111111",expiryMonth:"12",expiryYear:"2030",cvv:"111"}}}}, "*")` — **omit `payment_type`** — and read the `PUT` off the network tab. No real checkout id, no live payment state.
impact: any web origin, unauthenticated and with no merchant cooperation, makes a first-party `sumup.com` origin emit a state-changing request to the production payments API with fully attacker-chosen credential header, product origin and body (card, personal details incl. CPF, customer details, direct debit, mandate, installments, SCA mode); plus cross-origin `input--initialize` rewrites placeholder/type/autocomplete/maxLength/disabled on the real PAN/CVV inputs, a UI-redress primitive inside a PCI frame inheriting SumUp's certificate. Medium, bounded by needing a valid checkout id.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] Restoring the broken `form--on-result` channel without adding `event.origin` validation converts the gateway defect into a card-data read primitive
class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (`hosted.js` container messenger) → api.sumup.com
confidence: 74
reasoning: `Re()` builds the container messenger as `u({fromWindow:e, toWindow:e.parent, origin:document.referrer, …})`, so `send` takes the `if(t) return t.postMessage(e, n||"*")` path with `n` = a full URL — an invalid `targetOrigin` that throws and is swallowed by `try/catch`. The success handler is `processCheckout(...).then(e => t.send({message:"form--on-result", value:e}))`, so the checkout result is the payload that never arrives. The two messengers that *do* deliver use hardcoded `"*"` (`fromWindow.parent.postMessage(e,"*")`) and send only `{name}`, `{name,error,shouldShowError}` and the recognised brand. Hence the read direction fails by accident of a wrong-shaped value, not by design, while the wildcard-sink pattern is already load-bearing elsewhere in the same file.
evidence_needed: a `form--on-result` delivery. Not obtainable passively — the current code cannot emit one, by construction. Would require a SumUp-side change or a local patched copy of the bundle to demonstrate.
verify_steps: static — `grep -o 'origin:document.referrer' hosted.js`; then confirm the success path exists via `grep -o 'message:L,value:e'` against the same sha256-pinned bundle, showing the only barrier is the `targetOrigin` shape.
impact: if the `form--on-result` path is fixed to pass a real origin while the missing `event.origin` check remains, an attacker-parent would read live checkout results for any addressable checkout id — raising this finding from a write primitive to a read primitive on a PCI surface. Filed as a remediation-sequencing hazard, **not** as a present vulnerability; no exfiltration is claimed today.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The first-party SDK treats the checkout id as its widget-session credential and ships it to two API origins, so any log, Referer or analytics sink that captures it yields payment-API authority
class: AUTH
asset: gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js (`Ai`, `Hi`) → api.sumup.com + js.sumup.com/api
confidence: 48
reasoning: `Ai(href)` returns `{origin:"Hosted Checkout", sessionId: <segment of /pay/{id}>}` only for host `checkout.sumup.com`, and `Hi` wires that `sessionId` into both `api: Fi("https://api.sumup.com", sessionId)` and `apiBFF: zi("https://js.sumup.com/api", sessionId)`. `Te` then sends it as `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` on every request. So the credential is a URL-borne resource identifier rather than a secret, and it is deliberately duplicated across two origins — which widens the set of places it can be captured. This is a *hardening* observation, materially weaker than the 55 it was carried at before the derivation was read.
evidence_needed: one concrete sink that durably records a live `sessionId` outside SumUp's control (third-party analytics, a merchant log, a `Referer` to an external origin). None observed; `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` returns 403 externally (Vercel WAF) and `gateway.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` 404, so the legitimate embed could not be instrumented from outside.
verify_steps: none available without a merchant test checkout; instrument a controlled embed and enumerate every `message` event and outbound header for the session id. AUTH_HELPED.
impact: if captured, replay against `/v0.2/checkouts/{id}` from any origin. Unproven, and the value is a resource id rather than a secret, so no privilege boundary is crossed by possessing it.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

