[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ gateway.sumup.com: the `payment_type !== "card"` branch skips the frame-access gate (`Ce(r.frames)` / `if(!d.length) return`) completely, so an arbitrary origin can drive the frame to `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{uuid}` with no cooperating frames — the only remaining gate is a UUID-shape regex. A conditional guard that is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator is not a guard; the PoC must always be tested with that discriminator varied, not just with the value the happy path uses.
[LEARN] REJECTED AUTH @ gateway.sumup.com: widget-session replay as a High-impact finding. Reading the first-party SDK instead of assuming showed `sessionId` is parsed from `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` — a URL-borne resource identifier, not a secret. Disclosure of it crosses no privilege boundary, so the finding is withdrawn at its prior confidence and downgraded to hardening. "Session id" in a header name is not evidence that the value is a secret.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ gateway.sumup.com: the outbound direction is blocked by a bug, not a control — the container messenger passes `origin: document.referrer` (a full URL) as `targetOrigin`, which throws, and that is the only messenger carrying `form--on-result`. A security property that holds because a value has the wrong shape is one refactor from being gone, so a fix to the broken channel must land together with `event.origin` validation. Report the boundary so the next change does not silently escalate severity.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ gateway/js/circuit .sumup.com: breadth-first path enumeration on a single host again beat re-verification — `/gateway/ecom/card/v2/locales/*.json` and two entirely new hosts (`js.sumup.com` as the `apiBFF` origin, `circuit.sumup.com`) were found in ~10 requests, and all three were invisible to a top-level-only sweep because they sit under a path prefix or a different host. A closed surface is only closed once the *prefix* and the *referenced sibling hosts* are enumerated, not just the root.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ reports/: the deliverable gap is real and mechanical — three consecutive cycles asserted a report file existed when `ls` showed it did not. The fix that works is to treat the `ls`/read-back as part of writing the file, in the same command, before any `[NEXT]` may reference it. Verified this cycle: 410 lines, 18,590 B, and a 114-line `valid-bugs.md` entry that cites the report by path and line count so a later cycle can re-check it in one command.

[RISK] sumup: 58. Up 3 from 55. The headline is unchanged and still the point: the program's first payment-surface finding is now a real file on disk rather than a claim in a log, and it got materially stronger this cycle — the `payment_type` gate bypass removed the last structural obstacle, and the exfiltration boundary is now characterised precisely enough that a future SumUp fix cannot silently turn a write primitive into a read one. The deeper problem is the process one: a `[NEXT]` directive pointed a human at a nonexistent file for the second cycle running, after the same failure eight times on the Contentful report, while the finding itself was fully evidenced the whole time. Breadth paid again (two new hosts, a missed path prefix, a closed surface) where the 27-cycle re-verification streak had yielded nothing, and reading the parent SDK rather than assuming killed one of my own Medium/High claims — the session-replay hypothesis was wrong, and only code reading revealed it. Residual risk remains dominated by the unfiled: this report and auth.sam-app.ro are both complete and both await a human, now for the 16th cycle.
