## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

```
[HYP] gateway.sumup.com hosted-fields frame accepts cross-origin form--submit and drives PUT to the production payments API
class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (/, /hosted.js) -> api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}
confidence: 92
reasoning: Handler is `addEventListener("message",p)`; `p` destructures `{data,source}` only. Zero origin comparisons exist in 26,839 served bytes. No X-Frame-Options, no CSP frame-ancestors, no Referrer-Policy on / or /hosted.js. Frame gate is conditional on caller-supplied `a.payment_type`: `if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return;` — omit it and the only structural barrier is skipped, leaving the UUID regex `le(r)`. `Te` writes `"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id":r` and `i.headers["Sumup-Product-Origin"]=t.origin` straight from the message; the same values enter the body via `n.product_origin=i.origin,n.session_id=i.sessionId`. Emitted request is `s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r),{method:"PUT",...})` with the checkout id in the path, in `X-Sumup-Correlation-Id`, and in the body.
evidence_needed: none. Defect, cross-origin dispatch and the outbound PUT were all reproduced; the payment_type bypass is a static read of the sha256-pinned bundle.
verify_steps: `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum` (expect 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873) -> `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin==='` (expect 0) -> frame `https://gateway.sumup.com/` from any origin and postMessage `{type:"SumUpCard",message:"form--submit",value:{sessionId:"<invalid>",product:{appId:"poc"},payment:{checkoutId:"11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555",card:{number:"4111111111111111",expiryMonth:"12",expiryYear:"2030",cvv:"111"}}}}` with payment_type omitted, targetOrigin "*" -> read the PUT off the network tab.
impact: any unauthenticated web origin, with no merchant cooperation, makes a first-party sumup.com origin emit a state-changing request to the production payments API with attacker-chosen credential header, product origin and body (card, personal details incl. CPF, customer details, direct debit, mandate, installments, SCA mode); `input--initialize` additionally rewrites placeholder/type/autocomplete/maxLength/disabled on the live PAN/CVV inputs, a UI-redress primitive inside a PCI frame. Medium: bounded by needing a valid widget session. No exfiltration is claimed — see report §5, the outbound `form--on-result` channel is broken by a `targetOrigin` bug (container messenger passes `origin:document.referrer`, a full URL, which throws) rather than by a control, so repairing it without adding origin validation would create a read primitive. Both must land in one change.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] pos-payment AWS API Gateway trusts its own service principal, so any AWS-context foothold in the SumUp account obtains a signed request to a money-path endpoint — and the identical public staging replica doubles the target set
class: AUTH
asset: pos-payment.sumup.com + staging.pos-payment.sumup.com (AWS API Gateway, raw IPs 46.51.170.212 / 54.77.206.131)
confidence: 58
reasoning: Both hosts are AWS API Gateway (`x-amzn-apigw-id`, `x-amzn-errortype: MissingAuthenticationTokenException`) resolving to raw AWS IPs with no Cloudflare headers, unlike every other inventoried host. All non-root routes are IAM/SigV4-gated; `/ping` is the sole unauthenticated 200. Prod and staging return byte-identical bodies (root sha256 823397edd76b88b4…) and differ only in IP and region, so staging is a full replica on a public name. The service is a POS payment-link validator ("This link is invalid or a payment has already been processed for this order"), i.e. it answers per-order state. IAM-gated API Gateway is the textbook confused-deputy shape: API Gateway signs with its own credentials when the calling principal is allowed by the resource policy, so the trust boundary is the resource policy, not the route. The CT set shows a large AWS footprint that could host such a foothold (dwh-replica.internal, k8s-eu-west-1-live, 6f74656c-grpc/-http, cash-operation-ingestor, promo-service, netsuitetest1/2, sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo).
evidence_needed: the resource policy trust relationship on each API (prod vs staging), and any AWS-context request primitive that can set an arbitrary path. Neither is obtainable without credentials or a foothold; the reconnaissance half is complete and passive.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://pos-payment.sumup.com/pay/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555` (expect 403 + `x-amzn-errortype: MissingAuthenticationTokenException`) -> same against staging and diff the bodies (expect identical) -> `curl -sS https://pos-payment.sumup.com/ping` (expect `healthy`) -> with authorised creds, `aws apigatewayv2 get-api --api-id <id>` on both to compare `resourcePolicy` and route `authorizationType`.
impact: if a resource policy trusts a broad or assumable principal, a low-privilege AWS-context foothold elsewhere in the SumUp account escalates to authenticated, signed access to a payment endpoint with attacker-chosen path — and staging doubles the surface with a near-certainly weaker policy. Currently a reconnaissance-complete posture finding, not a demonstrated bypass; the origin exposure and public production-equivalent staging replica are established facts independent of the policy question.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

[HYP] The support_centre OAuth client's registered redirect host has had a broken TLS trust chain since October 2022, making the classic-scoped grant either dead or interceptable
class: AUTH
asset: auth.sumup.com client `support_centre` -> support-centre.sumup.com
confidence: 47
reasoning: `support_centre` is registered on the modern auth server with redirect `/api/auth/callback` and scopes `openid+classic+offline`, and is absent from the legacy gateway. Its redirect host `support-centre.sumup.com` (66.33.60.194) is Vercel-served and presents `CN=*.sumup.com` from Let's Encrypt R3 with notBefore 2022-07-20 / notAfter 2022-10-18, while issuing `308 -> https://support-centre.sumup.com/` on plain HTTP — actively steering clients to the broken endpoint. This is a stale/decommissioned deployment, which also explains the earlier observation that the family's callback route serves a bare Next.js 404 shell. The hypothesis is about the OAuth code-return trust boundary, not certificate hygiene: a host whose chain has been unverifiable for four years cannot be relied on to carry an authorization code, and is a standing target for anyone able to present any certificate to a lax client.
evidence_needed: whether any live first-party or merchant client still performs the code exchange against this host, and whether any client relaxes verification for it. Not determinable passively.
verify_steps: `echo | openssl s_client -connect support-centre.sumup.com:443 -servername support-centre.sumup.com 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates` (expect notAfter 2022-10-18) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null http://support-centre.sumup.com/` (expect 308 to https and `server: Vercel`).
impact: conditional and modest. Modern clients hard-fail on an expired chain, so the likelier outcome is a dead grant. If any client relaxes verification, the authorization code for the `classic` scope becomes interceptable in a network position. Recorded because it is a precise, reproducible fact about an auth-flow host and because it indicates the `support_centre` registration is abandoned — a dormant grant to be aware of, not a demonstrated vulnerability. Explicitly not filed as a TLS-hygiene item, which the program lists out of scope.
testability: PASSIVE
