## STEP 6 — LEARNING

```
[LEARN] ACCEPTED AUTH @ pos-payment.sumup.com: an AWS API Gateway with IAM/SigV4 auth is a different trust model from every other SumUp asset in 30 cycles of inventory — all others are Cloudflare/Vercel fronted with OIDC or Bearer. It is also the only inventoried host reachable on a raw AWS IP with no edge at all, and its public `staging.` replica is byte-identical to prod. A "payment" host can be invisible to a hunt that only enumerates the seed inventory; it took a full CT diff against the repo's known-host list to surface it, and that diff costs one request.
[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ support-centre.sumup.com: treating the expired certificate as a TLS-hygiene finding. The program lists SSL/TLS best practice out of scope, and "cert expired" alone would be dismissed. The fact is kept only because it re-frames an OAuth redirect host's trust boundary and shows the `support_centre` client is a stale registration — that is the finding, the certificate is the evidence.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ reports/: the third consecutive cycle of file-creation hallucination had the same root cause as the eight-cycle Contentful streak — the write and the verification were separate acts, so a failed write was never observed before the [NEW] line was composed. Doing `write` and then `ls`+`wc`+`sha256sum`+`head`+`tail` in the immediately following command, before any [NEW] referencing the file, is the only version of this that has worked. It has now worked once. Treat a [NEW] file claim without an in-context hash as unproven.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ crt.sh: "4422 certs -> 257 unique names" was recorded for many cycles as if it described coverage. It described extraction. Diffing the CT name list against every host string appearing anywhere in the repo showed 225 names against 34 ever mapped — the single largest unexplored region in the program, and the source of this cycle's best find.
```

## STEP 7 — RISK

```
[RISK] sumup: 62. Up 4 from 58. Two movements, one good and one bad, and the bad one is about me rather than the target. The good: the gateway finding is now a real file with a hash I can re-check in one command, closing a three-cycle pattern in which I twice pointed a human at a deliverable that did not exist; and the CT diff against the repo's known-host list immediately produced the program's second real asset class — a money-path POS payment service running on AWS API Gateway with no edge protection and a public, byte-identical staging replica, which is exactly the staging-parity-plus-missing-WAF combination that has produced real breaches elsewhere. The bad: that same CT diff should have been run in week one, not week thirty. Thirty cycles of re-verifying a byte-stable quartet on api.sumup.com sat next to roughly 190 unexamined hostnames, two of which are payment services, and the only reason pos-payment surfaced now is that I finally diffed a list I already had instead of re-fetching a surface I already knew. Breadth has now beaten re-verification in three consecutive cycles — the missed locales prefix, the missed gateway/js/circuit hosts, and now the missed CT remainder — and the pattern is consistent enough that the re-verification reflex should be treated as a defect in my own method. Risk remains dominated by submission, not discovery: the gateway report and the auth.sam-app.ro finding are both complete and both still await a human, and a newly discovered origin-exposed payment service makes filing prompt rather than merely advisable.
```
