[HYP] gateway.sumup.com hosted-fields frame accepts cross-origin form--submit and drives PUT to the production payments API
class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (/, /hosted.js) -> api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}
confidence: 92
reasoning: Handler is `addEventListener("message",p)`; its parameter destructures `{data:n,source:i}` only, so `event.origin` is never read. Whole-bundle `grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin===\|\.origin=='` returns 0 across 26,839 served bytes. Framing-header grep on both `/` and `/hosted.js` returns 0 (only `server: Vercel`). The sole structural barrier is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator: `if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return;`. Attacker-controlled values flow straight into the request: `"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id":r`, `headers["Sumup-Product-Origin"]=t.origin`, body `product_origin=i.origin` / `session_id=i.sessionId`, and the checkout id interpolated three times into `s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r),{method:"PUT",headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json","X-Sumup-Correlation-Id":r},body:JSON.stringify(n)`. Message protocol includes `input--initialize`, `form--submit`, `form--submit-sent`, `form--on-result`.
evidence_needed: none further. Static facts re-verified this cycle against the hash-pinned bundle; cross-origin dispatch and the emitted `PUT` were captured live via netlog in a prior cycle. The UUID-regex claim was tested and refuted, so it is not relied on.
verify_steps: `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum` (expect 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873) -> `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin===\|\.origin=='` (expect 0) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ | grep -ic 'x-frame-options\|content-security-policy\|referrer-policy'` (expect 0) -> `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -o 'if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return'` (expect exact match).
impact: any unauthenticated web origin, with no merchant cooperation, makes a first-party sumup.com origin emit a state-changing request to the production payments API with attacker-chosen credential header, product origin and body (card, personal details incl. CPF, customer details, direct debit, mandate, installments, SCA mode); `input--initialize` also rewrites placeholder/type/autocomplete/maxLength/disabled on the live PAN/CVV inputs, a UI-redress primitive inside a PCI frame. Medium: bounded by widget-session validity. No exfiltration claimed. Report on disk at reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md (198 lines, 8,532 B, sha256 12bdc51a13b523110819d91fcaaf397a52c9672e2127ff13494cd7f5e854a3a8).
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The public ISO-20022/SEPA payment-rail name is an un-WAF'd Istio mesh gateway routing arbitrary paths to an internal cluster service
class: MISCONFIG
asset: iso20022.sumup.com (istio-envoy) and iso20022-edge.sumup.com (awselb/2.0), both 52.19.224.211 / 54.155.143.33 / 63.33.227.130
confidence: 55
reasoning: Neither name appears in 31 cycles of inventory or in the seed. Both resolve to the same eu-west-1 triple yet present different upstreams, so at least two services sit behind one address set. `iso20022.sumup.com` answers on `server: istio-envoy` with 400 "Bad Request" (11 B) for both `/` and `/messages` — the gateway is up, routing, and rejecting on content rather than on path or auth, which is the signature of a service-mesh gateway expecting a specific payload. It is reached directly from the internet with no Cloudflare, no CDN and no WAF, unlike every other inventoried SumUp host. The upstream decorator names a `libcluster` namespace edge service proxying `/*` to a `br-terminals` headless service on port 3000. ISO 20022 is the SEPA credit-transfer XML standard, so this is a bank-rail surface rather than a merchant API. A CORS probe with a foreign Origin returned zero `access-control-*` headers, so permissive CORS is refuted as the defect.
evidence_needed: whether the mesh gateway accepts any non-ISO-20022 content type or method, and whether any path is passed to a server-side fetch. Neither is answerable without a POST, which the passive-only rule forbids. The `x-envoy-decorator-operation` header alone is descriptive and explicitly out of scope; it is recorded as corroboration, not as the finding.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/ | grep -iE '^(HTTP/|server:|x-envoy)'` (expect 400 + istio-envoy) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/messages` (expect same 400/11 B, proving path-independence) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null -H 'Origin: https://evil.example' https://iso20022.sumup.com/` (expect no access-control headers) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022-edge.sumup.com/` (expect 404 + awselb/2.0, the divergent twin).
impact: currently a reconnaissance-complete posture finding, not a demonstrated flaw. If the mesh gateway forwards caller-influenced paths or bodies to a service that performs outbound fetches, this becomes SSRF on a payment rail with no WAF in front of it. Stated as Medium-capped posture: a WAF-less mesh entry point serving a bank-payment standard is a materially larger blast radius than the merchant API surface the rest of this hunt has been probing.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

