# gateway.sumup.com — cross-origin message dispatch drives state-changing requests to the production payments API

**Asset:** `https://gateway.sumup.com/` (SumUp hosted-fields / card-entry iframe)
**Class:** MISCONFIG (missing origin validation + missing framing protection)
**Severity:** Medium
**Testability:** PASSIVE — no authentication, no merchant cooperation, no card data
**Report date:** 2026-09-26
**Bundle pin:** `GET /hosted.js` = 26,839 B, sha256 `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873`

---

## 1. Summary

`gateway.sumup.com` serves the PCI-scoped card-entry iframe for SumUp Hosted Checkout. The
iframe's only integration surface is a `postMessage` API implemented in `/hosted.js`. That
handler **never reads `event.origin`**, and the frame ships **no framing protection of any
kind**. Any web page on the internet can therefore embed the frame and drive it.

Driving it with `form--submit` causes the frame to emit a **`PUT` to the production payments
API** — `https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{attacker-chosen-id}` — carrying an
attacker-supplied widget-session header, an attacker-supplied product origin, and an
attacker-supplied JSON body (card, personal details incl. CPF, customer details, direct debit,
mandate, installments, SCA mode).

Two facts make this worse than a generic "missing origin check":

1. **The one structural barrier is conditional on a caller-supplied value.** The frame checks
   that cooperating frames exist, but only inside a `payment_type === "card"` branch. Send any
   other `payment_type` and the check is never executed.
2. **The response channel is closed by a bug, not by a control.** The messenger that would
   return the API result throws on every call, because it passes a full URL where
   `postMessage` requires an origin. A fix to that unrelated bug, landed on its own, converts
   this into a full read primitive. See §6 — this must be fixed *with* the origin validation.

---

## 2. What was verified, and how

All claims below are re-verified against the served bytes on 2026-09-26. Reproduce with the
commands in §8.

### 2.1 The frame is a real PCI card-entry surface

`GET https://gateway.sumup.com/` returns a 546 B shell:

```html
<title>hostedfields</title>
<script type="text/javascript" src="./hosted.js"></script>
<script charset="utf-8">
  const hform = HostedForm.init(window, window.parent || window.top)
  window.addEventListener('load', () => { hform.onLoad(); });
</script>
```

`window.parent || window.top` confirms the asset's only intended caller is a container page.

### 2.2 No framing protection

```
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ \
  | grep -icE 'x-frame-options|content-security-policy|referrer-policy'
0
```

Zero occurrences on `/` and on `/hosted.js`. There is no `X-Frame-Options`, no
`Content-Security-Policy` (hence no `frame-ancestors`), and no `Referrer-Policy`. Any origin
may frame it.

*Contrast within the same estate:* `payout-settings-edge.sumup.com` returns
`x-frame-options: SAMEORIGIN`. The control exists in SumUp's stack; it is simply absent here,
on the one asset where framing is most dangerous.

### 2.3 No origin validation — the core defect

The message handler is registered as:

```js
addEventListener("message",p)
```

and its parameter destructures the payload and the sender window only:

```js
{ data: n, source: i }
```

`event.origin` is never read anywhere in the bundle:

```
$ curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js \
  | grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin===\|\.origin=='
0
```

Zero matches across all 26,839 served bytes. There is no allowlist, no comparison, no
`instanceof` check on the sender. The only discriminator is a self-echo check plus a
caller-supplied string:

```js
type === o.TYPE          // o.TYPE === "SumUpCard"
```

`"SumUpCard"` is a constant in the attacker's JSON, not a property of the sender.

### 2.4 The frame-access gate is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator

The bundle contains exactly one structural barrier, and it is inside a conditional:

```js
if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return;
```

Read plainly: *if* `payment_type` is the string `"card"`, *then* collect the cooperating frames
and bail out if there are none.

The attacker controls `payment_type`. Omit it, or set it to anything other than `"card"`, and:

- `Ce(r.frames)` is never called,
- `d` remains empty,
- the `return` never fires,
- execution falls straight through into the authenticated request path with **no cooperating
  frames at all**.

A conditional guard that a caller-supplied discriminator can skip is not a guard. Any PoC for
this class must vary that discriminator, not only exercise the value the happy path uses.

### 2.5 The request the attacker reaches

The `form--submit` path terminates in:

```js
s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r),{
  method:"PUT",
  headers:{
    "Content-Type":"application/json",
    "X-SumUp-Correlation-Id":r
  },
  body:JSON.stringify(n)
  ...
})
```

with, upstream of it:

```js
"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id": r
```

and a caller-set `Sumup-Product-Origin` header. The attacker controls all of:

| Value | Source in attacker JSON | Lands in |
|---|---|---|
| `checkoutId` (`r`) | `payment.checkoutId` | URL path, `X-SumUp-Correlation-Id`, `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` |
| session id (`r`) | derived | `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` |
| product origin | `t.origin` | `Sumup-Product-Origin`, body `product_origin` |
| body (`n`) | verbatim | request body |

Note `checkoutId` is read from `payment.checkoutId`, not from the top level — a PoC that sends
it at the top level will not reach the handler.

### 2.6 Full inbound message protocol

Recovered from the bundle (11 verbs):

```
input--initialize   input--change   input--recognize   input--focus   input--blur
form--submit        form--submit-sent   form--on-result
form--on-error      form--invalid    form--invalid-input
```

`input--initialize` is a second primitive: it rewrites `placeholder`, `type`, `autocomplete`,
`maxLength` and `disabled` on the live PAN and CVV inputs. That is a UI-redress capability
*inside* the card frame — useful to an attacker layering a deceptive overlay on top of a
trusted SumUp origin.

---

## 3. Attack scenario

1. Attacker hosts a page on any domain, including one they own outright.
2. Page embeds `https://gateway.sumup.com/` in an `<iframe>` — permitted, there is no framing
   protection (§2.2).
3. Page `postMessage`s `{type:"SumUpCard", message:"form--submit", data:{...}}` with
   `payment_type` set to anything other than `"card"` (§2.4).
4. The SumUp frame, running on a genuine `sumup.com` origin, emits an authenticated
   `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` to the production payments API (§2.5), with attacker-chosen
   body and headers.
5. The request originates from a first-party SumUp origin, so it is not subject to
   third-party-cookie or cross-origin request restrictions that would otherwise apply.

The victim is not required to authenticate, enter a card, or consent to anything. Merely
loading the attacker's page is sufficient.

---

## 4. Impact

- **Primary:** a state-changing write to the production payments API (`PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}`)
  is driven by an arbitrary unauthenticated web origin, with attacker-controlled path
  identifier, credential header, product origin and body. Requested body fields include card
  data, personal details (incl. CPF), customer details, direct-debit, mandate, installments
  and SCA mode.
- **Secondary:** `input--initialize` permits attribute rewriting of the live PAN/CVV inputs,
  a UI-redress primitive inside a PCI frame.
- **Bounded (stated honestly):** the request is only useful while the referenced widget session
  remains valid, so this is not an unauthenticated takeover of arbitrary merchant data. It is
  the *write* primitive that is unbounded, and it is executed by a first-party origin on the
  attacker's behalf.
- **Not claimed:** no response-exfiltration path is demonstrated. See §6 — the reason is a
  code defect, not a control, and that distinction matters for remediation ordering.

**Severity: Medium.** Bounded by widget-session validity; no card data or exfiltration path
proven; requires only that a victim load a page.

---

## 5. Out of scope / limits of this test

- No card numbers, no real PAN/CVV, no live customer data was used or submitted.
- No merchant account, no API key, no bearer token, no authentication of any kind.
- The real checkout page `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` returns `403` (Vercel WAF) from the
  internet, so the genuine container page cannot be framed. This does not affect the finding:
  the vulnerable frame is directly framable on its own.
- The 3-D Secure hop `3dsecure.sumup.com` returns `403` with the 118 B AWS default body
  (`server: awselb/2.0`), so an SCA flow terminates there rather than completing. This limits
  the flow to the checkout-write stage.

---

## 6. Remediation — ordering is part of the fix

Fix the two gaps **together**. Fixing only the broken response channel converts this finding
from a blind write primitive into a full bidirectional one.

1. **Validate `event.origin`** in the `message` handler against an explicit allowlist of
   SumUp checkout container origins. Do not use `*`, and do not treat the absence of an
   exception as acceptance.
2. **Add framing protection** to the frame. Prefer CSP `frame-ancestors` over
   `X-Frame-Options`, so subdomains can be granted deliberately. The rest of the estate
   (`payout-settings-edge.sumup.com`) already sets `SAMEORIGIN`; this asset should at minimum
   match it.
3. **Do not gate access on a caller-supplied discriminator.** The
   `if("card"===a.payment_type){ ... }` structure means the frame-access check runs only for
   card payments. Hoist the check so it cannot be skipped, and fail closed on unknown
   `payment_type`.
4. **Bind the request to a server-side session.** `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` and the checkout
   id are both caller-supplied; the server should resolve the checkout from the authenticated
   session rather than trusting the path identifier.
5. **Treat the broken `send()` as a defect in its own right.** The messenger passes
   `document.referrer` — a full URL — as `targetOrigin`, which throws on every call. It is
   currently the only thing preventing response delivery. Repair it *together with* step 1,
   never before it, and pass an origin, not a URL.

---

