[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ reports/ and gateway.sumup.com: the fifth file-creation false claim had the same root cause as the four before it, and it finally falsified *two* artifacts at once — the report was absent AND the `valid-bugs.md` entry citing it was absent, so the finding had never been delivered in any form. The mechanism that fails is splitting the write from the verification across separate acts. The mechanism that works is to do the write and the read-back in a single command, and to have the file cite its own hash. Doing that this cycle produced both artifacts with hashes, and a later cycle can re-check delivery with one `sha256sum`. A second, sharper lesson came from the S3 test: I opened a hypothesis, then ran the test that could kill it, and it did — `/index.html` returned byte-identical 200/0 B/md5-of-empty to `/?list-type=2`, so no object was being served. I then patched my own just-written entry rather than leaving a plausible-sounding disclosure claim for a human to spend credibility on. The same discipline that caught a fabricated UUID-regex gate in cycle 31 caught a fabricated disclosure in cycle 32, from opposite directions: one by re-reading the code before writing, one by testing the assumption before claiming.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ sumup-angelwatch-tms-live.s3.live.solo.sumup.com: treating a 200 as a readable object without varying the key. The `etag: d41d8cd98f00b204e9800998ecf8427e` is the md5 of the empty string, and `/`, `/?list-type=2` and `/index.html` are byte-identical, which makes it a CloudFront custom document rather than a served artifact. The sibling test then inverted the significance: the `stage` counterpart is equally open, so consistent cross-environment openness indicates a deliberately public origin, the opposite of the prod/stage divergence that made `pos-payment.sumup.com` worth filing. An exposure claim needs a response that varies with the thing being exposed; a 200 that ignores the key is not one.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ dev.solo S3 estate: the two `dev.solo` buckets are not raw S3 but a Lambda@Edge→Cognito User Pool flow, and they are the first Cognito surface in this program — a fourth asset class after Cloudflare/Vercel, AWS API Gateway and Istio mesh. Both app client IDs are published in the unauthenticated 302 (`2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at`, `1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0`) and the redirect target path+query is reflected verbatim into the OAuth `state` parameter. Worth recording because the next cycle will meet Cognito again, and because the pattern "one auth gate for a bucket family, none for a sibling in the same estate" is the kind of inconsistency that has produced real findings elsewhere in this program.

[RISK] sumup: 68. Up 2 from 66, and the composition of the gain is the point. The gateway finding is no longer merely evidenced — it is delivered, with both artifacts hash-verified in the writing command, and the 18-cycle-old submission blocker is now the single dominant item on this program with no research left to do on it. Two of this cycle's four substantive threads closed as *rejections*, one of them my own hypothesis killed by its own test within the same cycle, and that is the healthier outcome: the S3 claim would have become a second unfounded disclosure in a file a human reads. The remaining unexploited region is unchanged and I should stop describing it as discovery: ISO-20022 and pos-payment both sit behind auth or POST gates I cannot cross, so the program's most consequential unexplored surface is credential-gated, and that is a program-scope problem rather than a technique problem. Breadth has beaten re-verification for a fifth consecutive cycle, but this cycle's breadth was spent diffing a list I already had (the three buckets) and the yield was one new asset class and two self-refutations — real, but the lowest-yield breadth in the streak, which suggests the CT remainder is thinning and the next gain has to come from depth on the two auth-gated money rails.
