# Deprecated OAuth 2.0 Implicit Grant (`response_type=token`) enabled on two SumUp AWS Cognito app clients

**Program:** SumUp Payments Limited / SumUp Group — bugs.olivermaicher.eu
**Class:** MISCONFIG / OATH (deprecated grant enabled on a production cloud service)
**Severity:** Low (hardening / standards-compliance). No token leak demonstrated — see "Why severity is capped" below.
**Testability:** PASSIVE — all evidence below is unauthenticated HTTP `GET`. No credentials, no card data, no customer data, no state-changing request.

## Summary

Two AWS Cognito User Pool app clients fronting SumUp's internal `dev.solo` S3 build-artifact
namespace have the **deprecated OAuth 2.0 implicit grant enabled** alongside the authorization
code flow. With the implicit grant, Cognito returns the `access_token` and `id_token` **in the URL
fragment** rather than in a redirect body, which is the delivery mechanism OAuth 2.1 (and the OAuth
Security BCP) removes.

The configured `redirect_uri` for both clients is a **CloudFront/S3 static content origin**
(`*.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com`) — i.e. tokens would be delivered to the origin that is *also the
resource being protected*, which the OAuth Security BCP explicitly advises against.

This is a configuration defect, not an authentication bypass. It requires a user to authenticate
at the Cognito Hosted UI, exactly as the code flow does. No token was obtained and none is claimed.

## Affected assets

| # | Cognito domain | App client ID | `redirect_uri` (registered) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com` | `2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at` | `https://sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com` |
| 2 | `hardware-shared.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com` | `1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0` | `https://hardware-shared.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com` |

Both clients are reachable **without authentication** and both app client IDs are published in the
unauthenticated `302` response of their respective buckets.

## How the clients are exposed

`sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com` is not raw S3. It is a CloudFront distribution with a
Lambda@Edge function that gates every request behind Cognito:

```
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null 'https://sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com/?list-type=2&max-keys=5'
HTTP/2 302
server: CloudFront
cache-control: no-cache, no-store, max-age=0, must-revalidate
x-cache: LambdaGeneratedResponse from cloudfront
set-cookie: XSRF-TOKEN=...; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax
location: https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize
          ?redirect_uri=https://sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com
          &response_type=code
          &client_id=2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at
          &state=/%3Flist-type%3D2%26max-keys%3D5
```

Two observations from that redirect:

* `response_type=code` is hard-coded by the Lambda@Edge function.
* The request **path and query string are reflected verbatim into the OAuth `state` parameter**
  (`state=/%3Flist-type%3D2%26max-keys%3D5`). This is the application post-login return target, not
  an attacker-injectable value on its own, and it is not the subject of this report.

## The defect: implicit grant is enabled

Holding `client_id`, `redirect_uri` and `scope=openid` constant and varying **only** `response_type`
produces a clean discriminator. `code` and `token` are accepted (Cognito proceeds to the Hosted UI
`/login`); every other value is rejected with `invalid_request` on the Cognito error page.

### Client 1 — `2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at`

```
$ B='https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize
     ?client_id=2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at
     &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fsumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com
     &state=AAAAAAAAAAAA'

$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=code&scope=openid"              # -> /login        ACCEPTED
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=token&scope=openid"             # -> /login        ACCEPTED  <-- implicit
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=id_token&scope=openid"          # -> error=invalid_request
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=code%20id_token&scope=openid"  # -> error=invalid_request
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=bogus&scope=openid"            # -> error=invalid_request
```

### Client 2 — `1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0` (replicated)

```
$ B2='https://hardware-shared.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize
      ?client_id=1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0
      &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fhardware-shared.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com
      &state=AAAAAAAAAAAA'

$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B2&response_type=code&scope=openid"   # -> /login             ACCEPTED
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B2&response_type=token&scope=openid"  # -> /login             ACCEPTED  <-- implicit
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B2&response_type=bogus&scope=openid" # -> error=invalid_request  (control)
```

Accepting exactly `{code, token}` and rejecting `id_token`, hybrid `code id_token`, and an unknown
value is the signature of a Cognito app client whose `allowedOAuthFlowsUserPoolClient` contains
**both `code` and `implicit`**. The `bogus` control confirms the negative results are a real
rejection and not a generic redirect.

### The implicit request is carried into the login session, not converted

The accepted implicit request propagates into the Hosted UI URL rather than being silently
downgraded, so a successful authentication would return tokens in the fragment:

```
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=token&scope=openid"
location: https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/login
          ?client_id=2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at
          &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fsumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com
          &state=AAAAAAAAAAAA
          &response_type=token
          &scope=openid
```

## Scope is minimal, which bounds the ID token

Holding `response_type` constant and varying only `scope`:

| Scope | Result |
|---|---|
| *(omitted)* | `/login` — accepted (Cognito defaults to `openid`) |
| `openid` | `/login` — accepted |
| `openid email` | `error=invalid_scope` |
| `aws.cognito.signin.user.admin` | `error=invalid_scope` |

The clients allow bare `openid` only. No `email`, and no AWS administrative scope. Any ID token
obtained through the implicit flow would therefore carry `sub`/`aud`/`iss` and not user PII. This
materially limits the value of a leaked token and is the main reason severity is capped.

## Why severity is capped at Low

The registered `redirect_uri` is the CloudFront origin, and that origin **serves no content at all** —
every path is redirected to Cognito:

```
/              -> HTTP 302  bytes=0
/index.html    -> HTTP 302  bytes=0
/hosted.js     -> HTTP 302  bytes=0
/main.js       -> HTTP 302  bytes=0
```

Consequently there is **no same-origin script today that could read a delivered fragment token**,
and URL fragments are never sent in a `Referer` header, so they do not leak to third parties through
referrer propagation. The realistic exposure of the implicit flow here is limited to browser history
and a malicious browser extension.

The origin also ships **no** `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options`
or `Referrer-Policy` (only `server: CloudFront` and a no-store `cache-control`).

Stated plainly: **this is a deprecated-grant-enabled and token-delivery-to-a-content-origin
configuration defect, not a demonstrated vulnerability.** It is reported because the implicit grant
is removed in OAuth 2.1, because it is enabled on two independent clients rather than one, and
because the safe configuration is a one-field change. It should be triaged as Low.

The reason it still matters: the `redirect_uri` is the *resource* origin, so this configuration
becomes a genuine token-disclosure primitive the moment that bucket's content posture changes — if
any user-influenceable object is ever served from `*.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com`, or if the Lambda@Edge
gate is ever removed, script on that origin can read `location.hash` and exfiltrate the bearer token
for the bucket it fronts. That is a latent boundary worth fixing before it is live, not after.

## Remediation

Remove `implicit` from `allowedOAuthFlowsUserPoolClient` for both app clients, leaving `code` with
PKCE. Re-verify with the `response_type=token` request above, which must then return
`error=invalid_request` like the `bogus` control does.

## Reproduction

All commands are unauthenticated `GET`. Run at most 1 request/second.

```bash
# Client 1 — expect: /login then /login (implicit ACCEPTED)
#            then error=invalid_request, error=invalid_request, error=invalid_request
RU='https%3A%2F%2Fsumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com'
B="https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize?client_id=2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at&redirect_uri=$RU&state=AAAAAAAAAAAA"
for rt in code token id_token "code%20id_token" bogus; do
  curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B&response_type=$rt&scope=openid" | grep -i '^location:'
done

# Client 2 — same matrix
RU2='https%3A%2F%2Fhardware-shared.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com'
B2="https://hardware-shared.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize?client_id=1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0&redirect_uri=$RU2&state=AAAAAAAAAAAA"
for rt in code token bogus; do
  curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null "$B2&response_type=$rt&scope=openid" | grep -i '^location:'
done
```

## Scope note

The User Pool IDs themselves are not disclosed in this report; the pool domain alias does not
publish OIDC metadata or a JWKS:

```
$ curl -sS 'https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/.well-known/openid-configuration'
HTTP/2 404  {"error":"This URL doesn't exist on the authorization server."}
$ curl -sS 'https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/.well-known/jwks.json'
HTTP/2 404  {"error":"This URL doesn't exist on the authorization server."}
```

No unauthenticated access to bucket contents, user data, or tokens was obtained at any point.
