# `gateway.sumup.com` hosted-fields frame accepts cross-origin `postMessage` and drives state-changing PUTs to the production payments API

**Program:** SumUp Payments Limited / SumUp Group — bugs.olivermaicher.eu
**Class:** MISCONFIG (missing origin validation + missing framing protection on a PCI card-entry frame)
**Severity:** Medium — bounded by widget-session validity; no data exfiltration path exists as implemented (see "What is NOT claimed").
**Testability:** PASSIVE to demonstrate the defect; the state-changing leg was captured live in an earlier cycle and is reproduced here from served bytes.
**Evidence pin:** `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` — 26,839 bytes, sha256 `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873`

Every code citation below was re-read from the served bundle at the pinned hash immediately before
this report was written. No detail here is from memory.

## Summary

`gateway.sumup.com` serves SumUp's **hosted card-entry iframe** — the PCI-scoped frame that
collects card data for hosted checkout. The frame is embedded by merchant pages and communicates
with its parent entirely through `window.postMessage`.

Two independent controls that should be present are both absent:

1. **The `message` handler never reads `event.origin`.** It accepts a message from any origin.
2. **The frame ships no framing protection** — no `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy`
   (hence no `frame-ancestors`), no `Referrer-Policy`.

Consequently any web page on the internet can frame the SumUp card-entry component and drive it.
The `form--submit` message causes the frame to issue a **`PUT` to
`https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{checkoutId}`** with attacker-supplied session identifier,
product origin and payment payload, from a first-party `sumup.com` origin.

## The message handler, verbatim

```js
const p = r => {
  const { data: n, source: i } = r;      // <-- event.origin is never destructured or read
  n && n.message && (
    d(n) && (m[n.message] || []).forEach(/* ... */)
  ),
  t && i !== t || (c[n.message] || []).forEach(/* ... */)
};
...
addEventListener("message", p)
```

* The event is destructured as `{ data, source }` only. `event.origin` is not read anywhere.
* The only gate on an inbound message is `d(n)`, where `d = e => e.type === o.TYPE` and
  `o.TYPE === "SumUpCard"` — a **string inside the attacker-controlled message payload**.
* `m` carries frame→parent handlers; `c` carries parent→frame handlers. The guard
  `t && i !== t ||` means the `c` handlers run when no `toWindow` is configured, or when
  `source === toWindow`. **No origin comparison participates in either branch.**

A whole-bundle grep for any origin comparison returns zero matches:

```bash
$ curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js \
    | grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin===\|\.origin=='
0
```

## No framing protection

```bash
$ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ \
    | grep -ic 'x-frame-options\|content-security-policy\|frame-ancestors'
0
```

Only `server: Vercel` is set. There is no `frame-ancestors` directive, so the PCI card-entry frame
is framable by any origin. For contrast, a sibling SumUp host in the same estate,
`payout-settings-edge.sumup.com`, does send `x-frame-options: SAMEORIGIN` — the divergence is
within SumUp's own estate, not a house style.

## The gate is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator

The intended access control is a frame-presence check, but it sits inside a conditional on an
attacker-controlled field:

```js
if ("card" === a.payment_type) {
  if (d = Ce(r.frames), !d.length) return;   // <-- only barrier
  ...
}
```

When `payment_type` is anything other than `"card"`, `Ce()` is never called, `d` is never assigned
and the `!d.length` early return never executes. The gate is conditional on a value the caller
supplies, so omitting or altering `payment_type` bypasses it entirely. A conditional guard that a
caller-supplied discriminator can skip is not a guard.

## The state-changing request, verbatim

```js
i.checkouts.processCheckout({
  checkoutId: a.checkoutId,   // read from payment.checkoutId, not top level
  payload: u,
  redirectMechanism: ...
})
```

```js
processCheckout: e => {
  let { checkoutId: r, payload: t, redirectMechanism: a } = e;
  const n = we(t);
  if (!r) throw new Error("Unable to process checkout without a valid checkoutId");
  return qe(r) ? Ee(n) : le(r) ? (
      a && (n.sca_experience = a),
      i && (n.product_origin = i.origin, n.session_id = i.sessionId),
      s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r), {
        method: "PUT",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json", "X-Sumup-Correlation-Id": r },
        body: JSON.stringify(n)
      }).then(xe).then(ge).then(ve)
  ) : p
}
```

The emitted request carries attacker-influenceable values:

* URL path — `checkouts/{checkoutId}`
* `"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id": r` header
* `n.product_origin` and `n.session_id` body fields, from the caller-supplied session object
* the full payment payload `n`

### The precise gate on the checkout identifier

Three branches, stated exactly as the code reads:

* `qe(r)` is `[he, _e].includes((r || "").toLowerCase().trim())` where `he = "demo"` and
  `_e = "demo-sandbox"`. Those identifiers short-circuit to `Ee(n)`, a **no-op stub** returning
  `{ demo: true, message: "This is a demo. Not a real checkout is processed." }` — no API call.
* Otherwise `le(r)` must be true, where `le = e => ue.test(e)`.
* If `le(r)` is false the call falls through to `p` and no request is made.

**Honest limitation:** `ue` is constructed at runtime via `new RegExp(a.pattern)` and its pattern is
not statically resolvable in the minified bundle. The only literal regexes present are
`/^\d{3,4}$/` (a 3–4 digit CVV length check) and a customer-name pattern
`/^[a-zA-ZÀ-ž]{1}[a-zA-ZÀ-ž'\s\-\.]{1,128}$/`. So a shape check on `checkoutId` **does** exist but
its exact pattern is not recoverable from the served bytes.

An earlier revision of this report asserted the remaining gate was a "UUID-shape regex". That claim
was tested against the bundle, did not reproduce, and has been **removed** rather than restated in
weaker form. What can be said is bounded: the request requires a `checkoutId` that is neither
`demo`/`demo-sandbox` nor rejected by that runtime-built pattern, and requires a live widget
session.

## Live proof captured in an earlier cycle

* **Cross-origin dispatch** — an attacker page on `https://127.0.0.1:8443` framing the shell and
  sending a single `postMessage { type: "SumUpCard", message: "form--submit" }` produced the
  handler's activity in the SumUp iframe console, with `source` being the attacker window.
* **The emitted request** — Chrome netlog recorded
  `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 HTTP/1.1` with the `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`
  header, from the first-party `gateway.sumup.com` origin.

## Full inbound protocol accepted from any origin

Each of these is handled by the ungated `c`/`m` dispatch:

`input--initialize`, `input--change`, `input--recognize`, `input--focus`, `input--blur`,
`form--submit`, `form--submit-sent`, `form--on-result`, `form--on-error`

`input--initialize` rewrites `placeholder`, `type`, `autocomplete`, `maxLength` and `disabled` on
the live PAN and CVV inputs (e.g. `{ input: r } => (r.disabled = !0)`), so it is also a
**UI-redress primitive inside a PCI frame** — the real card fields can be visually repurposed while
remaining the elements the user types into.

## Flow is bounded at the SCA hop

`3dsecure.sumup.com` returns `403` with a 118-byte AWS default body from an ELB, so the
Strong Customer Authentication hop terminates. The demonstrated flow therefore ends at the
checkout write; it does not reach 3-D Secure.

`checkout.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` returns `403` from the Vercel WAF to external requests, so the real
checkout page cannot itself be framed; `gateway.sumup.com/pay/{uuid}` returns `404`.

## What is NOT claimed

* **No token or card data exfiltration.** The only messenger carrying `form--on-result` is the
  container, which passes `origin: document.referrer` — a full URL — as `targetOrigin`. That is an
  invalid `targetOrigin` and the call **throws**. The outbound direction is blocked *by a bug, not
  by a control*. Two candidate exfiltration channels were tested directly and neither delivered.
* **The `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` is not a secret.** The first-party SDK derives it from
  `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` — it is the checkout identifier taken from a URL. Disclosure of a
  URL-borne resource identifier crosses no privilege boundary, so a "session replay" framing was
  **withdrawn** and downgraded to hardening. "Session id" in a header name is not evidence that the
  value is a secret.

That the outbound channel is closed by a defect rather than a control is itself worth reporting: a
security property that holds only because a value has the wrong shape is one refactor from being
gone. **Any fix to the broken messenger must land together with `event.origin` validation**, or the
severity of this finding silently increases.

## Impact

Any unauthenticated web origin, with no merchant cooperation and no valid SumUp account, can cause
a first-party `sumup.com` origin to emit a state-changing `PUT` against the production payments API
with an attacker-chosen session header, product origin and payment body — card data, personal
details including CPF, customer details, direct debit, mandate, installments, or SCA mode —
targeting an attacker-chosen checkout identifier. The frame can additionally be redressed for
credential phishing inside a trusted, PCI-branded context.

Bounded by widget-session validity and by the checkout-identifier gate above, and with no
exfiltration path as implemented, this is rated **Medium**.

## Remediation

1. Validate `event.origin` in the `postMessage` handler against an explicit allowlist of merchant
   origins, in both the `m` and `c` dispatch paths, and pass a matching `targetOrigin` on every
   outbound `postMessage`. **Ship this together with a fix to the `document.referrer` messenger.**
2. Move the `Ce(r.frames)` frame-presence check out of the `payment_type === "card"` conditional so
   it cannot be skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator.
3. Send `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors <merchant allowlist>` and `X-Frame-Options` from
   the frame, matching the posture already used on `payout-settings-edge.sumup.com`.
4. Remove `implicit`-style token-in-URL delivery from the messaging protocol and treat
   `form--submit` as requiring a server-verifiable, single-use nonce bound to the session.

## Reproduction

```bash
# 1. Confirm no origin validation in the served bundle
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum
# expect 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js \
  | grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin===\|\.origin=='          # expect 0

# 2. Confirm no framing protection
curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ \
  | grep -ic 'x-frame-options\|content-security-policy\|frame-ancestors'  # expect 0

# 3. Confirm the ungated handler and the skippable gate
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -o 'addEventListener("message",p)'
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js \
  | grep -o 'if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return'

# 4. Confirm the emitted payments-API write
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js \
  | grep -o 'checkouts/"\.concat(r),{method:"PUT"[^;]\{0,120\}'
```

To observe the cross-origin leg, serve a page from any origin that iframes
`https://gateway.sumup.com/`, then post `{ type: "SumUpCard", message: "form--submit" }` into the
frame. Any widget session belonging to the page operator is required; no third-party merchant
account or card data is needed to demonstrate the missing origin check.

