# AWS Cognito app clients for SumUp dev.solo build buckets have the deprecated OAuth 2.0 implicit grant enabled

**Assets:**
- `sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com` — app client `2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at`
- `hardware-shared.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com` — app client `1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0`

**Protected resource:** `https://sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com` (registered `redirect_uri`)
**Class:** OATH (deprecated grant enabled on a production OAuth client)
**Severity:** Low as configured — see the explicit impact cap in section 4.
**Authentication required:** None. Unauthenticated GET only.
**Report self-check:** `sha256sum reports/cognito-implicit-grant-dev-solo-buckets.md`

---

## 1. Summary

The two `dev.solo.sumup.com` build-artifact buckets are not raw S3. They are fronted by a
Lambda@Edge that redirects to an **AWS Cognito User Pool**, and the redirect publishes the app
client ID. Both app clients have `allowedOAuthFlowsUserPoolClient` containing **`implicit`**, which
enables the OAuth 2.0 implicit grant — a grant removed in OAuth 2.1 and disallowed by current
OAuth 2.0 Security BCP guidance for confidential clients.

The configuration fact is proven by a controlled matrix below, with a `bogus` control that
establishes the negatives are genuine rejections rather than generic redirects.

---

## 2. Controlled `response_type` matrix — client `2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at`

`client_id`, `redirect_uri` and `scope` held **constant**; only `response_type` varies.

```bash
CID=2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at
RD='https%3A%2F%2Fsumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com'
for RT in code token id_token 'code id_token' bogus; do
  ENC=$(printf '%s' "$RT" | sed 's/ /%20/g')
  curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null \
    "https://sumup-hardware-s3-external.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize\
?client_id=$CID&redirect_uri=$RD&state=AAAAAAAAAAAA&response_type=$ENC&scope=openid"
done
```

| `response_type` | Status | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `code` | 302 | **accepted** — to `/login` |
| `token` | 302 | **accepted** — to `/login` |
| `id_token` | 302 | rejected — `error=invalid_request` |
| `code id_token` | 302 | rejected — `error=invalid_request` |
| `bogus` | 302 | rejected — `error=invalid_request` |

The accepted set is exactly the RFC 6749 §4.1 / §4.2 pair `{code, token}`. The `bogus` control is
what makes this a finding rather than a guess: it proves the server is genuinely discriminating on
`response_type` and that the three rejections are real, not a blanket redirect.

The sibling client `1dolam18pelmhvt9b9n3eqsmn0` on `hardware-shared.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com`
was tested independently and shows the same `{code, token}`-accepted pattern — a result on one
client would not have generalised.

---

## 3. The implicit request is carried into the login session

An accepted `response_type=token` is not silently converted to an authorization-code flow. The
`/login` URL echoes it back verbatim:

```
/login?client_id=2cepvoiffh5hlosl79knonc8at
      &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fsumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com
      &state=AAAAAAAAAAAA
      &response_type=token
      &scope=openid
```

So a completed authentication would return `access_token` and `id_token` in the **URL fragment**.

Scope is bare `openid` on both clients — `openid email` and
`aws.cognito.signin.user.admin` both return `invalid_scope`. This caps the ID token to
`sub`/`aud`/`iss`; no PII is carried.

---

## 4. Impact — stated as a cap, not inflated

**Low as configured.** A deprecated flow being enabled is a real defect. A *leak* requires a
reader, and I tested for one and did not find it.

The registered `redirect_uri` is the **bare bucket origin** — the resource being protected, not a
purpose-built callback. That is the delivery target the OAuth Security BCP advises against.

Content posture of that token-delivery origin, tested directly:

| Path | Status | Bytes |
|---|---|---|
| `/` | 302 | 0 |
| `/index.html` | 302 | 0 |
| `/hosted.js` | 302 | 0 |
| `/main.js` | 302 | 0 |

Security headers on that origin: `content-security-policy`, `x-frame-options`,
`referrer-policy` → **0 matches**.

Therefore, today:
- the origin serves **no content**, so there is no same-origin script able to read `location.hash`;
- URL fragments **never propagate via `Referer`**.

**No token leak is claimed.** The response is identical for all four paths, so the "origin serves
user-influenceable content" premise is not satisfied by current bytes.

### Latent boundary — the condition that would change this

If either of the following becomes true, script on `*.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com` can read
`location.hash` and exfiltrate a bearer token for the bucket it fronts:
1. any **user-influenceable object** is ever served from that namespace (a user-supplied upload key
   reachable on the same origin is sufficient), or
2. the Lambda@Edge Cognito gate is removed or narrowed for that origin.

Recorded explicitly so that condition 1 or 2 is treated as the trigger, not rediscovered later.

---

## 5. Recommended

1. Set `allowedOAuthFlowsUserPoolClient` to `["code"]` on both app clients; enable PKCE
   (`code_challenge`) if not already required.
2. Register a purpose-built callback host instead of using the bucket origin as `redirect_uri`.
3. Add a restrictive `Referrer-Policy` and CSP to the `*.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com` namespace so the
   latent boundary in section 4 is defended in depth rather than only by the absence of content.
4. Apply the same flow audit to any sibling Cognito pool in the estate — the `response_type` matrix
   above is a cheap, self-contained test that needs no authentication and no user interaction.
