## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] A per-bucket auth gate on the dev.solo.sumup.com estate is inconsistently applied, leaving serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com anonymously readable
class: MISCONFIG
asset: serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com (raw AmazonS3/CloudFront), vs siblings sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com and hardware-shared.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com (both Cognito-gated)
confidence: 52
reasoning: Same estate, same CloudFront distribution family, divergent posture. Two siblings 302 to `*.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize` with published `client_id`; serial-terminal returns 200 with `server: AmazonS3` and no redirect. The gate is applied per-bucket, not at the distribution or estate level, so a bucket added later is ungated by default. Name pattern `serial-terminal` places it in POS/IoT terminal tooling.
evidence_needed: at least one non-default object readable anonymously. The default document is not evidence — `/` is byte-identical to `/?list-type=2`, so this is a CloudFront fallback, and an unknown key returns a distinct 404. Bucket listing is denied, so object discovery requires key guessing. No such key found; the exposure leg is currently unproven, not disproven.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download}\n' https://serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com/` (expect 200 2723) -> `curl -sS https://serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com/?list-type=2 | md5sum` (expect `9d1538b513569007f8a801627615d17b`, identical to root, proving no ListBucket) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' https://serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com/nonexistent-key-zzz` (expect 404, distinct body, proving keys are discriminated) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com/ | grep -i '^location:'` (expect Cognito 302, the sibling control)
impact: At current evidence, a stale unauthenticated default document with no demonstrated data exposure — Low/hardening. The finding is the **inconsistent gate**, not a leak: if this bucket holds firmware or terminal configuration, the missing gate converts directly to unauthenticated read. A `serial-terminal` origin serving POS terminal artifacts ungated is a materially different risk from a stale HTML page, which is why the inconsistency is worth a triager's attention even at Low.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] SumUp's internal estate topology — per-environment Kubernetes clusters, data warehouse, and the RFC1918 edge tier — is enumerable from public Certificate Transparency logs
class: OTHER
asset: internal.sumup.com (dwh, dwh-replica, k8s-eu-west-1-live, k8s-eu-west-1-stage, k8s-eu-developers); solo-edge-private.live.solo.sumup.com and solo-edge-private.stage.solo.sumup.com
confidence: 58
reasoning: Public CT returns the five `internal.sumup.com` names, which are NXDOMAIN publicly but disclose that SumUp operates a data warehouse with a replica, plus three distinct Kubernetes clusters keyed by environment and team. Independently, `solo-edge-private.live` publishes `10.85.30.248/10.85.31.151/10.85.31.37` and `.stage` publishes `10.85.26.249/10.85.27.221/10.85.27.93` — RFC1918 space in public DNS, as a matched live+stage pair, extending the five-instance class already in the KB to seven and for the first time reaching the **production** edge tier. Together these give an attacker the cluster/environment split, the data-warehouse location, and the private address ranges, without touching a single SumUp server.
evidence_needed: none for the disclosure itself; it is a direct read of public CT and public DNS. What is *not* established is reachability of any RFC1918 address from outside, which is not testable and is not claimed.
verify_steps: `curl -sS 'https://crt.sh/?q=%25.sumup.com&output=json' | grep -o 'internal\.sumup\.com'` (expect 5 names: dwh, dwh-replica, k8s-eu-west-1-live, k8s-eu-west-1-stage, k8s-eu-developers) -> `getent ahostsv4 solo-edge-private.live.solo.sumup.com` (expect 10.85.x.x) -> `getent ahostsv4 solo-edge-private.stage.solo.sumup.com` (expect 10.85.x.x, distinct /24s from live)
impact: Information disclosure of internal architecture and private address space. No direct compromise — none of these hosts answer publicly, and RFC1918 space is not routable from the internet. Its value is reconnaissance: it converts a blind target-selection problem into a directed one, and the `live`/`stage` split tells an attacker which cluster carries production. Low severity on its own; relevant as an input to a later finding rather than a standalone bug.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The un-WAF'd ISO-20022/SEPA mesh gateway accepts caller-influenced paths or bodies and forwards them to a service that performs outbound fetches
class: SSRF
asset: iso20022.sumup.com (istio-envoy, x-envoy-decorator-operation: iso20022-edge-libcluster-headless.br-terminals.svc.cluster.local:3000/*), iso20022-edge.sumup.com (awselb/2.0)
confidence: 50
reasoning: Carried at 55, reduced to 50 — 32 cycles have now produced no new passive discriminator, and the previous estimate was not backed by any discriminating measurement. Facts: no Cloudflare, no CDN, no WAF, reachable from the internet on a payment rail; answers 400 Bad Request (11 B) identically on `/` and `/messages`, rejecting on content rather than path or auth, which is the signature of a mesh gateway expecting ISO 20022 XML. A foreign `Origin` returned **zero** `access-control-*` headers, so permissive CORS is refuted as the defect class. Both names share one AWS eu-west-1 address set but serve different upstreams, so at least two services sit behind that set.
evidence_needed: whether the gateway accepts any non-ISO-20022 content type or method, and whether any path reaches a server-side fetch. Both require a POST, which the passive-only rule forbids — this is the whole reason the hypothesis cannot be advanced or killed here.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/ | grep -iE '^(HTTP/|server:|x-envoy)'` (expect 400 + istio-envoy) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/messages` (expect identical 400/11 B, proving path-independence) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null -H 'Origin: https://evil.example' https://iso20022.sumup.com/` (expect no access-control headers) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022-edge.sumup.com/` (expect 404 + awselb/2.0)
impact: Unproven. If the gateway forwards caller-influenced paths or bodies to a service performing outbound fetches, this is SSRF on a SEPA payment rail with no WAF in front — one of the highest-consequence outcomes available in this program. That consequence is the reason to keep testing, not a reason to raise the number. Capped Medium as posture.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

