## STEP 6 — LEARNING

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ reports/: the seven-cycle file-creation streak had a mechanical cause that seven cycles of "verify your writes" advice missed. The filesystem was never the problem — `touch` and heredoc both succeeded while the artifacts were absent. The failing path was *write in one act, verify in a later act*, which leaves a window in which a plausible `[NEW]` line gets composed from intent. The fix that worked first-try on both artifacts: heredoc **inside the same command that prints `sha256sum`**, so the hash and the write are one act and there is nothing left to skip. The sharper rule: a `[NEW]` line quoting a line count or hash is a claim about a measurement, and a measurement not taken in the same act as the write is decoration — no matter how many times the advice is repeated.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com: treating an anonymous 200 as a data exposure. `server: AmazonS3` with 2,723 B of HTML reads exactly like the `sumup-angelwatch-tms-live` finding the previous cycle retracted, and the instinct was to file it. Varying the key killed it in one round: `/`, `/?list-type=2` and `/index.html` are byte-identical (md5 `9d1538b5…`, so `ListBucket` is denied) while an unknown key returns a distinct 404. What survives is the *sibling inconsistency* — two buckets on the identical estate are Cognito-gated and this one is not — which is a gate-application defect, not a leak, and is filed at Low with the exposure leg explicitly marked unproven rather than quietly assumed.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ crt.sh: the technique that produced this program's best finds for six consecutive cycles is now structurally exhausted, and it failed in a way worth naming. Diffing the CT name list against the repo returns 224 names and **0 unmapped** — because the analyst logs are themselves in the repo and record every name ever seen, so the diff measures my own writing rather than my coverage. The lesson generalises past CT: once a corpus is stored inside the thing being searched, "appears in the corpus" stops carrying information about "was examined." A sixth consecutive high yield from a method is a signal to check whether the method still has a discriminating power, not a reason to run it again.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ gateway.sumup.com hosted.js: re-reading the bundle in order to write it honestly changed the finding a third time, and each pass has moved it in a different direction. This pass found that the checkoutId gate *is* real — `le(r)` → `ue.test(r)`, built at runtime by `new RegExp(a.pattern)` — which the previous pass had retracted entirely, while confirming the pattern is unrecoverable. The accurate statement is now narrower and stranger than any earlier version: two gates exist, the frame-access gate is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator, the shape gate is not, and the shape gate's own pattern is attacker-influenceable and unauditable from the served bundle. Three passes of writing this report from memory would have shipped three different and mutually inconsistent descriptions of the remaining barrier into a human's hands.

## STEP 7 — RISK

[RISK] sumup: 74. Up 3 from 71, and for the first time the gain is real delivery rather than discovery. The seven-cycle false-claim streak is broken and — more usefully — *explained*: I stopped re-attempting the write and diagnosed why it kept failing, which produced a mechanism that worked on the first try for both artifacts. Two findings are on disk with hashes printed in the same command that created them, and `valid-bugs.md` at 127 lines cites both by path, line count and hash so a later cycle can re-check delivery in one command. I am deliberately not scoring the submission itself as risk reduction: 19 cycles is now a long enough streak that treating the reports as delivered would be the same category of error as the file claims, just one level up.

The honest cost of this cycle is that the research yield was thinner than the last several, and for an explicable reason. The CT-diff method is exhausted because my own logs poisoned the corpus it searched, so six cycles of reliable gain have stopped. Two new assets surfaced from a different angle (the ungated `serial-terminal` sibling and the RFC1918 live/stage edge pair), and the most valuable thing I produced was a *negative* — the key-variation control that stopped an exposure claim I was about to file, one cycle after an identical control stopped an identical claim. That is the second consecutive cycle a control I already knew about prevented a false report, which suggests the controls are now the highest-return thing in this program, not the probing.

The dominant risk is unchanged and I will state it plainly rather than dress it as technique: `iso20022` and `pos-payment` are both money rails sitting behind gates I cannot cross, so the most consequential unexplored surface in this program is credential-gated, and no amount of additional passive breadth will change that. Breadth has now demonstrably thinned — this cycle's was the lowest-yield in six — so the next real gain has to come from depth on those two rails or from a human submitting what is already complete.
