[0m$ [0mcd /home/runner/work/sumup-hunt/sumup-hunt/reports && cat > js-bff-checkout-existence-oracle.md <<'EOF'
# Unauthenticated per-checkout existence oracle on `js.sumup.com/api` (SumUp hosted-fields BFF)

- **Program:** SumUp Payments Limited / SumUp Group
- **Asset:** `js.sumup.com` (Vercel), path family `/api/checkouts/{id}`
- **Class:** MISCONFIG (information disclosure) with an IDOR/BOLA leg that is **not yet proven**
- **Severity:** Medium (posture). The impact leg is explicitly capped below.
- **Testability:** PASSIVE — every request below is an unauthenticated `GET`
- **Reported state:** not yet submitted

## 1. Why this asset was believed dead

`js.sumup.com` is the second of two API clients constructed by SumUp's own
hosted-fields bundle. In `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` (26,839 B,
sha256 `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873`)
there is **no** `apiBFF` identifier and **no** `js.sumup.com` reference. The
four hosts the bundle names are `api.sumup.com`, `checkout.sumup.com`,
`developer.sumup.com`, `static.sumup.com`.

The BFF is in `gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` (291,877 B,
sha256 `0fae546ae34ee5e97cf0c3107d87adaa79129983d423bce6cf9a0fc19916c7bd`),
which contains 5 `apiBFF` references:

```js
this.state = {
  sdkURL: "https://gateway.sumup.com/gateway/ecom/card/v2/",
  env: "production",
  api:    Fi("https://api.sumup.com",     this.props.sessionId),
  apiBFF: zi("https://js.sumup.com/api",  this.props.sessionId),
}
```

Previous cycles recorded the origin and closed it as *"`js.sumup.com/api`
prefix uniformly 404 across 6 shapes — closes the `apiBFF` second
money-touching origin"*. That sweep is **not exhaustive**, and the reason is
recoverable from the bundle. `zi` builds its client with a **null version
segment** (`zi(...)` → `Bi(e, t, n, null, ni)`), so the real paths are:

| method | path |
|---|---|
| `GET`  | `https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{checkoutId}[?attempts=N]` |
| `POST` | `https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{checkoutId}/fee-calculation` |

Both carry `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: <sessionId>` and, when the referrer
parses, `Sumup-Product-Origin: <origin>`.

Two of the previously-closed premises were therefore false:

1. The endpoint is **not** a bare `/api` prefix — it is `/api/checkouts/{uuid}`.
2. The client only issues that request when `checkoutId` matches a UUID regex,
   so a sweep of *prefix shapes* could never construct a request the real client
   would send. An exhaustive sweep is only exhaustive over the values actually
   sent.

## 2. The finding

`GET /api/checkouts/{id}` is **routed, application-handled and unauthenticated**.
With no `Authorization` header, no cookie and no `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`,
it returns an RFC 9457 problem document whose `detail` is a **resource-scoped
existence statement with the requested id reflected verbatim**:

```
$ curl -sS 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555'
HTTP/2 404
content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8
content-length: 181
server: Vercel

{"title":"Not Found","status":404,
 "detail":"Checkout 11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 does not exist.",
 "type":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9110#name-404-not-found"}
```

## 3. Controls — three distinct response classes

| request | status | ct | size | body class | md5 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /api` | 404 | `text/plain` | 84 | Vercel catch-all stub (`NOT_FOUND`) | — |
| `GET /api/merchants/{uuid}` | 404 | `text/plain` | 84 | Vercel catch-all stub | `f48ea6c3c810cbd86a735ade56523721` |
| `GET /api/checkouts/{uuid}` | 404 | `application/json` | 181 | `"Checkout <id> does not exist."` | `5e19511ce3b7d04787ca40001d2a7f03` |
| `GET /api/checkouts/{other uuid}` | 404 | `application/json` | 181 | `"Checkout <id> does not exist."` | `1ebaa1165ed931f551e3f8d1e7e23cf7` |
| `GET /api/checkouts/not-a-uuid` | 404 | `application/json` | 155 | `"Checkout not-a-uuid does not exist."` | `1aff06e0fe36f382e1cf5cf197618d1d` |
| `GET /api/checkouts/{uuid}/fee-calculation` | 404 | `application/json` | 144 | `"Endpoint does not exist."` | `67b17cb9bf1380695981be07bc2df88c` |

The `text/plain` / `application/json` split is what proves the application is
executing rather than the edge stub answering. The router resolves at least
three classes:

- **not under `/api`** → Vercel stub, 84 B `text/plain`
- **routed, method not exposed** → 144 B `"Endpoint does not exist."`
- **routed, resource absent** → 155–181 B `"Checkout <id> does not exist."`

The third class is a per-resource lookup. The second class confirms
`fee-calculation` is **routed**, established with a `GET` and without issuing
the `POST`.

## 4. Client/server validation divergence

The client validates the id shape before it will issue the request:

```js
xi = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
wi = e => xi.test(e)
```

`fetchCheckout` throws `Unable to fetch checkout. checkoutId: ${t} is not
valid.` when `!wi(t)`. **The server applies no equivalent check** — the
malformed `not-a-uuid` is accepted, looked up, and reflected. The only
id-shape gate on this path lives in the browser bundle and is trivially
omitted.

This also corrects a prior characterisation. Two earlier cycles recorded the
`checkoutId` gate as a runtime `new RegExp(a.pattern)` whose pattern was
"unrecoverable from served bytes". In `sdk.js` the validator is a **static
literal** and the pattern is recoverable as quoted above. The more useful
fact is the one that follows from it: the gate is **client-side only**.

## 5. Why this matters

`fetchCheckout` is the call the checkout UI makes to render the payment screen.
Per the bundle's own demo object and the caller's `delete e.lxn_session_token`,
a successful response carries `id`, `currency`, `amount`, `status`,
`merchant_code`, `merchant_name`, `merchant_country`, `merchant_sandbox`,
`purpose` and an `lxn_session_token`.

So the resource behind this unauthenticated 404 holds merchant-identifying and
financial fields. Whether a **valid** checkout id returns `200` with that
object *without* a session id is the entire impact question, and it is
**unproven** — I do not have a valid checkout id and will not enumerate one,
because those ids are customer data and the program forbids testing against it.

## 6. Impact, stated at the evidence boundary

**Proven:** a live, unauthenticated, application-routed per-checkout existence
lookup on a second money-touching origin, with no server-side id validation
and no authentication in front of the resource resolution.

**Not proven:** that any checkout id returns `200` unauthenticated, and
therefore that merchant or financial data is disclosed. An oracle that answers
"does not exist" for every id an attacker can guess is not yet a leak.

**If** the `200` path turns out to be reachable without a valid
`X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`, this becomes a pre-auth BOLA disclosing merchant
identity and transaction amounts for any checkout id, and escalates to High.

## 7. Remediation

1. Require a valid `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` **and** verify it is
   authorised for the requested `checkoutId` **before** the existence lookup,
   so a resource-scoped message is never emitted to an unauthenticated caller.
2. Enforce the UUID shape server-side. The regex already exists client-side;
   an id-shape check is a client convenience, not a control.
3. Serve a single non-reflecting 404 for absent and unauthorised resources
   alike, so the endpoint is not an existence oracle.
4. `access-control-allow-origin: *` is returned together with
   `access-control-allow-credentials: true`. Browsers reject that pairing for
   credentialed requests, so this is **hardening only, not token theft** — the
   client calls this endpoint with `withCredentials = false` (`Bi` sets it only
   when `opts.credentials === "include"`, and `fetchCheckout` passes
   `{method:"GET"}`). Flagged only so it is not mistaken for the same finding
   class elsewhere in the estate.

## 8. Reproduction

All requests unauthenticated `GET`, no cookies:

