## 2026-09-27 06:30 UTC — js.sumup.com/api unauthenticated checkout existence oracle — VALID 7.0 — FILE REPORT

**Asset:** `js.sumup.com` (Vercel), path family `/api/checkouts/{id}`
**Class:** MISCONFIG (information disclosure), IDOR/BOLA leg unproven
**Testability:** PASSIVE (unauthenticated GET only)
**Report:** `reports/js-bff-checkout-existence-oracle.md` — 192 lines, 9,357 B,
sha256 `a635d22c52080fbc19bd4fb63eea934f74e9f63377bd6eb426868639adc3f4ed`

**Summary.** `GET https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id}` is routed,
application-handled and unauthenticated. With no `Authorization` header, no
cookie and no `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` it returns an RFC 9457 document whose
`detail` is a per-resource existence statement with the id reflected verbatim:
`"Checkout 11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 does not exist."` (404, 181 B,
`application/json`).

**Three-class control, all unauthenticated GET:**

| request | status | ct | size | body |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `/api` | 404 | `text/plain` | 84 | Vercel catch-all stub |
| `/api/merchants/{uuid}` | 404 | `text/plain` | 84 | Vercel catch-all stub |
| `/api/checkouts/{uuid}` | 404 | `application/json` | 181 | `"Checkout <id> does not exist."` |
| `/api/checkouts/not-a-uuid` | 404 | `application/json` | 155 | id reflected, **no server-side UUID check** |
| `/api/checkouts/{uuid}/fee-calculation` | 404 | `application/json` | 144 | `"Endpoint does not exist."` (routed, proven without POST) |

The `text/plain` vs `application/json` split is what proves application
execution rather than an edge stub. md5s of the five bodies are recorded in the
report.

**Why the prior closure was invalid.** Prior cycles closed this origin as
"uniformly 404 across 6 shapes". Two premises were false, both recoverable from
`gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` (291,877 B, sha256 `0fae546a…16c7bd`):

1. The endpoint was recorded as a bare `/api` prefix. It is `/api/checkouts/{uuid}` —
   `zi` builds its client with a **null version segment**.
2. `hosted.js` was cited as the source of the `apiBFF` reference. It contains
   neither `apiBFF` nor `js.sumup.com`. The citation is in `sdk.js`.

The client also only issues the request when `checkoutId` matches
`/^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i`
(`wi = e => xi.test(e)`), so a sweep of *prefix shapes* could never construct a
request the real client would send.

**Bonus correction.** The `checkoutId` gate was recorded across three cycles as a
runtime `new RegExp(a.pattern)` whose pattern was "unrecoverable from served
bytes". In `sdk.js` it is a **static literal** and is recovered above. The
stronger consequence is that the gate is **client-side only** — the server
accepts `not-a-uuid`.

**Impact boundary.** Proven: an unauthenticated, application-routed per-checkout
existence lookup on a second money-touching origin, no server-side id
validation, no auth in front of resource resolution. **Not proven:** that any
checkout id returns `200` unauthenticated, so merchant/financial disclosure is
**not claimed**. A successful `fetchCheckout` carries `merchant_code`,
`merchant_name`, `merchant_country`, `merchant_sandbox`, `amount`, `currency`,
`status` and `lxn_session_token`; if the `200` path is reachable without a valid
session id this escalates to a pre-auth BOLA and to High. Not tested: obtaining
a valid checkout id would require enumerating customer data, which the program
forbids.

**CORS, stated at the same boundary.** `access-control-allow-origin: *` ships
with `access-control-allow-credentials: true`. Browsers reject that pairing for
credentialed requests and the client calls this endpoint with
`withCredentials = false`, so this is **hardening only, not token theft** —
consistent with the program's existing rejection of that class on
`mcp.sumup.com`.
EOF
