[HYP] An unauthenticated, application-routed per-checkout existence lookup on the hosted-fields BFF discloses merchant identity and transaction amounts for any checkout id
class: MISCONFIG
asset: js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{checkoutId} (Vercel; second API origin built by gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js)
confidence: 72
reasoning: `GET /api/checkouts/{id}` returns 404 `application/json` 181 B with `detail:"Checkout <id> does not exist."`, id reflected, with no `Authorization` header, no cookie and no `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`. Sibling `/api/merchants/{uuid}` and `/api` return 84 B `text/plain` `NOT_FOUND` — the Vercel catch-all — so the `application/json` response proves the application executes rather than the edge stub. `/api/checkouts/not-a-uuid` returns 155 B with the id reflected, so no server-side shape check exists. `/api/checkouts/{uuid}/fee-calculation` returns 144 B `"Endpoint does not exist."`, a third class, proving that route is registered. `fetchCheckout` in the bundle carries `id, currency, amount, status, merchant_code, merchant_name, merchant_country, merchant_sandbox, purpose, lxn_session_token`.
evidence_needed: a checkout id that exists returning `200` with that object and **no** valid `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`. Deliberately not obtained: valid ids are customer data and the program forbids testing against it. This is why 72 and not higher — the oracle is proven, the disclosure is not.
verify_steps: `curl -sS 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555'` (expect 404 181 B JSON, id reflected) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download} %{content_type}\n' https://js.sumup.com/api/merchants/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555` (expect 404 84 text/plain, the stub control) -> `curl -sS https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/not-a-uuid` (expect 155 B, id reflected, proving no server-side UUID check) -> `curl -sS 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555/fee-calculation'` (expect 144 B `"Endpoint does not exist."`, routed without POST)
impact: Proven today: unauthenticated per-checkout existence oracle on a second money-touching origin, with no server-side id validation and no auth in front of resource resolution. If the `200` path is reachable without a valid session id, it becomes a pre-auth BOLA disclosing merchant identity and amounts for any checkout id and escalates to High. Capped Medium as posture; disclosure leg explicitly unclaimed.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The per-bucket auth gate on the dev.solo.sumup.com estate is inconsistently applied, leaving serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com anonymously readable
class: MISCONFIG
asset: serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com (raw AmazonS3/CloudFront), vs sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com and hardware-shared.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com (both Cognito-gated)
confidence: 52
reasoning: Same estate, same CloudFront family, divergent posture — two siblings 302 to `*.auth.eu-west-1.amazoncognito.com/authorize` with a published `client_id`; serial-terminal returns 200 with `server: AmazonS3` and no redirect, `Last-Modified: 2022-11-04`. Gate is per-bucket, so a later-added bucket is ungated by default.
evidence_needed: at least one non-default object readable anonymously. The default document is **not** evidence: `/`, `/?list-type=2` and `/index.html` are byte-identical (md5 `9d1538b5…`, so `ListBucket` is denied) and an unknown key returns a distinct 404. No such key found.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download}\n' https://serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com/` (expect 200 2723) -> `curl -sS https://serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com/?list-type=2 | md5sum` (expect `9d1538b513569007f8a801627615d17b`, identical to root, proving no ListBucket) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' https://serial-terminal.dev.solo.sumup.com/nonexistent-key-zzz` (expect 404, distinct body, proving keys are discriminated) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://sumup-embedded-build.s3.dev.solo.sumup.com/ | grep -i '^location:'` (expect Cognito 302, sibling control)
impact: At current evidence a stale default document with no demonstrated exposure — Low/hardening. The finding is the **inconsistent gate**: if this bucket holds firmware or terminal configuration, the missing gate converts directly to unauthenticated read. A `serial-terminal` origin serving POS artifacts ungated is materially different risk from stale HTML.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The un-WAF'd ISO-20022/SEPA mesh gateway accepts caller-influenced paths or bodies and forwards them to a service that performs outbound fetches
class: SSRF
asset: iso20022.sumup.com (istio-envoy, `x-envoy-decorator-operation: iso20022-edge-libcluster-headless.br-terminals.svc.cluster.local:3000/*`), iso20022-edge.sumup.com (awselb/2.0)
confidence: 50
reasoning: Carried at 50. No Cloudflare, no CDN, no WAF, internet-reachable payment rail. Answers 400 Bad Request (11 B) identically on `/` and `/messages`, rejecting on content rather than path or auth — the signature of a mesh gateway expecting ISO 20022 XML. A foreign `Origin` returned **zero** `access-control-*` headers, so permissive CORS is refuted as the class. Both names share one AWS eu-west-1 address set but serve different upstreams, so at least two services sit behind it.
evidence_needed: whether the gateway accepts any non-ISO-20022 content type or method, and whether any path reaches a server-side fetch. Both require a POST, which the passive-only rule forbids — that is the whole reason this cannot be advanced or killed here.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/ | grep -iE '^(HTTP/|server:|x-envoy)'` (expect 400 + istio-envoy) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/messages` (expect identical 400/11 B, proving path-independence) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null -H 'Origin: https://evil.example' https://iso20022.sumup.com/` (expect no access-control headers) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022-edge.sumup.com/` (expect 404 + awselb/2.0)
impact: Unproven. If it forwards caller-influenced paths or bodies to a service performing outbound fetches, that is SSRF on a SEPA payment rail with no WAF — one of the highest-consequence outcomes available. That consequence is a reason to keep testing, not a reason to raise the number. Capped Medium as posture.
