## STEP 6 — LEARNING

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `js.sumup.com/api`: the whole finding came from **recovering the call shape instead of guessing path shapes**. Six cycles closed this origin with a 6-shape prefix sweep; the real paths were sitting in `sdk.js` and the sweep was structurally incapable of hitting them, because the client only issues the request when the id matches a UUID regex. The same shape of error the KB already recorded for the legacy-callback sweep — *an exhaustive sweep is only exhaustive over the values actually sent* — had simply been repeated on a different asset, by me, and the general lesson had not transferred. Two disciplines that paid off here are both about checking a citation against the artifact: `hosted.js` was credited with an `apiBFF` reference it does not contain, and a `checkoutId` pattern three cycles had recorded as unrecoverable is a static literal in a different file on the same host.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ `js.sumup.com/api`: treating `access-control-allow-origin: *` + `access-control-allow-credentials: true` as token theft. The pair is present, and the instinct to file it is strong — the program has a real CORS history here. Reading `Bi` shows `withCredentials` is set only when `opts.credentials === "include"`, and `fetchCheckout` passes `{method:"GET"}`, so the call is cookieless and browsers reject `*` for credentialed requests regardless. Hardening only, consistent with the program's existing rejection of that class on `mcp.sumup.com`. Writing it into the report as explicitly *not* the finding stops a later cycle from re-deriving the same claim as new.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ `reports/`: eight cycles of "verify your writes" advice, and this cycle finally falsified the diagnosis the advice was built on. The filesystem was writable, the bash heredoc worked, and the `write` tool **also** worked when tested in isolation — so the streak was intermittent write failure, not a missing-verification discipline. The lesson that actually generalises is narrower than "verify": when a failure repeats, test the failing component in isolation rather than re-running the failing path, because seven cycles of re-writing are what kept the wrong cause alive. The mechanism that has never failed is heredoc and `sha256sum` in one act — 3 for 3, with the hash printed in the same command as the write, so there is no window in which a `[NEW]` line can be composed from intent.
