# js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id} — unauthenticated per-checkout resource resolution on the hosted-fields BFF

**Asset:** `https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{checkoutId}` (Vercel; second API origin, referenced by the first-party SDK)
**Class:** MISCONFIG / BOLA (CWE-639 Authorization Bypass Through User-Controlled Key, CWE-306 Missing Authentication)
**Severity:** Medium as posture. Disclosure leg **unproven** — see §6.
**Testability:** PASSIVE — anonymous GET only, no auth, no customer data touched
**Evidence:** `https://gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` = 291,877 B,
`sha256 0fae546ae34ee5e97cf0c3107d87adaa79129983d423bce6cf9a0fc19916c7bd` (re-fetched 2026-09-27)

---

## 1. The surface

`gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` builds two API clients. One is the public API:

```js
Fi = (e, t, n = Ai(window.location.href)) => {
  const r = Bi(e, t, n, "v0.1", ...);   // api.sumup.com, versioned
  const i = Bi(e, t, n, "v0.2", ...);
  return { checkouts: Di(i, n), paymentMethods: Ei(i), analytics: qi(r) };
}
```

The other is an internal BFF with a **null version segment**:

```js
apiBFF: zi("https://js.sumup.com/api", this.props.sessionId)
```

so its URLs are `https://js.sumup.com/api/<path>` with no version prefix. Enumerating a
`/api` *prefix* cannot reach it, which is why six earlier cycles of prefix sweeps closed this
origin as "uniformly 404" and were wrong.

## 2. The BFF route table, measured

The response *class* is the discriminator: the Vercel catch-all is 84 B `text/plain`
`NOT_FOUND`, while any application-routed path returns `application/json`.

| Request | Code | Size | Type | Class |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /api/checkouts/{uuid}` | 404 | 181 | `application/json` | **app-routed, resource resolved** |
| `GET /api/checkouts/{uuid}/fee-calculation` | 404 | 144 | `application/json` | **app-routed, method mismatch** |
| `GET /api/checkouts/{uuid}/apple-pay-session` | 404 | 84 | `text/plain` | Vercel stub — **not on this BFF** |
| `GET /api/checkouts` | 404 | 84 | `text/plain` | Vercel stub |
| `GET /api/merchants/{uuid}` | 404 | 84 | `text/plain` | Vercel stub |

The BFF exposes exactly **two** application routes, both checkout-scoped. Bodies:

```
{"title":"Not Found","status":404,"detail":"Checkout <id> does not exist.",
 "type":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9110#name-404-not-found"}
{"title":"Not Found","status":404,"detail":"Endpoint does not exist.", ...}
```

**Correction to the previous cycle:** that cycle recorded `apple-pay-session` as a third
response class on this BFF. It is not. `apple-pay-session` and `payment-methods` are issued by
`Di`/`Ei` against `api.sumup.com` v0.2 via `Fi`, not against the BFF. The BFF has two routes.

`fee-calculation` is fee-touching: `fetchFeesByCheckout:(t,n)=>e.endpoint(\`checkouts/${t}/fee-calculation\`,{method:"POST",...})`.
It was confirmed routed with a GET; the POST was **not** issued (mutating).

## 3. The finding: the session header is a verbatim echo of the path id, and is not enforced

`Bi` is the shared endpoint builder. It injects the widget session into **every** call:

```js
Bi = (host, sessionId, session, version, transform) => ({
  host,
  endpoint: (path, opts = {}) => {
    opts.headers = { ...opts.headers, "X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id": sessionId };
    if (session) opts.headers["Sumup-Product-Origin"] = session.origin;
    url = `${host}/${version ? version + "/" : ""}${path}`;
    ...
    xhr.withCredentials = (opts.credentials === "include");
  }
})
```

`sessionId` originates from `Ai(window.location.href)`, which parses
`https://checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` and returns `{ origin: "Hosted Checkout", sessionId: <id> }`.

**So the value of `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` is the same checkout id that is already in the URL
path.** It carries no information an attacker does not already supply. It is a correlation
header, not a credential.

`fetchCheckout` sends nothing else — `{method:"GET"}`, so `withCredentials` is `false` and the
call is cookieless. No `Authorization` is ever attached anywhere in this client.

The server does not enforce the header either. Controlled three-way test, same id:

```console
$ curl -sS -H "X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: $ID" -H "Sumup-Product-Origin: Hosted Checkout" \
    "https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/$ID" -o a.body
$ curl -sS "https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/$ID" -o b.body
$ curl -sS -H "X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: $ID" \
    "https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/$ID?attempts=1" -o c.body
$ md5sum a.body b.body c.body
5e19511ce3b7d04787ca40001d2a7f03  a.body
5e19511ce3b7d04787ca40001d2a7f03  b.body
5e19511ce3b7d04787ca40001d2a7f03  c.body
```

**Byte-identical.** Reproducing the first-party call exactly yields nothing an anonymous
`curl` did not already get. The absence of a credential on this read path is therefore not a
gap in how the client calls it — there is no credential to present.

## 4. Client/server validation divergence

The client refuses to issue the request for a malformed id:

```js
xi = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
wi = e => xi.test(e)
// fetchCheckout: (t,n)=>{ ... !wi(t) && Ci(t) ... e.endpoint(`checkouts/${t}...`, {method:"GET"}) }
```

The server applies no equivalent check:

```console
$ curl -sS "https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/AAAA-not-a-uuid"
{"title":"Not Found","status":404,"detail":"Checkout AAAA-not-a-uuid does not exist.", ...}
```

The id is looked up and reflected. **The only id-shape gate on this path is client-side.**
(Supersedes an earlier note that the pattern was unrecoverable: it is a static literal in
`sdk.js`.)

## 5. Demo branch is inert — checked, not assumed

`processCheckout` branches three ways. `Ni` is true when the checkoutId lowercases to
`"demo"` or `"demo-sandbox"`, and then calls `Ti`:

```js
Ti = e => Promise.resolve({ demo: !0,
  message: "This is a demo. Not a real checkout is processed.", payload: e })
```

It resolves a literal and **never touches the network**. Not a finding; recorded so a later
cycle does not re-derive it as one.

## 6. Impact, and the leg that is NOT claimed

**Proven:** an unauthenticated, application-routed, per-checkout resource-resolution oracle on
a second money-touching origin, with no id validation server-side and no credential in front of
resource resolution. Response content on the success path is described by the first-party client
as `id, currency, amount, status, merchant_code, merchant_name, merchant_country,
merchant_sandbox, purpose, lxn_session_token` — i.e. merchant identity and transaction amount.

**Not claimed:** that any of those fields are reachable. That requires a checkout id that
exists. Checkout ids are UUIDv4 and not enumerable, and the program forbids testing against
customer data, so this was **deliberately not attempted**. The finding is therefore filed on
posture, capped Medium, with the disclosure leg explicitly unproven rather than assumed.

If the success path is later shown reachable with no valid widget session, this becomes a
pre-auth BOLA disclosing merchant identity and amounts for any checkout id, and should be
re-rated High.

`lxn_session_token` in the same payload deserves specific attention: if it is a bearer for the
payment-intent leg, its exposure would be materially worse than a metadata disclosure.

## 7. CORS on this origin — hardening only, explicitly not the finding

`OPTIONS /api/checkouts/{id}` with a preflight returns:

```
access-control-allow-origin: *
access-control-allow-credentials: true
access-control-allow-methods: GET,OPTIONS,PATCH,DELETE,POST,PUT
access-control-allow-headers: *
```

The `*` + `true` pair is the exact combination that has produced findings elsewhere in this
program, so it is recorded here. It is **not** exploitable as token theft:
`xhr.withCredentials` is set only when `opts.credentials === "include"`, and this client never
passes it, so no ambient cookie can be attached, and browsers reject `*` on credentialed
requests regardless. Since the resource requires no credential at all, permissive CORS grants
an attacker no capability they lacked. Hardening only.

## 8. Remediation

1. Require a real authorization check on the BFF read path — a signature or bound session the
   server validates, not a header whose value is the resource id.
2. Validate `checkoutId` server-side against the same UUID rule the client enforces; reject
   non-UUID input before any lookup.
3. If the BFF is intended to be merchant-scoped, bind it to a session and return only fields
   that session is entitled to; `lxn_session_token` in particular.
4. Add a control to `fee-calculation` before it is reachable in production.

## 9. Verification

