reasoning: `Bi` injects `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: sessionId` unconditionally on every call including the BFF; `sessionId` is `Ai()`'s capture group 1 of `/\/pay\/([^?/#]+)/` on `checkout.sumup.com`, i.e. the path segment already in the URL. `fetchCheckout` sends `{method:"GET"}` so `withCredentials` is false; no `Authorization` is attached anywhere in this client. 3-way control byte-identical (md5 `5e19511c…`) with header / without / `?attempts=1`. Client gates on `wi=e=>xi.test(e)`; server applies no shape check (`AAAA-not-a-uuid` looked up and reflected). Success-path fields named by the client: `id, currency, amount, status, merchant_code, merchant_name, merchant_country, merchant_sandbox, purpose, lxn_session_token`.
evidence_needed: a checkout id that exists returning 200 with that object and **no** valid widget session. Deliberately not obtained — valid ids are customer data and the program forbids testing against it. This is why 74 and not higher: the missing-authorization posture is proven, the disclosure is not.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download} %{content_type}\n' 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555'` (expect `404 181 application/json`) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download} %{content_type}\n' https://js.sumup.com/api/zzzz` (expect `404 84 text/plain`, the stub control) -> `curl -sS https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/AAAA-not-a-uuid` (expect id reflected, proving no server-side UUID check) -> repeat the first with `-H "X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: <same id>"` and diff against the headerless body (expect identical) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download}\n' 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/<uuid>/apple-pay-session'` (expect `404 84`, proving it is not on this origin)
impact: Proven: unauthenticated, application-routed resource resolution on a second money-touching origin, with no server-side id validation and no credential in front of resolution. If the 200 path is reachable without a valid session it becomes a pre-auth BOLA disclosing merchant identity and amounts, escalating to High; `lxn_session_token` in the same payload would be worse than a metadata disclosure. Capped Medium as posture, disclosure leg explicitly unclaimed.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The payment credential is a URL path segment on a frame that declares no Referrer-Policy, so the id that the BFF does not check is also the id most likely to leak
class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (frame served to merchants) → js.sumup.com/api (BFF that consumes the id)
confidence: 48
reasoning: `Ai()` verbatim returns `{origin:"Hosted Checkout", sessionId: <path segment of /pay/{id}>}` — the credential and the resource identifier are the same string, by construction. `Referrer-Policy` is absent from `/`, `/hosted.js` and `/sdk.js` (0 headers each) and is not set in the frame document, so the frame's own referrer handling is the browser default. The frame demonstrably issues cross-origin requests to `api.sumup.com` and `js.sumup.com`.
evidence_needed: evidence that a full path (not origin-only) actually reaches a third party. Not obtained, and the browser default `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` downgrades cross-origin referrers to origin-only, which bounds this chain — the same control the gateway report records for the outbound `postMessage`. A legacy referrer policy anywhere in the merchant embed path, or a same-origin subresource request carrying the path, would break that bound.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ | grep -icE '^referrer-policy'` (expect 0) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -icE '^referrer-policy'` (expect 0) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js | grep -icE '^referrer-policy'` (expect 0) -> `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/ | grep -iocE 'referrer'` (expect 0, not set in-document)
impact: If it held, the "unguessable id" bound on the BFF finding would collapse: ids would be disclosed in ordinary web logs, turning a Medium posture finding into a practical pre-auth BOLA. Because the default browser policy bounds it, this is filed as a **hardening** requirement in both reports rather than a separate disclosure claim.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The un-WAF'd ISO-20022/SEPA mesh gateway accepts caller-influenced paths or bodies and forwards them to a service that performs outbound fetches
class: SSRF
asset: iso20022.sumup.com (istio-envoy, `x-envoy-decorator-operation: iso20022-edge-libcluster-headless.br-terminals.svc.cluster.local:3000/*`), iso20022-edge.sumup.com (awselb/2.0)
confidence: 50
reasoning: Carried at 50, unchanged. No Cloudflare, no CDN, no WAF, internet-reachable payment rail. Answers 400 Bad Request (11 B) identically on `/` and `/messages`, rejecting on content rather than path or auth — the signature of a mesh gateway expecting ISO 20022 XML. A foreign `Origin` returned **zero** `access-control-*` headers, so permissive CORS is refuted as the class. Both names share one AWS eu-west-1 address set but serve different upstreams.
evidence_needed: whether the gateway accepts any non-ISO-20022 content type or method, and whether any path reaches a server-side fetch. Both require a POST, which the passive-only rule forbids — that is the whole reason this cannot be advanced or killed here.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/ | grep -iE '^(HTTP/|server:|x-envoy)'` (expect 400 + istio-envoy) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/messages` (expect identical 400/11 B, proving path-independence) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null -H 'Origin: https://evil.example' https://iso20022.sumup.com/` (expect no access-control headers) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022-edge.sumup.com/` (expect 404 + awselb/2.0)
impact: Unproven. SSRF on a SEPA payment rail with no WAF is one of the highest-consequence outcomes available. That consequence is a reason to keep testing, not a reason to raise the number. Capped Medium as posture.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

