[NEXT] HUMAN: submit both reports to bugs.olivermaicher.eu, then re-check delivery in one command. (1) `reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md` — 146 lines, 6,578 B, sha256 `f5153ea1d18f5ed47c9ab3a0191e7d8de16ad1655417251e9360430951eae1ea` — Medium, PASSIVE, anonymous, no card or customer data. (2) `reports/js-bff-checkout-resource-resolution.md` — 202 lines, 9,262 B, sha256 `9bb0c324efa46cfe96a39629fa102bd315e17766e476ecca28e8ef17ef6c5f53` — Medium, PASSIVE, anonymous GET, disclosure leg marked UNPROVEN in the report itself. Command: `sha256sum reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md reports/js-bff-checkout-resource-resolution.md`. This is the 20th cycle a complete finding has awaited a human; the blocker is submission, not triage or evidence, and no further passive probing of either asset can improve either report.

## STEP 6 — LEARNING

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `js.sumup.com/api`: the decisive evidence was not the endpoint existing, it was **proving the credential is void by control**. Every prior cycle described the header as "not sent"; this cycle read `Bi` and found it is sent unconditionally, then showed the response is byte-identical with it, without it, and with the query param. A missing credential and an *unenforced* credential are different findings with different remediation, and only the second one is provable passively. The general move: when a client appears to send nothing, read the shared request builder before concluding it sends nothing — the builder may inject a field the call site never mentions.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ `js.sumup.com/api`: `access-control-allow-origin: *` + `access-control-allow-credentials: true` on the BFF is hardening-only, and the correct reason is narrower than the previous cycle's. `xhr.withCredentials` is set only when `opts.credentials === "include"`, so no ambient cookie can ride along and browsers reject `*` on credentialed requests regardless. The decisive addition: because the resource needs **no credential at all**, permissive CORS hands a cross-origin attacker no capability they did not already have. Written into the report as explicitly *not* the finding, so a later cycle cannot re-derive it as novel.

[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ `js.sumup.com` and `iso20022`: three distinct 404 response classes (181 B `application/json` with a reflected id, 144 B `application/json`, 84 B `text/plain`) mean the 84 B stub is the **absence** of a route, not a route. Two of my own prior claims inverted this — `apple-pay-session` was recorded as a routed BFF sibling when it is the stub, and a six-shape `/api` prefix sweep "closed" an origin whose real paths carry a **null version segment** and therefore sit under no recognisable prefix. Size-plus-content-type, not status code, is the discriminator on Vercel-hosted apps.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ `reports/`: the ten-cycle file-creation streak ended the moment the write and the `sha256sum` became **one act** rather than two. The earlier diagnosis — "intermittent write failure", "missing verification discipline" — was wrong twice over, and the reason it survived nine cycles is that each cycle re-ran the *failing path* with a slightly different ceremony instead of testing the component. Tested in isolation this cycle, heredoc wrote 11 B on the first attempt, and both reports landed first-try. 4 for 4 now. The rule that finally generalised is not about verification at all: **when a failure repeats, test the failing component alone, because re-running the failing path is what keeps an unexamined cause alive.**

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ `iso20022.sumup.com`: 35 cycles, no new evidence, and the number has not moved. Restating it at its measured value every cycle is the cheapest way to keep a hypothesis alive, but it is not progress; the honest position is that this asset is blocked on an active POST the program's rules forbid, and the correct next step is a human deciding whether to authorise one rather than another analyst re-reading the same 400.

## STEP 7 — RISK

[RISK] sumup: 76. Up 2. The gain is delivery, not discovery — the first two artifacts in ten cycles that are verifiably on disk, each hashed in the same command that created it, with `valid-bugs.md` citing both by path, line count and hash so a human can confirm in one `sha256sum`. The mechanism is now 4 for 4, which matters more than the count: nine cycles of re-writing under an unexamined cause was the actual risk, and testing the component in isolation dissolved it in one round. The new BFF finding is honestly bounded — the missing-authorization posture is proven by a byte-identical control, the disclosure is explicitly unclaimed because proving it needs customer data I am forbidden to touch, and the report says so in its own impact section. I am not scoring the submission itself as risk reduction; 20 cycles is long enough that treating these as filed would be the same category of error as the file claims, one level up.
