[0m$ [0mcd /home/runner/work/sumup-hunt/sumup-hunt && cat > reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md <<'EOF'
# gateway.sumup.com — hosted-fields frame accepts privileged postMessage from any origin

Asset:     https://gateway.sumup.com/  (SumUp hosted-fields / card-entry iframe)
Bundle:    https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js
Verified:  2026-09-27 ~21:00 UTC, live, read-only (GET only)
Severity:  Medium  (missing-authorization posture proven; disclosure leg explicitly UNPROVEN)
Class:     Cross-origin trust / missing message-origin validation
Auth req:  none — fully anonymous
Report by: automated analyst, bugs.olivermaicher.eu

## 1. Summary

`gateway.sumup.com` serves SumUp's hosted-fields card-entry frame. It exposes a
`postMessage` API to its embedder. The **inbound** direction of that channel —
the one that carries `form--submit` and therefore drives a payment write — is
scoped by nothing but four attacker-controlled JSON string fields. The
`event.origin` property is never read anywhere in the bundle.

The frame also ships **no** `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy`
(`frame-ancestors`), and no `Referrer-Policy`, so any origin may frame it.

The consequence proven below is a **missing-authorization posture**: an
arbitrary origin can make the frame emit a state-changing
`PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{checkoutId}` against the production payments API with an
attacker-chosen checkout id and attacker-supplied body. Whether that PUT is
*accepted* by the server depends on a valid widget session and a valid payload,
neither of which this report claims or tests.

## 2. Reproduction of the precondition (framing)

    $ curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/
    HTTP=200 SIZE=546
    (no x-frame-options, no content-security-policy, no referrer-policy)

Served document body (546 B, complete):

    <title>hostedfields</title>
    <script type="text/javascript" src="./hosted.js"></script>
    <script type="text/javascript">
      const hform = HostedForm.init(window, window.parent || window.top)
      window.addEventListener('load',  () => { hform.onLoad();  });
      window.addEventListener('beforeunload', () => { hform.onUnload(); }, false);
    </script>

`HostedForm.init(window, window.parent || window.top)` — when this document is
framed by an attacker, `window.parent` **is** the attacker. Initialization is
already pointed at the embedder by design; the defect is that the inbound
channel is never scoped back to it.

## 3. The defect: the origin guard is applied in one direction only

Bundle identity (re-fetched this cycle, byte-identical):

    $ curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum
    1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873   (26,839 B)

### 3.1 The messenger factory accepts an `origin` parameter

    u = ({id: e, fromWindow: r, toWindow: t, variant: a,
         origin: n, autoMount: u = !1,
         onError: l = ({error: e}) => { throw e }}) => { ... }

### 3.2 The inbound handler never reads it

    p = r => { const {data: n, source: i} = r; ... }

The event object is destructured to `{data, source}` and nothing else.
`event.origin` is not read. Grep evidence over the 26,839 B bundle:

    event\.origin      0
    origin!==          0
    origin===          0
    targetOrigin       0
    parentOrigin       0

### 3.3 The single use of `n` (the origin) is on the OUTBOUND path

    send: s => { try {
      const e = Object.assign({variant: a, type: o.TYPE, action: o.ACTION_MESSAGE}, s);
      if (t) return void t.postMessage(e, n || "*");     // <-- origin used HERE only
      r && r.parent.postMessage(e, "*")
    } catch (r) { ... } }

### 3.4 The construction site does pass it

    function Re(e, r) { const t = u({
        id: e.name, fromWindow: e, toWindow: e.parent,
        variant: "hostedfield",
        origin: document.referrer
    }); ... }

**This is the reportable shape.** A developer reading this code sees an `origin`
option, sees it being supplied, and reasonably concludes origin scoping is in
place. It protects only the SumUp -> merchant direction. The direction that
matters — merchant -> SumUp, carrying `form--submit` — is unguarded. The
remediation is a one-line change to the inbound handler, and the entire reason
it is missing is that the guard exists but on the wrong side.

## 4. The complete inbound guard set, enumerated

The messenger filters an inbound payload with:

    (e, r, t) => Boolean(
        d(e)                              // e.type === "SumUpCard"
     && e.action === o.ACTION_MESSAGE     // e.action === "message"
     && (e.variant === r || t.ignoreVariant)  // e.variant === "hostedfield"
     && e.message                        // must be truthy
    )

plus, in the outer handler, a self-echo check `t && i !== t` comparing
`event.source` against the stored `fromWindow`.

Every one of the four payload fields is an **attacker-controlled string**, and
all three constant values are published in the public bundle
(`o = Object.freeze({TYPE:"SumUpCard", ACTION_MESSAGE:"message"})`,
`variant: "hostedfield"`). The self-echo check blocks the frame from processing
its own messages; it says nothing about which origin sent them.

There is no origin check, no allowlist, and no credential.

## 5. What the attacker reaches: the payment write

`form--submit` routes to a handler that builds a payload and calls the API
client constructed with base `https://api.sumup.com`:

    ... }("https://api.sumup.com", n, s); let o = {}, d = [];
    if ("card" === a.payment_type) {
      if (d = Ce(r.frames), !d.length) return;      // <-- frame-access gate
      const e = d.filter(e => !e.isValid);
      if (e.length) return ...                       // <-- input-validity gate
      o = $e(d.filter(...).map(...));                // <-- collect card fields
    }
    const u = be({ payment_type: a.payment_type || "card", ...,
                   card: {...a.card, ...o}, ... });

    // API client write paths:
    le(r) ? s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r), {method:"PUT", ...}) .then(...) : pe(r)
    le(r) ? s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r, "/apple-pay-session"),
                       {method:"PUT", ...}) : pe(r)

**The frame-access gate is inside the `payment_type === "card"` branch.** Set
`payment_type` to any other value and `Ce(r.frames)` is never called, the
input-validity check never runs, and `o` stays `{}` — so the emitted body is
entirely attacker-supplied, with no cooperating frames required.

A conditional guard that is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator is not a
guard. Any PoC for this issue must vary `payment_type`, not just use the happy
path value.

## 6. The last remaining gate is a public, static literal

    ue = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
    le = e => ue.test(e)
    pe = e => Promise.reject(new me("Invalid UUID checkout: ".concat(e)))

**Correction to earlier cycles.** Multiple prior cycles recorded this gate as a
runtime pattern "unrecoverable from served bytes", on the strength of a
`new RegExp(a.pattern)` occurrence in this bundle. That constructor is the
**card-brand / IIN detection table**, not the checkout-id gate:

    ",pattern:"^((627416)|(637202)|(637200)|(637036)|(637201))",min:16,max:16
    for (...) { const a = t[e]; if (new RegExp(a.pattern).test(r)) ... }

The checkout-id gate is the static `ue` literal above. It constrains the id
space to UUID versions 1-5, but the pattern ships in the public bundle, so it
costs an attacker nothing to satisfy.

## 7. The outbound direction is broken by a bug, not a control

`origin: document.referrer` is a full URL (e.g. `https://merchant.example/cart`).
`postMessage` requires a bare origin as `targetOrigin`, so
`t.postMessage(e, "https://merchant.example/cart")` throws. The `try/catch`
converts it to a logged `Error while sending message`.

**This must be fixed in the same change as the inbound origin check.** The
current lack of a response-exfiltration path is a property of a value having the
wrong shape, not a security control. Anyone repairing the outbound channel will
naturally reach for the inbound handler, and will find the `origin` variable
already in scope and already used — which reads as "already handled". Fixing the
outbound bug alone, without adding the inbound check, silently escalates this
issue to include response exfiltration.

## 8. Impact

**Proven (anonymous, no credentials, no customer data touched):**
- A PCI card-entry frame is framable by any origin.
- Its privileged `postMessage` inbound channel has no origin validation.
- Any origin can drive it to emit a state-changing
  `PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{checkoutId}` with an
  attacker-chosen id and an attacker-supplied body.

**Not claimed:** that the PUT is *accepted*. Acceptance requires a valid widget
session and a valid payload. This was deliberately not tested — valid checkout
ids and sessions are customer data, and the program excludes exposure of
customer/financial data during testing. If an attacker holds a victim's
checkout id, the likely outcome is a payment attempt on that checkout; the
severity of *that* is a business-logic judgement for the triager and is bounded
by the widget-session check on the server.

Suggested severity: **Medium**. Remediation is two lines: validate `event.origin`
against an allowlist in `p`, and add
`frame-ancestors 'self' https://*.sumup.com` (or XFO) to the frame document.

## 9. Out-of-scope classes explicitly NOT claimed

- Response exfiltration via `postMessage` — blocked today by the malformed
  `targetOrigin` described in section 7, which is a bug, not a control.
- Widget-session replay — the session id is parsed from
  `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` by `Ai` and is a URL-borne resource identifier,
  not a secret. Disclosure crosses no privilege boundary. Hardening note only.
- Framing alone — framed without the messenger exploit, this is clickjacking
  without a demonstrated exploit, which the program lists out of scope. The
  messenger drive is the finding.
- Referrer leakage of the checkout id — `Referrer-Policy` is indeed absent, but
  the browser default `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` downgrades cross-origin
  referrers to origin-only, which bounds the chain. Hardening note only.

