# js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id} — unauthenticated checkout resource resolution, no id validation

Asset:     https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{checkoutId}   (Vercel)
Origin:    referenced as the `apiBFF` base by gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js
Verified:  2026-09-27 ~21:00 UTC, live, read-only (GET only)
Severity:  Medium (posture proven; disclosure leg explicitly UNPROVEN)
Class:     Missing authorization / missing input validation on a money-touching origin
Auth req:  none — fully anonymous
Report by: automated analyst, bugs.olivermaicher.eu

## 1. Summary

`js.sumup.com` serves a BFF endpoint, `GET /api/checkouts/{id}`, which resolves a
checkout id with **no credential of any kind** and **no server-side validation
of the id format**. The 404 body reflects the supplied id verbatim.

This is a second, money-touching API origin separate from `api.sumup.com`. It is
built by SumUp's own first-party SDK, so its request shape and its credential
model are both recoverable from a public bundle.

## 2. Live evidence — three distinct response classes

    $ for p in checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 \
               checkouts/AAAA-not-a-uuid  zzzz ; do
        curl -sS -o body.txt -w "%{http_code} %{size_download} %{content_type}\n" \
          "https://js.sumup.com/api/$p"; done

| Path | Status | Size | Content-Type | Body |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555` | 404 | 181 B | `application/json` | `{"title":"Not Found","status":404,"detail":"Checkout 11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 does not exist.","type":"https://datatracker.ietf.org/..."}` |
| `checkouts/AAAA-not-a-uuid` | 404 | **160 B** | `application/json` | `...,"detail":"Checkout AAAA-not-a-uuid does not exist.",...` |
| `zzzz` | 404 | 84 B | `text/plain` | `The page could not be found` (Vercel stub) |

Three facts follow.

**(a) The route is application-routed, not a stub.** The 181 B / 160 B responses
are RFC 9457 `application/json` problem documents from a handler. The 84 B
`text/plain` response is the Vercel "no route" stub — that is the **absence** of
a route, not a route. Size-plus-content-type, not status code, is the
discriminator on Vercel-hosted apps; all three are 404.

**(b) The id is reflected, and the response size tracks the id length.**
181 - 160 = 21 B, matching the 36 - 16 = 20-character difference between the
two ids (plus one separator). The handler is echoing attacker input into the
response.

**(c) There is no server-side id validation.** `AAAA-not-a-uuid` is not
rejected as malformed — it is looked up and reported as "does not exist", i.e.
it is treated as a well-formed-but-absent resource.

No `Authorization` header, no cookie, and no `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` were
sent in any of the above. All three are anonymous.

## 3. The credential is provably not load-bearing

The first-party SDK injects `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` unconditionally in its
shared request builder, so prior cycles recorded the header as "not sent". That
was wrong. The correct statement is stronger: the header **is** sent, and the
server **ignores** it.

Three-way control, byte-identical response:

    # with the header
    curl -sS -H "X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: 11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555" \
      https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 | md5sum
    # without the header
    curl -sS \
      https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 | md5sum
    # with an extra query parameter
    curl -sS \
      'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555?attempts=1' | md5sum
    # all three: md5 5e19511ce3b7d04787ca40001d2a7f03

A missing credential and an **unenforced** credential are different findings with
different remediation. Only the second is provable without authentication, and
it is the one that matters here: adding the check to the code would not change
behaviour, so the gap is architectural rather than an oversight in one handler.

The header's value is not a secret either. It is a regex capture of the path
segment of `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` (`Ai`), i.e. the checkout id that is
already in the merchant's URL.

## 4. Client-side gate, absent server-side

The SDK gates the call client-side on a static literal:

    xi = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
    wi = e => xi.test(e)

The client throws before issuing the request if the id does not match. The
server applies **no equivalent check** — `AAAA-not-a-uuid` is looked up and
reflected. The gate exists in the only place an attacker does not run.

## 5. Route table correction

Earlier cycles recorded `/api/checkouts/{id}/apple-pay-session` as a third
response class on this origin. It is the 84 B `text/plain` Vercel stub — it is
issued by `gateway.sumup.com`'s own code against `api.sumup.com` v0.2, not by
this BFF.

This BFF has exactly two application routes:

- `GET  checkouts/{id}`                 -> 181 B `application/json`
- `POST checkouts/{id}/fee-calculation` -> 144 B `application/json` (confirmed
  routed by a GET returning "Endpoint does not exist."; the POST was not issued)

`/api/checkouts` and `/api/merchants/{uuid}` are stubs.

## 6. Impact

**Proven:** unauthenticated, application-routed per-resource resolution on a
second money-touching origin, with no server-side id validation, no enforced
credential, and attacker-controlled input reflected in the response.

**Not claimed:** any disclosure. This is a 404 for a non-existent id. Whether a
**valid, existing** checkout id returns 200 with the checkout object was
deliberately not tested — real checkout ids are customer data, and the program
excludes exposure of customer/financial data during testing. That is why this is
filed at Medium as a posture finding and not higher.

If the 200 path is reachable without a valid session, this becomes a pre-auth
BOLA disclosing merchant identity, amount, currency and status. The SDK's own
success path additionally names `lxn_session_token` in the response object,
which would be materially worse than a metadata disclosure — but that is
speculation about a field name, not an observed response, and is not claimed.

## 7. Hardening notes, explicitly NOT the finding

- `access-control-allow-origin: *` **and** `access-control-allow-credentials: true`
  are both present. This is **not** token theft. The shared builder `Bi` sets
  `xhr.withCredentials` only when `opts.credentials === "include"`, and
  `fetchCheckout` passes `{method:"GET"}`, so the call is cookieless and browsers
  reject `*` for credentialed requests regardless. Decisive point: the resource
  needs **no credential at all**, so permissive CORS hands a cross-origin
  attacker no capability they did not already have. Recorded so a later cycle
  does not re-derive this as novel — it is the same class the program has
  already rejected on `mcp.sumup.com`.
- The `referrer` hardening note in the companion gateway report applies here too.

