## STEP 3 — HYPOTHESES

[HYP] The hosted-fields origin guard is present but wired to the wrong direction, so the privileged inbound channel is open to every origin
class: MISCONFIG
asset: gateway.sumup.com (Vercel; PCI card-entry iframe) -> api.sumup.com v0.2
confidence: 88
reasoning: Messenger factory accepts `origin:n`; container passes `origin:document.referrer`; sole use is outbound `t.postMessage(e,n||"*")`. Inbound handler `p=r=>{const{data:n,source:i}=r}` never reads `event.origin`; greps `event.origin`/`origin===`/`origin!==`/`targetOrigin` all 0. Inbound gates are four attacker-controlled JSON strings (`type`,`action`,`variant`,`message`) plus a self-echo check on `event.source`; all three constants ship in the public bundle. Frame ships no XFO, no CSP `frame-ancestors`, no Referrer-Policy; root 200/546 B. The frame-access gate `if((d=Ce(r.frames)),!d.length)return;` sits inside `if("card"===a.payment_type)`, so a non-card `payment_type` skips it and leaves the body fully attacker-supplied. Remaining gate is the static public literal `ue`/`le`.
evidence_needed: a browser-confirmed cross-origin `form--submit` from an attacker page producing a live `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` — obtained in a prior cycle via netlog + iframe console. The *acceptance* leg is not claimed and must not be tested: it needs a real customer checkout id.
verify_steps: `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum` (expect `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf17e0fd59cbecc9f220873`) -> `curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -c 'event\.origin'` (expect 0) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ | grep -icE '^(x-frame-options|content-security-policy)'` (expect 0)
impact: Proven: any origin can drive a PCI card-entry frame to emit a state-changing payment write with attacker-chosen id and body. Capped Medium — the missing-authorization posture is proven, the authorization outcome is not. Remediation must land the inbound `event.origin` check together with the outbound `targetOrigin` bug fix, since a guard currently held by a malformed value disappears the moment that value is corrected.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The BFF enforces no credential and no id validation, so checkout resolution is bounded only by id unguessability
class: IDOR
asset: js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{checkoutId} (Vercel; second money-touching origin built by gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js)
confidence: 74
reasoning: 404/181 B `application/json` problem+json with the id reflected; 404/160 B for `AAAA-not-a-uuid` proves no server-side format check (size tracks id length); 84 B `text/plain` is the no-route stub, not a third class. Three-way control byte-identical at md5 `5e19511ce3b7d04787ca40001d2a7f03` with header / without / `?attempts=1` — the SDK's `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` is injected unconditionally by `Bi` and ignored by the server. Client gate `wi=e=>xi.test(e)` on a static `xi` literal; no server equivalent.
evidence_needed: an existing checkout id returning 200 with the checkout object and no valid widget session. Deliberately not obtained — valid ids are customer data and the program excludes exposure of customer/financial data during testing. This is why 74, not higher: the posture is proven, the disclosure is not.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download} %{content_type}\n' 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555'` (expect `404 181 application/json`) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download}\n' 'https://js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/AAAA-not-a-uuid'` (expect `404 160`, proving reflection and no format check) -> `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{size_download}\n' https://js.sumup.com/api/zzzz` (expect `404 84`, the stub control)
impact: Proven: unauthenticated application-routed per-resource resolution with no enforced credential. If the 200 path is reachable it becomes a pre-auth BOLA disclosing merchant identity and amounts, escalating to High; `lxn_session_token` in the same payload would be worse — but that is a field name read from client code, not an observed response, and is not claimed. Capped Medium as posture.
testability: PASSIVE

[HYP] The un-WAF'd ISO-20022/SEPA mesh gateway accepts caller-influenced paths or bodies and forwards them to a service that performs outbound fetches
class: SSRF
asset: iso20022.sumup.com (istio-envoy, `x-envoy-decorator-operation: iso20022-edge-libcluster-headless.br-terminals.svc.cluster.local:3000/*`), iso20022-edge.sumup.com (awselb/2.0)
confidence: 50
reasoning: Carried at 50, unchanged. No Cloudflare, no CDN, no WAF, internet-reachable payment rail. Answers 400 Bad Request (11 B) identically on `/` and `/messages` — rejecting on content, not path or auth, the signature of a mesh gateway expecting ISO 20022 XML. A foreign `Origin` returns zero `access-control-*` headers, so permissive CORS is refuted as the class. Both names share one AWS eu-west-1 address set but serve different upstreams.
evidence_needed: whether the gateway accepts any non-ISO-20022 content type or method, and whether any path reaches a server-side fetch. Both require a POST, which the passive-only rule forbids — that is the entire reason this cannot be advanced or killed here.
verify_steps: `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/ | grep -iE '^(HTTP/|server:|x-envoy)'` (expect 400 + istio-envoy) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://iso20022.sumup.com/messages` (expect identical 400/11 B, proving path-independence) -> `curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null -H 'Origin: https://evil.example' https://iso20022.sumup.com/` (expect no access-control headers)
impact: Unproven. SSRF on a SEPA payment rail with no WAF is among the highest-consequence outcomes available. That consequence is a reason to keep testing, not a reason to raise the number. Capped Medium as posture.
testability: AUTH_HELPED

