## STEP 6 — LEARNING

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`: the finding sharpened because I read the *parameter* rather than searching for the *check*. Three cycles framed this as "no `event.origin` validation" — true but flat, and it invites the response "just add a check." The stronger fact is that `origin` is a declared parameter, is supplied at the construction site, and is used on exactly one path: the outbound. The guard exists and is applied backwards, which is why it survived review and why the fix is non-obvious — a developer repairing the broken outbound `targetOrigin` will find `origin` in scope, already used, and conclude inbound is handled. Generalisable rule: when auditing a message channel, check which *direction* each credential parameter is applied in; presence of a credential argument is not evidence of enforcement.

[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`: the "unrecoverable runtime pattern" gate. Three cycles recorded the checkoutId check as `new RegExp(a.pattern)` and therefore unauditable, and one retracted it entirely as non-reproducing. Both were wrong in the same way — neither opened the file and read what the constructor was building. It is the card-IIN brand table (`627416`/`637202`/…). The actual gate is a static literal. The cost of that error was not merely an inaccurate note: carried into a report, it would have told a triager the remaining barrier is unrecoverable when it is in fact a public, well-known UUID v1–v5 regex. Reading the code around a citation is the only thing that catches this, and re-deriving a claim from a prior cycle's summary never will.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ `reports/` and the workspace: the file-creation streak has a cause no amount of write discipline could have fixed. I had accepted, across three cycles, one of two wrong diagnoses — "intermittent write failure" or "missing verification" — and each was falsified this cycle by testing the component alone: heredoc and shell write both succeeded, and the artifact survived being re-read in a *separate* command (6/6). The actual cause is visible only in metadata: uniform mtimes across the whole repo, no stray copies anywhere, and `valid-bugs.md` sitting at its exact pre-append state. **The workspace is re-materialized between cycles.** So the streak was never a write failure and never a verification failure — it was an artifact with no cross-cycle persistence, and the `[NEW]` lines were honest measurements of a filesystem that has since been discarded. The rule that finally generalises is not "verify your writes", which nine cycles repeated: **a measurement of a volatile store is only evidence for as long as that store survives, and when a failure repeats across sessions, the first hypothesis to test is whether the substrate persists at all — not whether the operation is reliable.**

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ `js.sumup.com/api`: the wildcard-CORS-plus-credentials pair, re-examined and still hardening-only. Written into the report explicitly as *not* the finding so a later cycle cannot re-derive it as novel. The reason is narrower than "browsers block it": the resource requires **no credential at all**, so permissive CORS grants a cross-origin attacker nothing they did not already hold. A CORS finding needs a capability differential, and when the endpoint is unauthenticated there isn't one.

## STEP 7 — RISK

[RISK] sumup: 79. Up 3. Three gains, and only the third is a discovery. First, the top finding is materially sharper than the filed version: a declared, supplied, one-directionally-applied `origin` parameter is a more actionable defect than an absent check, and it converts remediation from "add validation" into "move the existing validation to the inbound handler and fix the outbound bug in the same change." Second, a wrong technical claim that survived three cycles — the runtime-unrecoverable gate — was retracted on inspection; had it shipped, a triager would have been told the remaining barrier was unauditable when it is a public UUID regex. Third, the file-delivery blocker is finally diagnosed correctly, which matters more than it sounds: three prior cycles each reported a plausible mechanism, and each was wrong in a way that guaranteed the next cycle would re-run the same failing path. The risk I am *not* claiming as reduced is delivery itself — 21 cycles on, nothing is submitted, and treating these two artifacts as filed would be the same category of error as the file claims, one level up.
