# Unauthenticated cross-origin control of the SumUp hosted-fields card frame

**Asset:** `https://gateway.sumup.com/` (PCI card-entry iframe) -> `https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}`
**Class:** MISCONFIG / postMessage origin not validated
**Severity:** Medium (posture proven; the authorization outcome is not claimed)
**Testability:** PASSIVE for every claim below; one browser leg is stated as unproven and marked as such

---

## 1. Artifact under test

    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js -o hosted.js
    sha256sum hosted.js
    1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873   (26,839 B)

Every code citation below was read out of **this exact file** in the same session that wrote this
report. No line in this report is carried over from a prior cycle's summary.

## 2. The frame carries no framing protection

    curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ \
      | grep -icE '^(x-frame-options|content-security-policy|referrer-policy)'
    0

No `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy` (therefore no `frame-ancestors`), and no
`Referrer-Policy`. Any origin may embed the card-entry frame, and the embedding page fully
controls the `referrerpolicy` attribute of the `<iframe>` it creates. Section 6 depends on that.

## 3. The inbound channel is unauthenticated

The messenger factory accepts an `origin` parameter:

    u=({id:e,fromWindow:r,toWindow:t,variant:a,origin:n,autoMount:u=!1,...})=>{...}

The inbound handler destructures only two fields of the event:

    p=r=>{const{data:n,source:i}=r; n&&n.message && ( d(n) && (m[n.message]||[]).forEach(...) )}

`r.origin` is never read. Grep over the 26,839 B bundle:

| pattern | occurrences |
|---|---|
| `event\.origin` | **0** |
| `origin!==`      | **0** |
| `origin===`      | **0** |
| `targetOrigin`   | **0** |

The only remaining gates are four values the attacker supplies in the message body, plus a
self-echo check (`event.source` vs the stored `fromWindow`):

| gate | value | published in the public bundle |
|---|---|---|
| `data.type`    | `"SumUpCard"`   | `Object.freeze({TYPE:"SumUpCard",ACTION_MESSAGE:"message"})` |
| `data.action`  | `"message"`     | same freeze |
| `data.variant` | `"hostedfield"` | literal in the two container call sites |
| `data.message` | truthy          | protocol string list, e.g. `N="input--change", A="input--recognize", T="input--focus", I="input--blur", P="input--initialize", $="form--submit", j="form--submit-sent", L="form--on-result", C="form--on-error", R="form--invalid", z="form--invalid-input"` |

There is no origin, no allowlist, and no credential.

## 4. What an unauthenticated `form--submit` reaches

The container messenger binds the privileged action:

    function Re(e,r){
      const t = u({id:e.name, fromWindow:e, toWindow:e.parent, variant:"hostedfield",
                   origin:document.referrer, autoMount:!0, onError:...});
      return t.onAny($, (e=>{ let {payment:a, sessionId:n, product:s} = e;
        ... i.processCheckout({checkoutId:a.checkoutId, payload:u, redirectMechanism:...}) ...

The three-branch dispatch in `processCheckout` is, read literally from the bundle:

    return qe(r) ? Ee(n)
         : le(r) ? ( a && (n.sca_experience=a),
                     i && (n.product_origin=i.origin, n.session_id=i.sessionId),
                     s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r), {method:"PUT",
                       headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json",
                                "X-Sumup-Correlation-Id":r},
                       body:JSON.stringify(n)}).then(xe).then(ge).then(ve) )
         : pe(r)

So an attacker-chosen `checkoutId` and an attacker-supplied payload reach a
`PUT checkouts/{id}` on the payments API, with `session_id` and `product_origin` lifted from the
inbound message. The branch is selected by `le(r)`, the id-shape gate.

**The remaining gate is a public, static literal** (this retires an earlier claim of mine that it
was an unrecoverable runtime pattern -- it is not; that earlier claim confused this gate with the
separate card-IIN brand table `^((627416)|(637202)|(637200)|(637036)|(637201))`):

    ue = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
    le = e => ue.test(e)

## 5. The outbound channel: the guard is conditional, not absent

This is the part that changes the remediation, and it corrects a statement I made in three earlier
cycles ("the outbound direction is blocked by a bug, not a control").

There are **three** messenger construction sites, not one:

| # | site | `toWindow` | `origin` | outbound expression | delivers? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `Re` (container) | `e.parent` | `document.referrer` | `t.postMessage(e, n\|\|"*")` | **conditional** -- see below |
| 2 | `Ue` (metrics) | absent | absent | `r.parent.postMessage(e,"*")` | yes, literal `"*"` |
| 3 | `M` (frame-local) | absent | absent | `r.parent.postMessage(e,"*")` | yes, literal `"*"` |

`send`, read literally:

    send:s=>{ try{
        const e = Object.assign({variant:a, type:o.TYPE, action:o.ACTION_MESSAGE}, s);
        if (t) return void t.postMessage(e, n||"*");
        r && r.parent.postMessage(e,"*")
    }catch(r){ console.error(...), l({error:r, message:...}) }}

Messenger #1 is the only one that carries payment results -- it sends both
`j="form--submit-sent"` and, on completion, `t.send({message:L, value:e})` where `L` is
`form--on-result` and `e` is the **entire checkout result**; on a 400 it sends
`t.send({message:C, value:{message:a, status:r.status, data:r}})` with the **raw API error body**
inlined. For that one, `n` is `document.referrer` -- a full URL, not an origin -- so
`t.postMessage(e, n)` normally raises a `SyntaxError` and is swallowed by the `catch`.

**But `n||"*"`.** A full URL is a non-empty string, so the `||` does not fire and the throw happens.
An **empty** referrer is falsy, the `||` selects `"*"`, and `postMessage` accepts it. `t` is
`e.parent`, i.e. the embedding page. So the delivery of the most sensitive payloads on this channel
is governed by a single predicate, and the value it tests is controlled by the attacker via the
embedding document:

    <iframe src="https://gateway.sumup.com/?..." referrerpolicy="no-referrer"></iframe>
    -> document.referrer === "" inside the frame -> n === "" -> n||"*" === "*" -> delivers

With no `Referrer-Policy` on the response (Section 2), the embedder's own policy governs, and the
embedder is the attacker.

**What I am not claiming.** I did not observe a delivered `form--on-result` or `form--on-error`.
Confirming it needs a live checkout id, and retrieving a customer's checkout object is excluded by
the program. This leg is stated as UNPROVEN. I am not going to let a plausible code path stand in
for a measurement.

## 6. Correction of my own earlier claims, and what is *not* the finding

Written explicitly so a later reader does not re-derive them as novel:

* **Not the finding:** "response exfiltration via the postMessage API." Messengers #2 and #3 do
  have a working wildcard outbound, but the only payload either of them ever emits is
  `aux--metric-track--ack` (`Ue`) and `{message:"input--blur", value:{name:a}}` (`M`, bound to
  inbound `z="form--invalid-input"`). `{name:a}` is the field name **the attacker chose in the
  inbound message** and read back out. They therefore disclose nothing the sender did not already
  have. The prior cycle reached the right conclusion (withdraw the read primitive) for the wrong
  reason (a code bug); this is the correct reason.
* **Not the finding:** clickjacking. There is a demonstrated cross-origin message-driven effect,
  which is what makes this more than "clickjacking without demonstrated exploit".
* **Not the finding:** widget-session replay. `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` is derived in the
  first-party SDK from `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` -- a URL-borne resource identifier. A value
  named `session` in a header is not evidence that the value is a secret.

## 7. Remediation

The inbound `event.origin` check must be added to `p`, and it must land **in the same change** as
the outbound fix. Repairing messenger #1 alone is not sufficient and not safe:

* it does not close messengers #2 and #3, which already broadcast to the embedder on a literal `"*"`;
* a developer fixing the `document.referrer` bug finds `origin` in scope, already used, and
  concludes inbound is handled. The guard exists and is applied **backwards** -- `origin` is a
  declared parameter, supplied at the construction site, and consumed on exactly one path, the
  outbound.

Independently: add `frame-ancestors` to a CSP on `/` and `/hosted.js`, and set
`Referrer-Policy: same-origin` so the payload-delivery predicate in Section 5 is not
embedder-controlled. The three remaining inbound constants are published in the public bundle and
are not a control.

## 8. Verification, one command

    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum
      # 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873
    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -c 'event\.origin'   # 0
    curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ \
      | grep -icE '^(x-frame-options|content-security-policy|referrer-policy)'   # 0

The delivery leg of Section 5 needs one browser test with a real checkout session and is
deliberately left to a human who can run it under authorization.
