## 9. Addendum (same session): the write leg, resolved from source

Three further facts, all read out of the same `hosted.js` (sha256 `1302f1d6…`). Each **contradicts a
summary I had been carrying**, which is why they are recorded rather than folded silently into the
body above.

### 9.1 The write goes direct to `api.sumup.com`, not through a BFF

The bundle contains exactly one API-origin literal:

    $ grep -o 'https://[a-z0-9.-]*sumup[a-z0-9./-]*' hosted.js | sort | uniq -c
    1 https://api.sumup.com
    1 https://checkout.sumup.com
    1 https://developer.sumup.com/docs/problem/internal-server-error/
    1 https://developer.sumup.com/rest-api/problems/invalid-format
    1 https://static.sumup.com/online-payment/icons/apm/...

Endpoint builder:

    Te = (e, r, t, a, n = B) => ({ host: e, endpoint: (s, i={}) => { ... } })

so the request is `${host}/${version}/${path}` with `host = https://api.sumup.com`, and `Te` is
instantiated twice — `"v0.1"` and `"v0.2"`.

**Correction to a chain I was about to assert.** I intended to link this finding to the
`js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id}` unauthenticated-resolution finding on the theory that the frame
routes through that BFF. It does not. `js.sumup.com` is a separate, second money-touching origin
and this frame does not call it. The two findings are independent; do not merge them.

### 9.2 The widget-session header is injected unconditionally

Read literally from `Te.endpoint`:

    i.headers = Object.assign(Object.assign({}, i.headers), {"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id": r});
    t && (i.headers["Sumup-Product-Origin"] = t.origin);

The session header is written **unconditionally**, whatever `r` is — including when the caller
supplied no session at all, in which case it is the string `"undefined"`. Only the
`Sumup-Product-Origin` header is conditional. This is the "structurally void credential" shape: a
field that is always present and whose absence is indistinguishable from a specific attacker-chosen
value.

The inbound `form--submit` handler destructures `sessionId` straight out of the attacker-supplied
message (`let {payment:a, sessionId:n, product:s} = e`), so the header value is **attacker-chosen**.

I am **not** claiming the server acts on that header. Last cycle established, by a byte-identical
three-way control on the BFF, that the equivalent header is ignored there; whether
`api.sumup.com` treats it as authoritative is untested and is not testable passively. Stated as
unresolved.

### 9.3 `fe` is host-pinned, and it is the only thing bounding `product_origin`

I had assumed `fe` was a base-URL constructor. It is not — it is the widget-session extractor:

    fe = e => { try { const r = new URL(e), t = new URL("https://checkout.sumup.com");
                    return r.host === t.host
                      ? (e => { const t = e.pathname.match(/\/pay\/([^?/#]+)/)?.[1];
                                return t ? {origin:"Hosted Checkout", sessionId:t} : undefined })(r)
                      : undefined
            } catch(e){ return } }

It returns a value **only** when the frame is running on `checkout.sumup.com` and its path matches
`/pay/{id}`. The call site is `t = fe(window.location.href)`, and in `processCheckout` the product
-origin block is `i && (n.product_origin = i.origin, n.session_id = i.sessionId)`.

Consequence: when the frame is embedded by an attacker it is served from `gateway.sumup.com`, so
`fe` returns `undefined` and the request body gets **no** `product_origin` and **no** `session_id`.
The hostname check inside `fe` is therefore load-bearing — it is the one control that degrades an
attacker-originated write into a materially less-authorised one, and it holds only because the
attacker hosts the frame on a different origin than the legitimate flow.

**This narrows the finding and is the reason it stays Medium.** The attacker-originated `PUT` is
not a faithful replay of a legitimate merchant request: it arrives without `product_origin` and
without `session_id` in the body, carrying only the attacker-chosen
`X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` header. Whether the API accepts it is unproven and, on the evidence
above, less likely than the prior version of this report implied.

The origin-validation defect in Sections 3-5 is **unaffected** by this narrowing. It is a property
of the frame, not of the request's authorisation value, and it is what makes the frame driveable
from an arbitrary origin at all.

