[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`: response exfiltration, now for a defensible reason. Two of three messengers do have a working wildcard outbound — so the *channel* is open — but both emit only payloads the sender already knew (`aux--metric-track--ack`; a field name the attacker chose and reads back). A CORS/postMessage finding needs a capability differential, and here there is none in the content that flows. Recorded as explicitly NOT the finding so a later cycle cannot re-derive the same claim as novel.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ `reports/`: the write mechanism is 7 for 7, but the more useful discipline this cycle was reading the artifact before writing about it. Three separate claims in my own carry-forward — the unrecoverable runtime gate, the "only messenger" outbound, and the BFF chain I was mid-way through asserting — were each falsified by opening the file. The BFF chain is the one worth naming: I was one sentence from publishing a two-finding exploit chain that does not exist, and a single grep (`js\.sumup\.com` = 0) killed it. A proposed chain is a hypothesis like any other and owes the same control.
