# Unauthenticated, application-routed checkout resource resolution on api.sumup.com

**Asset:** `https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{checkout_id}/payment-methods`
(and version twin `/v0.1/checkouts/{checkout_id}/payment-methods`)
**Class:** IDOR / business-logic (unauthenticated resource resolution, no shape validation)
**Confidence:** 82 (surface + routing + control proven; positive case NOT proven)
**Date verified:** 2026-09-29 UTC
**Method:** read-only unauthenticated `GET` only. No credentials, no mutating request.

---

## 1. Summary

The production API gateway `api.sumup.com` is not uniformly gated. Alongside the
gateway's generic 404, a **second, application-layer 404 class exists** and it is
served by the checkout service itself, on an operation that is **absent from the
public OpenAPI specification** (`developer.sumup.com` / `github.com/sumup/sumup-openapi`
documents only the *merchant*-scoped `/v0.1/merchants/{merchant_code}/payment-methods`).

An unauthenticated request to the checkout-scoped payment-methods operation reaches
an application handler that **evaluates the supplied checkout identifier** and returns
a business-layer determination (`checkout not found`) rather than an authz denial.

## 2. How the path was found (not guessed)

Recovered from the served first-party SDK rather than by path-shape guessing:

    gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js  — 291,877 B
    sha256 0fae546ae34ee5e97cf0c3107d87adaa79129983d423bce6cf9a0fc19916c7bd

    function Fi(e,t,n=Ai(window.location.href)){
      const r=Bi(e,t,n,"v0.1",(e=>e.text())),
            i=Bi(e,t,n,"v0.2",ni);
      return { checkouts: Di(i,n), paymentMethods: Ei(i), analytics: qi(r) }
    }
    function Ei(e){ ... e.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(n,"/payment-methods")...) }

`Ei` is bound to the **v0.2 `api.sumup.com`** builder, so the client issues:

    GET https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{checkout_id}/payment-methods

Complete operation model recovered from the bundle (all previously unrecorded):

| Builder | Operation |
|---|---|
| `api` v0.2 | `PUT  /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` (processCheckout, `X-Sumup-Correlation-Id`) |
| `api` v0.2 | `PUT  /v0.2/checkouts/{id}/apple-pay-session` |
| `api` v0.2 | `GET  /v0.2/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods` (+`?hosted_checkout=true`) |
| `api` v0.1 | `POST /v0.1/internal/analytics` |
| BFF no-version | `GET  checkouts/{id}` (+`?attempts=N`) — `js.sumup.com/api` |
| BFF no-version | `POST checkouts/{id}/fee-calculation` |

## 3. Evidence — two error taxonomies on one host

Control pair, same host, same method, same status code, same path suffix:

    GET /v0.2/merchants/MH4H92C7/payment-methods
      -> 404  150 B  application/json
         {"type":"https://developer.sumup.com/docs/problem/problem/404-not-found",
          "title":"Not Found","status":404,"detail":"Resource not found"}
         ^ GATEWAY (RFC 9457 problem document)

    GET /v0.2/checkouts/1111...5555/payment-methods
      -> 404   58 B  application/json
         {"error_code":"NOT_FOUND","message":"checkout not found"}
         ^ APPLICATION (domain error code + domain message)

The 58 B body has no `type`/`title`/`status` problem-document fields and carries a
**domain-specific message naming the resource class**. It is not produced by the
gateway. This directly falsifies the long-standing characterisation of this host as
"fully gated at gateway / uniformly 404" — there are two 404 classes.

## 4. Evidence — no credential is enforced, and none is ignored

    GET /v0.2/checkouts/{uuid}/payment-methods                  -> 404  58 B "checkout not found"
    GET /v0.2/checkouts/{uuid}/payment-methods
        -H "Authorization: Bearer <any>"                        -> 500   0 B (no body)

The presence of *any* `Authorization` header changes the response class. That proves
the credential is **parsed and branched on**, not discarded — so the unauthenticated
404 is a **resolver miss inside the handler**, not an authorization denial at the edge.

An unauthenticated 500 with an empty body on a malformed-but-present bearer is a
robustness defect in its own right (unhandled token-verification failure path).

## 5. Evidence — no server-side shape validation

    GET /v0.2/checkouts/not-a-uuid/payment-methods               -> 404  58 B (identical)
    GET /v0.1/checkouts/{uuid}/payment-methods                   -> 404  58 B (identical)
    GET /v0.2/checkouts/{uuid}/payment-methods?hosted_checkout=true -> 404  58 B (identical)

Arbitrary strings are accepted and evaluated. The UUID-v1–5 gate exists **only
client-side**: `const xi=/^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i, wi=e=>xi.test(e)`
— the server imposes no equivalent constraint, so client-side validation is the only
thing standing between the internet and this resolver.

## 6. What this does NOT prove (explicit cap)

Every identifier tested returned 404. **No positive (200) response was obtained**,
because no valid checkout identifier was available. Therefore:

- **Proven:** the route is application-routed, credential-optional, shape-unvalidated,
  and its handler performs a merchant-domain resource resolution on attacker-supplied input.
- **NOT proven:** that a *valid* identifier yields 200 with payment methods, and therefore
  NOT proven that payment methods or checkout metadata are disclosed to an unauthenticated caller.
- The existence-oracle characterisation therefore rests on a **domain-specific miss
  response**, not on an observed hit/miss differential. A triager should treat the
  disclosure leg as unproven until a valid checkout id is supplied.

The sibling `js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id}` BFF endpoint (181 B RFC 9457,
`"Checkout <id> does not exist."`) shows the same client-only shape gate and the same
parameter-invariance (`?attempts=2` is byte-identical, 181 B).

## 7. Remediation

1. Apply the UUID shape constraint server-side (or reject non-conforming ids).
2. Return the gateway's generic 404 rather than a domain error that confirms the
   resource class, so the resolver cannot be distinguished from an unrouted path.
3. Require authentication on the checkout-scoped payment-methods operation, and
   authorize the checkout id **against the caller's merchant**, not merely its existence.
4. Handle token-verification failure explicitly rather than returning 500/0 B.

## 8. Out-of-scope note

`access-control-allow-origin: *` is present on this operation. It is **explicitly not
claimed as a finding**: the request path requires no credential to reach, and the
credential is header-borne only, so permissive CORS grants no capability differential.
EOF
