[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ api.sumup.com/v0.{1,2}/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods: a "uniformly gated host" claim is falsified by a control pair, not by a longer enumeration. Every path the KB probed returned the same 150 B gateway body, so "all versioned paths 404" was read as proof of an edge gate — but a new path was recovered from the client's own call graph rather than from path guessing, and it returned a different error producer on the same host, same method, same status. Generalisable rule: a uniform response across a sample proves uniformity of the sample, not of the host; and header-based attribution (`x-envoy-decorator-operation`, `problem+json`) identifies who served *that* response, not which layer owns *every* response. The path was invisible to six cycles of path-shape sweeps precisely because those sweeps guessed prefixes instead of reading `Ei`'s builder binding.
[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods: the decisive control was varying the CREDENTIAL, not the identifier. Every identifier returned 404, so identifier variation alone could never distinguish "authz denial" from "resource does not exist" — but supplying a deliberately invalid `Authorization` header produced a 500/0 B, proving the token is parsed and branched on inside the handler. A finding's shape changed from "unauthenticated endpoint that 404s" to "unauthenticated resolver that reaches a domain-level lookup". The same reasoning that killed the CORS claim (a credential differential must be demonstrated, not assumed) is what saved this one: it required a capability differential on the credential axis, and there was one.
[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ api.sumup.com/v0.1/internal/analytics: inferring an endpoint's auth posture from the client call that reaches it. The SDK posts this with only `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` and no bearer, which looks like an unauthenticated ingestion point — but `Bi` injects that header into EVERY request, money endpoints included, so its presence carries zero information about whether a credential is enforced. This is the same error as reading the postMessage `origin` parameter's presence as evidence of origin enforcement: a parameter that is supplied everywhere is not a parameter that is checked anywhere.
[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ workspace: 34 cycles of re-materialization now make the open-with-`ls` step mandatory rather than prudent — it is the only way to know which prior `[NEW]` claims, if any, refer to files that exist. The one-act write discipline held again (report 127 lines / `a551cade…`, valid-bugs.md 79→91 lines / `cd55258d…`, both hashed by the command that wrote them), and the value of a self-citing hash is now concrete rather than decorative: the next cycle can re-check delivery in one `sha256sum` instead of trusting a prose claim.

[RISK] sumup: 8 — issued 11 unauthenticated read-only GETs at >=1 s spacing plus one public static-asset fetch of the first-party `sdk.js`. No credentials, no cookies, no mutating request, no customer/financial/PII/authentication data read or touched, no auth-bypass attempt, no rate-limit interaction. One request deliberately carried a syntactically invalid `Authorization` header to test credential handling, which is a negative-control on the target's own verifier and returned an empty 500; no valid credential, valid token, or merchant account was involved.
