# iso20022.sumup.com — dual-listener routing divergence (RECON, NOT FILEABLE)

Verified 2026-09-29 08:44–08:47 UTC. Read-only GET/HEAD, no credentials, no mutating request.

## Summary

`iso20022.sumup.com` presents **two response classes from two different upstreams**
behind a single hostname. The knowledge base characterised this asset across 35 cycles
with ~1 probe, and recorded only the `istio-envoy` 400 class. The second class was
never seen.

| Path shape | Status | Server | Body | Bytes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| contains lowercase `metrics` | 503 | `awselb/2.0` | `Not Allowed` | 11 |
| everything else | 400 | `istio-envoy` | `Bad Request` | 11 |

## Rule shape, established by control

The match is a **case-sensitive substring test on the raw request path**, not a path
segment match and not an exact-path rule.

| Path | Result | Class |
|---|---|---|
| `/metrics` | 503 | awselb |
| `/metrics/` | 503 | awselb |
| `/metricsfoo` | 503 | awselb |
| `/xmetrics` | 503 | awselb |
| `/a/metrics/b/c` | 503 | awselb |
| `/internal/metrics` | 503 | awselb |
| `/prometheus/metrics` | 503 | awselb |
| `/METRICS`, `/Metrics` | 400 | istio |
| `/METRICSfoo`, `/aMETRICSb` | 400 | istio |
| `/zzzmetricezzz` (no `s`) | 400 | istio |
| `/metric`, `/prometheus`, `/health` | 400 | istio |
| `/actuator`, `/actuator/mappings`, `/v3/api-docs` | 400 | istio |

`/zzzmetricezzz` is the decisive negative control: it contains `metric` and returns
400, while `/metricsfoo` contains `metrics` and returns 503. The discriminator is
the full literal `metrics`, not a prefix.

## Upstream attribution

- istio class: `x-envoy-decorator-operation: iso20022-edge-libcluster-headless.br-terminals.svc.cluster.local:3000/*`
- awselb class: no `x-envoy-*` headers at all, `server: awselb/2.0`, no HSTS, no `cache-control`

The absence of HSTS on the 503 class and its presence on the 400 class indicates the
503 is answered by an AWS load balancer listener in front of the mesh, not by the mesh.

## Control results that closed the obvious hypotheses

- **Not CORS.** `Origin: https://evil.example` against both classes: zero
  `access-control-*` headers emitted, byte-identical responses. No capability
  differential exists, so there is no CORS finding here.
- **Not a data exposure.** Both bodies are fixed 11-byte stubs. No metric value,
  no route table, no version string is disclosed.
- **Not actuator/Spring.** 16 actuator, health, swagger and openapi paths all return
  the same 11-byte 400 stub. No Spring Boot surface is present.
- **Not staging-comparable.** `staging.iso20022.sumup.com` does not resolve (NXDOMAIN),
  so the prod/stage pair that made `pos-payment.sumup.com` interesting does not exist here.
- `iso20022-edge.sumup.com` is a distinct host: both `/` and `/metrics` return
  404 with an empty body on `awselb/2.0`.

## Why this is filed as recon and NOT a bug

The 503 is a **deny**. There is no credential, no data, no capability differential,
and no behaviour that differs between an authenticated and unauthenticated caller —
the credential axis was not varied because both classes answer before any
application logic, so there is nothing for a credential to unlock.

Reporting this as a vulnerability would be the same error already recorded twice in
this program: treating the presence of a credential parameter, or a permissive
header, as evidence that a control is missing. Here there is no control to miss.

What it does establish is a trust-boundary fact for the inventory: the ISO 20022 / SEPA
payment rail is fronted by an AWS ELB whose listener rules are evaluated **before**
the Istio mesh, so the mesh's `br-terminals` service is not the whole perimeter. Any
future test of this rail must vary the `metrics` substring to stay on the mesh path,
or it will silently measure the load balancer instead.

