[PARKED] "iso20022.sumup.com dual-listener routing" as a fileable vulnerability: not a bug, it is recon. Both classes are 11-byte denies generated at the listener before any application logic, so there is no credential to vary, no data to disclose, and no capability differential. Filing it would repeat the error this program has already recorded twice — treating the presence of a parameter or header as evidence a control is missing. Kept as a documented measurement blind spot with the control evidence attached.

[FINAL] Survivors ranked: 1) api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods unauthenticated spec-absent resource resolution (82, IDOR, AUTH_HELPED); 2) js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id} unauthenticated existence oracle (80, BUSLOGIC, AUTH_HELPED). Both are blocked on the same single missing artifact — a valid checkout identifier — and neither was re-probed this cycle. The iso20022 routing divergence is carried forward as recon, not as a finding.

[NEXT] RAG: recover a valid-format checkout identifier from public first-party sources — `github.com/sumup/sumup-php`, `sumup-go`, `sumup-dotnet` test fixtures, `developer.sumup.com` sandbox docs, and the `sumup-openapi` repo example values — then issue ONE read-only `GET https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods` to close the only unproven leg of the leading finding. This single retrieval unblocks both surviving hypotheses at once; no further probing of the byte-stable surface has any expected yield.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ iso20022.sumup.com: a routing rule is characterised by its **negative control**, not by the paths that match. I first recorded `/metrics` as a segment match, then `/metricsfoo` and `/xmetrics` falsified that, and only `/zzzmetricezzz` — a path containing `metric` and returning 400 while `metricsfoo` returns 503 — fixed the rule as a case-sensitive substring test. Generalisable: on a host with multiple listeners, the sample that matches tells you what is allowed through, and only the sample that does NOT match tells you what the rule actually keys on. Recording "segment match" from three positive results would have put a wrong rule shape into the report.

[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ iso20022.sumup.com: filing a two-class routing divergence as a vulnerability. The 503 is a deny emitted before application logic, both bodies are fixed 11-byte stubs, and an attacker `Origin` control produced zero `access-control-*` headers on both classes. A routing observation is not a security finding; it is a finding only when the two classes differ in what an attacker can reach, and here they differ in which server answers, not in what is disclosed. The honest deliverable was a recon note plus the methodological warning, and the temptation to file it was the same error already logged twice in this program on `js.sumup.com/api` and `mcp.sumup.com`.

[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ pos-payment.sumup.com: reading the artifact in full closed a lead faster than probing would have. The 3,477 B root 404 body was characterised in the KB by a quoted fragment, which invited an assumption that a payment-link form or route was reachable in it. Reading all of it showed inline SVG and two strings and nothing else — no form, no script, no canonical, no path template. The route shape is not in the served bytes, so the cheap path to that asset's link-validator surface is closed and a future cycle should not re-derive it from a quoted fragment. Same discipline that recovered the `Xi` UUID literal from `sdk.js` applies to deciding that something is absent.

[RISK] sumup: 7 — issued 19 unauthenticated read-only GETs and 1 HEAD at >= 1.1 s spacing against two in-scope SumUp hosts (`iso20022.sumup.com`, `iso20022-edge.sumup.com`), plus one DNS lookup. No credentials, no cookies, no mutating request, no customer/financial/PII/authentication data read or touched, no auth-bypass attempt, no rate-limit interaction, no scanner output relied upon. The probes were path-shape and Origin-header controls returning fixed 11-byte deny stubs; no response body contained any data of any kind.
