## Objective
- Continue the SumUp bug-bounty POC cycle for `api`, using the required eight-step structured output.
- Recover a public sandbox checkout identifier and test the two surviving unauthenticated checkout-resolution hypotheses.

## Important Details
- Current phase/target: `{"phase":"POC","target":"api"}`.
- Scope: SumUp-owned assets listed by `scope.yml` and `inventory/sumup.md`; report channel `bugs.olivermaicher.eu`.
- Live testing: passive-first, read-only `GET`/`HEAD`/`OPTIONS`, ≤1 request/second; no mutation, authentication bypass, customer data access, or scanner-only claims.
- Rejected classes include DoS, descriptive headers/errors, TLS hygiene, clickjacking without exploit, public files, and unauthenticated customer/employee/financial/authentication data exposure.
- Top survivor: `api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods` — application-routed but absent from the public OpenAPI spec; unauthenticated miss returns 58 B `{"error_code":"NOT_FOUND","message":"checkout not found"}`; invalid `Authorization` produces 500/0 B, proving credential parsing; non-UUID IDs receive the same response.
- Second survivor: `js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id}` — unauthenticated 404/181 B RFC 9457 JSON reflects the identifier; `?attempts=2` is byte-identical; no Authorization, cookie, or `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id`.
- Both survivors require a valid public sandbox checkout identifier to prove a 200/hit; disclosure remains unproven.
- `api.sumup.com/v0.1/internal/analytics` is parked at confidence 38: POST-only and the shared widget-session header is injected on every request, so client headers do not prove missing authentication.
- `iso20022.sumup.com` dual-listener behavior is recon only: paths containing case-sensitive `metrics` receive an AWS ELB 503; other paths receive fixed Istio 400 stubs, with no capability differential.
- Workspace is re-materialized between cycles. On latest cycle open, `reports/` contained only logs/hypotheses plus the 79-line baseline `reports/valid-bugs.md`; prior report claims were absent.
- Always open with `ls`; do not trust prior `[NEW]` file claims. Write and hash in one act, but expect artifacts to disappear next cycle.
