[HYP-1] Confirmed - unauthenticated resolver serves a real third party's record, 95/95
  Claim: GET /checkouts/{id} on api.sumup.com and js.sumup.com resolves live checkout sessions with no auth. The identifier is obtainable from SumUp's own published docs.
  Legit: Broken Access Control / IDOR / missing authentication on a payment-path resource. Merchant identity, settlement status, transaction references and a checkout-scoped session token disclosed to an anonymous caller.
  Illit: The 404 is an existence check, not a validation error. "Undocumented" was my error. Unauthenticated access to a public checkout is arguably by design for the payment-methods step; the js.sumup.com record and the session token are not by design.
  Discriminating test executed: valid documented id vs arbitrary UUID on the same endpoint, both versions, plus a second independent documented id. Result: 200/200/200-404. Both hypotheses separated cleanly.
  Status: PROVEN, n=1.

[HYP-2] Doc-example identifiers across the docs leak further live resources, 55/40
  Claim: the redaction failure in Defect A is not isolated. Other worked examples (artefact, refund, boleto, PIX, customer) likely also contain live IDs.
  Legit: Same defect class, same fix, expands the root-cause finding from one instance to a systemic docs-hygiene bug. Would let SumUp fix it once.
  Illit: Every other documented id I tested was dead. artefact returns gateway 404, the second checkout id 404s, the boleto token is a third-party homologation. Plausible that Create-checkout is the only example captured from a live production system; the rest may be older or synthetic. Could be a dead end that costs a lot of probe budget.
  Discriminating test: resolve each remaining documented id against its own documented endpoint, one GET each, ~10 requests, no enumeration, no non-doc ids. If 1 of 10 resolves, systemic; if 0 of 10, isolated and I stop.
  Status: OPEN - untested this cycle.

[HYP-3] The lxn_session_token authorises session continuation for the disclosed checkout, 60/25
  Claim: the unauthenticated HS256 JWT, scoped to checkout_id and lxn_session_id, would let an anonymous party continue or complete that checkout session.
  Legit: If true this converts a read-only disclosure into a payment-flow action, which is the difference between a Medium and a Critical finding. It is the single highest-leverage open question in this cycle.
  Illit: HS256 implies a shared secret I cannot obtain, so the token is likely only honoured in a context that also requires the widget session or a same-origin cookie. The token may in fact be a client-side decoration, not an authorisation artefact. Testing it means replaying a real merchant's credential against a live payment flow, which I am not entitled to do and which the program forbids.
  Discriminating test: NONE available within authorisation. Explicitly decline. Recommend the program assess it with the merchant's consent, or test against a checkout the tester owns.
  Status: PARKED by policy, not by weakness. Deliberately untested.

