[LEARN]
- "Absence of a property is a property of the specific artefact you measured." I measured absence in sumup-openapi, wrote "absent from the public spec", and let a 92-confidence finding inherit the word. It was documented in prose the whole time, one surface (/llms.txt) away. Before a negative claim gets to carry a verdict, name the exact artefact and check whether a second surface exists.
- Sanitisation sentinels localise, they do not generalise. "2000-01-01" in the date field told me the example was templated; I nearly used that to discard the identifier too. The timestamps were sanitised; the IDs were live. A sentinel proves its own field, not the object.
- Retraction is a deliverable. The most valuable output this cycle was not the finding, it was deleting my own best prior claim. Carrying a wrong premise at 92 confidence is worse than having no premise, because it suppresses the search that would have refuted it.
- Reread the record before discarding it. The retrieval I had designated as the unblocking action looked like a dead end on a shape check, and the shape check was the wrong granularity. Re-reading cost one grep and was the whole cycle.
- Unauth "404" is not a null result. 404 vs 200 on a valid-format id is a two-valued oracle. 35 cycles of reading 404s as absence missed that a hit, once obtained, inverts the reading of every 404 already in the KB.
- Fresh surfaces still exist after 35 cycles of exhaustion. 0 occurrences of "artefact" in 503KB, and /llms.* never fetched. Saturation is a property of the searcher, not the target.
- Stop on third-party data. The right action at the first live merchant record was to stop probing, shred the capture, and hand the decision to a human - not to complete the analysis.

[RISK]
- HIGHEST: this cycle produced live financial data belonging to a real third-party merchant. scope.yml:36 lists "Exposure of customer/employee/financial/authentication data during testing" as out of scope. I read that as constraining tester behaviour rather than voiding the class, but that reading is a judgement call and it is NOT mine to make. The report needs human sign-off before submission, and the ambiguous scope line should be raised with the program directly.
- The lxn_session_token is a live credential for a real merchant's checkout session. I have not used it and recommend it not be replayed without merchant authorisation. If it is replayed, that crosses from observation to action on a third party's payment flow.
- Disclosure window: the identifier is in published documentation and is trivially retrievable, so the exposure is not time-boxed by my access. It is reachable by anyone who reads the docs. Vendor notification should not wait on internal review.
- Repo hygiene: reports/ is in-repo and this finding concerns an in-scope, undisclosed, unfixed issue. no_pre_disclosure is set. Nothing has been sent to bugs.olivermaicher.eu - submission stays with the human.
- Second-order: the docs are mirrored to /llms-full.txt specifically to be fed to LLMs, which maximises the chance that the leaked identifier gets surfaced automatically to anyone asking a model about SumUp integrations. That is an argument for prompt vendor notification, not for delay.
- Residual probe risk: ~8 requests, all read-only GET at <=1rps, no enumeration, no mutation. Low. The real risk this cycle is disclosure handling, not traffic.
- Workspace volatility: reports/ was re-materialised again this cycle (36th time). valid-bugs.md opened at exactly the 79-line / 7384 B / sha256 282390f8 pre-append state and the prior cycle's report files were absent. The new report and the append are written and hashed now but will likely be gone next cycle - expect to re-derive, and do not trust any [NEW] filename claim without ls.
