# gateway.sumup.com — hosted-fields iframe accepts cross-origin postMessage with no sender-origin validation, and ships no framing protection

Asset: `https://gateway.sumup.com` (`/`, `/hosted.js`)
Class: MISCONFIG (missing sender-origin validation on a message channel)
Severity: Medium — proven control weakness on a PCI-scope frame; impact is capped, see §7.

---

## 1. Summary

SumUp's hosted-fields asset is the card-entry iframe embedded in merchant checkout pages.
It communicates with its container over `postMessage`. The inbound handler never reads
`event.origin`. The asset also ships no `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy`
`frame-ancestors`, and no `Referrer-Policy`.

The consequence is that any web origin can frame the card-entry component and drive it with
the documented message protocol, causing the frame to issue a state-changing
`PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}` with an attacker-chosen id.

The `origin` value that *would* be used for a check is a declared, supplied parameter of
the message-channel factory. It is applied to exactly one path: the outbound `postMessage`.
It is never applied inbound. See §4 — this is the most important detail in the report,
because it changes the remediation from "add a check" to "move the existing check".

## 2. Asset integrity

All code citations below are from the exact bytes measured:

    GET https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js
    HTTP 200, 26,839 bytes
    sha256 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873

Occurrence counts over that file (`grep -o | wc -l`):

| pattern | count |
|---|---|
| `postMessage` | 3 |
| `event.origin` | **0** |
| `origin!==` | **0** |
| `origin===` | **0** |
| `targetOrigin` | **0** |

The frame document:

    GET https://gateway.sumup.com/
    HTTP 200, 546 bytes
    x-frame-options / content-security-policy / referrer-policy  ->  0 occurrences

## 3. The inbound channel

Message-channel factory (`origin` is a declared parameter):

    u=({id:e, fromWindow:r, toWindow:t, variant:a, origin:n,
        autoMount:u=!1, onError:l=(...)=>{...}})=>{

Container supplies it, with the parent window as the peer:

    { ...name, fromWindow:e, toWindow:e.parent,
      variant:"hostedfield", origin:document.referrer }

Inbound handler, in full:

    const p = r => { const{data:n, source:i} = r;
                     n && n.message && ( ... ) }

The handler destructures `data` and `source` from the event. It does not destructure, read,
or compare `origin`. That is the defect: the event's sender is never identified.

Listener registration:

    mount: () => { null==r || r.addEventListener("message", p) }

## 4. Why the checks that ARE present are not sender-origin enforcement

Inbound acceptance requires (all four are literals published in the served bundle):

    d(e) = e.type === "SumUpCard"
    Boolean( d(e) && e.action === o.ACTION_MESSAGE && (e.variant === r || ignoreVariant)
             && e.message )

`type: "SumUpCard"`, `action: "message"`, `variant: "hostedfield"` and every message name
(`form--submit`, `input--initialize`, `form--on-result`, ...) ship in the public bundle, so
an attacker satisfies all four without difficulty.

There is one identity comparison in the handler:

    (t && i !== t) || <dispatch>

`t` is the configured `toWindow` (the container sets it to `e.parent`) and `i` is
`event.source`. This compares the *event source* to the *configured peer window*. For an
embedding attacker, `event.source` **is** the configured parent window, so the condition is
false and dispatch proceeds. It is a self-echo filter, not a sender check, and it exempts
precisely the party a security check would need to exclude.

Net: an attacker does not have to forge anything. It supplies the published constants and
the message is dispatched.

## 5. Outbound direction

Three `postMessage` call sites:

    sendTo: (e,r) => { ...; e.postMessage(t,"*") }                       // literal "*"
    send:   ... if(t) return void t.postMessage(e, n||"*");
                r && r.parent.postMessage(e,"*")                          // literal "*"

Two of the three deliver to a wildcard. The third uses `n`, which the container sets to
`document.referrer`. A full URL is an invalid `targetOrigin` and that call throws; an empty
referrer is falsy, so `||` selects `"*"`. The boundary is held by the *shape* of a value
rather than by a control — it is not a security property and is one refactor from changing.

No response-content exfiltration is claimed: the payloads these channels emit
(`aux--metric-track--ack`, `{message:"input--blur", value:{name:a}}`) are values the
sender already knows. See §8.

## 6. Remaining gates, and what they actually are

**(a) Frame-access gate — skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator.**

    if("card"===a.payment_type){ if(d=Ce(r.frames), !d.length) return; ... }

The "only talk to a cooperating parent" gate lives *inside* the `payment_type === "card"`
branch. Supply any other `payment_type`, or omit it, and `Ce(r.frames)` is never called and
the `return` is never reached. A guard that a caller-controlled value can switch off is not
guarding.

**(b) checkoutId shape gate — a real, static, public check.**

    ue = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
    le = e => ue.test(e)

This is a static UUID v1-v5 literal present in the served `hosted.js`. (Earlier internal
notes recorded this as an unrecoverable runtime `new RegExp(...).pattern` and one pass
retracted it entirely; both were wrong. It is a literal, and the file hash above pins it.)
It constrains the shape of the attacker-chosen id but not its ownership.

**(c) Credential — a URL-borne identifier, not a secret.**

The `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` value is parsed from a `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` URL by
a host-pinned extractor. It is a checkout id taken from a page URL. Disclosure of it crosses
no privilege boundary, so widget-session replay is NOT claimed at any severity.

## 7. Impact — proven vs not proven

Proven (from the code and from live dispatch):

* Any origin can frame the card-entry component — no framing protection of any kind.
* Any origin can dispatch the documented protocol; the sender is never identified.
* The frame will issue `PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{attacker-chosen-id}`
  with attacker-influenced content, bypassing the frame-access gate by varying
  `payment_type`.

NOT proven, and therefore not claimed:

* **That the write is accepted.** The widget-session extractor is host-pinned to
  `checkout.sumup.com` and returns `undefined` on `gateway.sumup.com`, so an
  attacker-originated request arrives without the normal `product_origin` / `session_id`
  pair. No acceptance test was performed.
* Any exfiltration of card or response data.

Severity is therefore **Medium**: a demonstrated, reproducible control weakness on a
PCI-scope component, with the authorisation outcome unestablished. It is not High.

## 8. Explicitly NOT this finding

Recorded so a later reader does not re-derive it as novel:

* **No chain to the `js.sumup.com` BFF finding.** `grep -c 'js\.sumup\.com' hosted.js` = **0**.
  `https://api.sumup.com` appears exactly **1** time. The two money findings are independent.
* **No CORS / token-theft story.** This is a `postMessage` channel, not a credentialed
  fetch. No ambient credential rides these messages.
* **No clickjacking claim** — the impact is protocol-driven, not visual.

## 9. Remediation

1. Validate `event.origin` in the inbound handler against an explicit allowlist, and reject
   on mismatch. Because `origin` is already a declared factory parameter, this is a change
   of *direction* for an existing value, not a new mechanism — but it must be made together
   with (2), or a developer repairing the outbound `targetOrigin` will find `origin` in
   scope, already used, and conclude inbound is handled.
2. Fix the outbound call to pass a real origin (`window.location.origin` or the validated
   peer) instead of `document.referrer` / `"*"`, and prefer per-message ids over wildcard
   delivery.
3. Move the frame-access gate out of the `payment_type === "card"` branch so it is not
   selectable by the caller.
4. Add `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors <merchant allowlist>` (and keep
   `X-Frame-Options` as fallback), plus a restrictive `Referrer-Policy`.

## 10. Verification (read-only, passive)

    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | sha256sum
      -> 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873
    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -o 'event\.origin' | wc -l   -> 0
    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -o 'targetOrigin'  | wc -l   -> 0
    curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ \
      | grep -icE '^(x-frame-options|content-security-policy|referrer-policy)'       -> 0
    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js | grep -o 'js\.sumup\.com' | wc -l  -> 0
EOF
