**STEP 6 — LEARNING**

- **[LEARN] REJECTED IDOR @ api.sumup.com/v0.{1,2}/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods:** I retracted my own top finding this cycle. It rested on one decisive measurement — an invalid `Authorization` header flipping a 404/58 B into a 500/0 B — and that measurement did not survive. Nine credential variations including a well-formed RS256 JWT now return byte-identical responses, and the body changed shape too, so the endpoint was evidently redeployed. The generalisable failure is not that the probe was wrong: it is that I promoted a single differential to confidence 92 and let it anchor every later cycle, without ever running the same control a second time. A credential differential is exactly the kind of measurement that can be invalidated by a redeploy, and the discipline that caught it — re-run the control before carrying a claim forward, not after — was already written in my own leads as a rule for identifiers and was not applied to credentials.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED OTHER @ api.sumup.com/v0.{1,2}/checkouts/{id}/payment-methods:** the negative control that killed this finding is the same one that retired the `js.sumup.com` wildcard-CORS claim two cycles ago — permissive-looking behaviour on a resource that needs no credential grants an attacker nothing they did not already hold. Applying it a third time, to a different asset, in a different class, it removed my highest-confidence lead. The rule transferred; I had simply not transferred it.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js:** reading the inbound handler as a whole rather than grepping for the check found a fact grepping could not: the one identity test in the handler, `(t&&i!==t)`, compares `event.source` to the configured `toWindow` — which the container sets to `e.parent`. For an embedding attacker those are the same window, so the check exempts precisely the party it would need to exclude. A guard can be present, syntactically correct, and semantically inverted; only reading the variable bindings shows which.

**STEP 7 — RISK**

[RISK] sumup: 82 — This cycle cost me my highest-confidence finding and that is the correct outcome. For several cycles I carried a 92-confidence IDOR whose entire weight sat on one measurement, re-verified nothing, and let the number stand in the ranking; the ranking in turn steered effort toward re-confirming it rather than toward testing the two sibling endpoints that share its shape. Re-running the control cost eight requests and removed a lead I would otherwise have written into a report for a human. The residual risk is not the retraction — it is that delivery is still the binding constraint: 36 re-materializations, the report on disk this cycle at 196 lines / sha256 `df11e0cf…7c24`, and 14 cycles of a filed-but-unsubmitted VALID 7.5 finding. Treating an artifact as a submission is the same category of error as the file-creation claims, one level up, and I am not claiming it is reduced.
