**STEP 6 — LEARNING**

- **[LEARN] REJECTED IDOR @ js.sumup.com/api/checkouts/{id}:** I invented this control last cycle, used it to retract my own top finding, and then immediately applied it to the second asset sharing the claim's shape — where it also came back empty. Two hosts, two independent apps, identical outcome. The transferable part is the ordering: a capability differential has to be *demonstrated* before a finding is written, and the cheap way to demonstrate it is to find the axis on which the response varies, then check whether that axis crosses a privilege boundary. On both hosts the axis that varied was routing (app 404 vs platform 404), not authorization. I had been treating "the route is real" as evidence of "the data is reachable" for cycles.
- **[LEARN] ACCEPTED IDOR @ api.sumup.com/v0.1/merchants/{code}/payment-methods:** the 200 / 46 B `{"available_payment_methods":[{"id":"card"}]}` response is identical with and without a credential, which retires it as an oracle while simultaneously confirming the merchant-code segment is not authorization-relevant on this endpoint. Worth recording because a uniform *200* is as dead as a uniform 404 — the differential, not the status code, is the finding.
- **[LEARN] REJECTED MISCONFIG @ js.sumup.com BFC id echo:** an id echoed into an RFC 9457 `detail` field under `application/json` is not injection and the content type precludes XSS. I went looking for a finding in the reflection because the reflection was the interesting-looking thing, and had to conclude the response shape is the absence of a bug.

