One correction to my own turn: I framed the two S3 siblings as a "redundancy control" for the framing claim, and on inspection that was vacuous — S3 static origins don't set frame headers by default, so 0 matches there carries no information. It neither supports nor undermines the `payout-settings-edge` control, which is the one that actually holds. I should have checked whether the control could fail before calling it a control.

## STEP 6 — LEARNING
[LEARN] ACCEPTED OTHER @ comparative controls: **a control must be able to fail.** My framing audit was built on one positive fetch (`payout-settings-edge` → `SAMEORIGIN`), and I immediately reached for two more S3 hosts as redundancy — which is exactly backwards, since a baseline control is only informative when the estate's default is known to be "no protection". The right second control is another *SumUp application origin* of comparable role, not a static bucket host. This is the mirror of the differential-matrix lesson: in a matrix the outlier is probably transient; in a control set the agreement is probably vacuous. Both failure modes are a check passing for the wrong reason.

## STEP 7 — RISK
[RISK] sumup: 80 — down from 84. The finding's evidence quality improved rather than the program's prospects: 10/10 claims now reproduce from a hashed artifact, and the comparative control holds. The remaining risk is concentrated entirely in delivery, not discovery — a complete, audited, 167-line report has now been destroyed and rewritten three times on a workspace that re-materializes every cycle, and no part of this pipeline can move it into the channel that pays.

