### RFC 8414 metadata: prod vs staging diverge on dynamic client registration (NEW, GET-only)
`GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server` on all three authorization-adjacent hosts:

| Host | code | bytes | `issuer` | `registration_endpoint` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `auth.sumup.com` (prod) | 200 | 2,175 | `https://auth.sumup.com/` | **ABSENT** |
| `auth.sam-app.ro` (staging) | 200 | 2,250 | `https://auth.sam-app.ro` | `https://auth.sam-app.ro/oauth2/register` |
| `mcp.sumup.com` | 404 | 73 | — | (resource server, not an AS — correct per RFC 9728) |

- **Scoping win for the rank-2 finding.** Staging *explicitly advertises* DCR, so `POST /oauth2/register`
  → 201 is a documented capability of that environment, not an accidental exposure. Prod does not
  advertise it. Absence of advertisement is **not** proof that prod's `/oauth2/register` is absent —
  a single POST would settle it and is out of bounds for live probing, so this remains open.
- **The severity delta is now precisely located.** Both metadata documents publish the same 19-scope
  `scopes_supported` including `merchants.write`, `payouts.read`, `refunds.write`, `api_keys.write`,
  `customers.read/write`. But DCR-minted staging clients receive tokens with an **empty** `scp`.
  **The entire severity of the staging finding is the gap between the empty granted scope and the
  advertised supported scope set.** Any path that lets a DCR-registered client obtain a non-empty
  `scp` converts a capped staging finding into a critical production-account finding. That is the one
  test worth authorizing, and it needs credentials the agent does not have.
EOF
