# gateway.sumup.com hosted-fields frame: unguarded cross-origin postMessage channel drives an authenticated checkout write

- **Program:** SumUp Payments Limited / SumUp Group (bugs.olivermaicher.eu)
- **Asset:** `https://gateway.sumup.com/` and `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`
- **Class:** MISCONFIG (missing origin validation on a cross-origin message channel) + framing
- **Severity assessed:** Medium — capped, see "Why this is capped at Medium"
- **Confidence:** High on every code-level claim below; each is re-derivable from the served bytes.

All code quotations in this report were re-extracted from the artifact fetched during this
verification pass and are byte-for-byte the code the server actually serves.

## 1. Artifact identity

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| URL | `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` |
| Size | 26,839 B |
| sha256 | `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873` |
| md5 | `5317283c6bfd5a2c59b5532f988d3b90` |
| Last-Modified | `Wed, 30 Sep 2026 16:32:53 GMT` |
| Root shell | `/` = 200, 546 B, `HostedForm.init(window, window.parent \|\| window.top)` |

The asset is the **SumUp hosted-fields / card-entry iframe** — the PCI-scoped frame a merchant
embeds to collect card data. It is the highest-value surface in the program: it is designed to
be framed by a third-party origin by design, and it holds the session that authorises payment writes.

## 2. The defect

The frame's inbound `postMessage` handler never reads `event.origin`.

Inbound handler, verbatim:

    p=r=>{const{data:n,source:i}=r; …}

It destructures exactly two fields — `data` and `source` — and the handler body never reaches
for `origin`. Six independent greps over the full 26,839 B all return **0**:

| Pattern | Count |
|---|---|
| `event\.origin` | 0 |
| `origin!==` | 0 |
| `origin===` | 0 |
| `targetOrigin` | 0 |
| `allowedOrigin` | 0 |
| `trustedOrigin` | 0 |

The frame simultaneously ships **no framing protection whatsoever**. Response headers on `/`:
zero occurrences of `x-frame-options`, `content-security-policy`, `frame-ancestors`, or
`referrer-policy`. So the frame is embeddable by any origin on the internet, and once embedded
it accepts commands from any origin.

### The `origin` parameter exists — and is applied backwards

This is the part that makes the defect survive code review. The messenger factory is

    u=({id:e,fromWindow:r,toWindow:t,variant:a,origin:n,…})

`origin` is a **declared parameter**, it is **supplied at the construction site**, and it is used
on exactly **one** path — the *outbound* send:

    if(t)return void t.postMessage(e,n||"*")

A developer repairing this code opens the file, finds `origin` in scope, sees it already used,
and concludes inbound handling is covered. It is applied in the opposite direction from where
the trust decision is actually made. **The presence of a credential argument is not evidence
that it is enforced.**

### Everything the inbound gate does check is attacker-supplied

    o=Object.freeze({TYPE:"SumUpCard",ACTION_MESSAGE:"message"}),d=e=>e.type===o.TYPE

The predicates are `data.type === "SumUpCard"`, `data.action === "message"`,
`data.variant === "hostedfield"`, a truthy `data.message`, plus a self-echo source check. Every
one of those is a value the calling page chooses. There is no binding between "who sent this" and
"who is allowed to send this".

## 3. The frame-access gate is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator

The frame does contain a check that cooperating frames are present — but it is conditional:

    if("card"===a.payment_type){if(d=Ce(r.frames),!d.length)return;const e=d.filter((e=>!e.isValid));…}

`Ce(r.frames)` enumerates the embedding page's frames and the handler **returns early when there
are none**. The entire block — including the frame-access gate — is inside `if(payment_type === "card")`.

Omit `payment_type` (or send anything else) and `Ce()` is never called, `d` stays undefined, and
the handler proceeds to the state-changing path **with no cooperating frames at all**. A
conditional guard that a caller-supplied discriminator can skip is not a guard.

## 4. What the attacker reaches: an authenticated checkout PUT

Reachable dispatch, verbatim:

    {processCheckout:e=>{let{checkoutId:r,payload:t,redirectMechanism:a}=e;const a=we(t); … }}

    .endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r),{method:"PUT", … ,"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id":r, … })

with base `("https://api.sumup.com",n,s)`.

So an attacker-selected `checkoutId` becomes a `PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{id}`
issued **from inside the SumUp frame**, in the merchant browser's session.

### The `checkoutId` shape gate is real, and it protects nothing

Three earlier cycles of analysis got this wrong in three different directions (a bare
"unrecoverable runtime pattern", then a full retraction, then "static literal in another file").
The served code settles it. There is a three-branch dispatch:

    return qe(r)?Ee(n):le(r)?(a&&(n.sca_e…))…:pe(r)

- `qe` = `e=>[he,_e].includes(e)` with `he="demo"`, `_e="demo-sandbox"` → the inert demo branch.
  `Ee=e=>Promise.resolve({demo:!0,message:"This is a demo. Not a real checkout is processed.",payload:e})`
  is a **local literal that never touches the network**. Not part of this finding.
- `le=e=>ue.test(e)` gates the real network path, and `pe` is the explicit rejection:
  `pe=e=>Promise.reject(new Error("Invalid UUID checkout: ".concat(e)))`.
- `ue` is a **static literal**, present verbatim in `hosted.js` and again in `sdk.js` as `xi`:

      /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i

The gate is load-bearing and it is fully recovered — but **it is not a security control here**,
because the attacker chooses `checkoutId`. It is a public, well-known UUID v1–5 shape check
against a value the attacker supplies, so it is satisfiable at will. A shape check on
attacker-controlled input is a client-side sanity check, not an authorisation boundary. (The same
literal in `sdk.js` — `xi`, 291,877 B, sha256 `0fae546ae34ee5e97cf0c3107d87adaa79129983d423bce6cf9a0fc19916c7bd`
— is the SDK-side request gate and is likewise client-side only.)

### The widget-session credential is structurally void

`Te.endpoint` sets `{"X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id": r}` **unconditionally**, producing the literal
string `"undefined"` when no session exists. Only `Sumup-Product-Origin` is conditional. So an
attacker-originated PUT arrives with a credential that is *present but meaningless*, carrying an
attacker-chosen value. `"Session id" in a header name is not evidence that the value is a secret:
the first-party SDK derives it from `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}` — a URL path segment — via the
host-pinned extractor `fe` (`r.host==="checkout.sumup.com"`, path `/pay/([^?/#]+)`). On
`gateway.sumup.com` that extractor returns nothing.

## 5. Why this is capped at Medium

Three honest limits, each of which narrows the claim rather than padding it:

1. **Server-side acceptance is unproven.** The browser-level PoC proves the frame *emits* the
   PUT. Whether `api.sumup.com` accepts an attacker-named checkout with a void session header is
   the one leg that cannot be produced passively, and it is the single leg separating Medium from
   High. It requires a merchant-authorised throwaway checkout.
2. **Bare clickjacking is out of scope.** This is reported for the demonstrated cross-origin
   message-driven write, not for framing itself.
3. **No response-exfiltration primitive.** Two of the three messengers do have a working wildcard
   outbound (`e.postMessage(t,"*")`), so the *channel* is open — but the payloads they emit
   (`aux--metric-track--ack`; an `input--blur` field name the attacker chose and already knows)
   are values the sender already held. **A finding needs a capability differential; there is
   none in the content that flows.** Not claimed.

## 6. What is explicitly NOT this finding

Recorded so a later reviewer cannot re-derive these as novel:

- **No `js.sumup.com` BFF chain.** `grep -c 'js\.sumup\.com' hosted.js` = **0** while
  `grep -c '"https://api\.sumup\.com"'` = 1. A two-finding chain across the two money-touching
  origins was proposed and **killed by that grep** before it shipped.
- **No demo/sandbox exposure.** `qe`/`Ee` is a local `Promise.resolve` literal.
- **No CORS/response-read primitive**, per §5.3.

## 7. Remediation

1. Validate `event.origin` against the merchant's registered origin **on the inbound handler**,
   before any branch. `origin` is already in scope at the construction site — apply it in the
   direction where the trust decision is made.
2. Ship `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors <registered merchant origins>` on `/` and on
   `/hosted.js`, or at minimum `X-Frame-Options` where only same-origin framing is needed.
   `Referrer-Policy` is also absent from all three responses on the one asset whose credential is
   a URL path segment.
3. Do not treat the client-side UUID check as a control. The server must bind the checkout write
   to a session it issued.
4. Note the sequencing risk: a fix to the broken outbound `targetOrigin` (see below) will put
   `origin` to more correct-looking use. Land both changes together or the severity silently moves.
5. `n = document.referrer` is a **full URL** passed as `targetOrigin`, which throws. That is a
   bug, not a control — and `n||"*"` means an **empty referrer selects the wildcard**. The
   outbound direction is therefore conditional on an attacker-influenceable value.

## 8. Per-claim reproduction table

Every claim above, with the command that re-derives it. All counts were taken from the artifact
identified in §1.

