**Verified:** 2026-10-01, sha256 `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873`, md5 `5317283c6bfd5a2c59b5532f988d3b90`
**Class:** MISCONFIG / OATH-postMessage trust boundary — missing origin validation on a state-changing message channel
**Severity:** Medium (capped — see §6)

---

## 1. Summary

`gateway.sumup.com` is SumUp's PCI card-entry iframe. The merchant page embeds it and drives
it over `window.postMessage`. The inbound message handler never reads `event.origin`, and the
frame ships no framing protection. Any origin can therefore frame the card-entry component and
post the submit message, causing the frame to issue a state-changing write to the merchant API
from the browser session of whoever framed it.

This is not a bare clickjacking finding. It is an unauthenticated **message-channel** control
failure with a demonstrated API call behind it.

---

## 2. Affected component

`gateway.sumup.com/` serves a minimal shell (546 B) whose only job is to initialise the
component against its embedder: `HostedForm.init(window, window.parent || window.top)`.

Framing protection on `/`:

| header | present |
|---|---|
| `X-Frame-Options` | no |
| `Content-Security-Policy` (incl. `frame-ancestors`) | no |
| `Referrer-Policy` | no |

Count of framing/referrer headers on `/` = **0**. Note this is a direct contrast with
`payout-settings-edge.sumup.com`, which does send `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`.

---

## 3. The defect — inbound direction is unguarded

Greps over the served `hosted.js` (26,839 B), all returning **0**:

```
grep -cE 'event\.origin'      = 0
grep -cE 'origin!=='          = 0
grep -cE 'origin==='          = 0
grep -cE 'targetOrigin'       = 0
grep -cE 'allowedOrigin'      = 0
grep -cE 'trustedOrigin'      = 0
```

The inbound handler destructures only `data` and `source`:

```js
p = r => { const { data: n, source: i } = r; … }
```

It compares `event.source` (which window sent it) but never `event.origin` (which origin sent it).
Every gate it does apply is attacker-controlled:

| gate | attacker can control? |
|---|---|
| `data.type === "SumUpCard"` | yes — a string in a JSON message |
| `data.action === "message"` | yes |
| `data.variant === "hostedfield"` | yes |
| `data.message` truthy | yes |
| self-echo suppression | yes — control the iframe's own message id |

**Why this is a real control and not a hardening gap:** the `origin` parameter exists in this
codebase and *is* used — but only in the **outbound** direction. The messenger factory is
`u = ({ id: e, fromWindow: r, toWindow: t, variant: a, origin: n, … })` and the send is
`t.postMessage(e, n || "*")`. A developer repairing anything in this area will find `origin`
in scope, already used, and reasonably conclude inbound is handled. The guard is present and
applied backwards.

---

## 4. Reaching the state-changing call

Two conditions gate the write.

**(a) The frame-access gate is skippable.** For `payment_type === "card"` the code requires
cooperating frames. That entire branch is inside:

```js
if("card"===a.payment_type){ … }
```

Supply any other `payment_type` (or omit it) and the guard never executes — the frame will
proceed with no cooperating frames at all. *A conditional guard that a caller-supplied
discriminator can skip is not a guard.* The happy-path value must never be the only value tested.

**(b) A shape gate does exist and is not skippable.** The checkout id is checked against a
static literal regex present in the bundle:

```js
ue = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
le = e => ue.test(e)
```

This is the honest remaining barrier: the attacker-chosen checkout id must be a well-formed
UUID (any version 1–5). It is a shape check, not an authorisation check — it constrains the
*form* of an identifier, never whether the caller may use it.

---

## 5. What the frame then does

On `form--submit` the frame issues:

```
PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{attacker-chosen-uuid}
```

with a caller-supplied session identifier and payment payload.

`Te.endpoint` sets `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` **unconditionally** (literally the string
`"undefined"` when no value is present); only `Sumup-Product-Origin` is conditional.

**The credential is structurally void, not merely absent.** `fe` is not a base-URL builder — it
is a host-pinned widget-session extractor requiring `r.host === "checkout.sumup.com"` and path
`/pay/{id}`. On `gateway.sumup.com` it returns nothing, so an attacker-originated PUT arrives
with **no `product_origin` and no `session_id`**. It is therefore not a faithful replay of a
merchant session. Disclosure of the session id is *not* the finding: the id is a URL path
segment of `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}`, so disclosing it crosses no privilege boundary.

---

## 6. Severity — capped at Medium, with the cap stated explicitly

Not claimed:
- **Exfiltration of the API response.** Two of three messengers do have a working wildcard
  outbound, but the payloads they emit are ones the sender already knows (`aux--metric-track--ack`;
  an `input--blur` echoing a field name the attacker chose). No capability differential, so no
  read primitive. Recorded here so it is not re-derived as novel.
- **A working two-hop chain with `js.sumup.com/api`.** I was one step from asserting this and
  killed it myself: `grep -c 'js\.sumup\.com' hosted.js` = **0**, `grep -c 'https://api\.sumup\.com'` = 1.
  The chain does not exist in the served bytes.
- **Server-side acceptance.** Not demonstrated. See §7.

**The single untested leg is server-side acceptance of the emitted PUT on a throwaway
checkout.** That is what separates Medium from High, and it cannot be produced without a live
merchant session.

---

## 7. Reproduction

Step 1 — confirm the artifact and the missing controls:

```bash
curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js -o hosted.js
sha256sum hosted.js   # 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873
grep -cE 'event\.origin' hosted.js   # 0
curl -sS -D- -o /dev/null https://gateway.sumup.com/ | grep -icE 'x-frame-options|content-security-policy'   # 0
```

Step 2 — frame it from an arbitrary origin (self-signed cert on localhost is sufficient):

```html
<iframe src="https://gateway.sumup.com/"></iframe>
<script>
  const f = document.querySelector('iframe');
  f.contentWindow.postMessage(
    { type:"SumUpCard", action:"message", variant:"hostedfield", message:"form--submit",
      payload:{ payment:{ checkoutId:"11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555" } } },
    "*"
  );
</script>
```

Step 3 — observe the `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` in the network log of the frame.

Step 4 (**requires a human with a merchant session**) — repeat with a real, well-formed
checkout id belonging to the logged-in merchant's own session and observe whether the server
accepts the write. This step is the open question and the reason severity is capped.

---

## 8. Remediation

1. Validate `event.origin` against an explicit allowlist in the inbound handler, before any
   other gate. This is the primary fix.
2. Ship framing protection on the frame (`Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors ...`).
3. Make the frame-access gate unconditional — do not key it on a caller-supplied
   `payment_type` discriminator.
4. Fix the outbound `targetOrigin` **together with** the inbound check. Outbound delivery is
   currently broken by `origin: document.referrer` (a full URL, which throws). If inbound
   validation lands and outbound is later "repaired" independently, the risk profile changes;
   the two should be one change.
5. Treat `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` as non-authoritative. It is set unconditionally and is
   attacker-chosen; authorisation must come from the session itself.

---

