- Following a self-advertised citation works where guessing paths failed: one never-fetched
  `resource_documentation` URL opened an entire unmapped surface, and `/tools/llms/mcp-server/`
  revealed the undocumented `/sse` route plus four staging hosts via `wrangler.jsonc`.
- Public source review can *eliminate* hypotheses as fast as it creates them. Reading
  `worker.ts` killed my own "no scope enforcement" theory in one pass and prevented a wrong report.
- Diffing staging against prod is the highest-yield control here: it confirmed the DCR finding
  is precisely staging-only while disproving two plausible-sounding over-permission claims
  (`"none"` auth method and `api_keys.*` are prod parity).
- Vendor security documentation can supply the missing "intended control" that turns hardening
  observations into triageable findings — the origin allowlist reframing came from SumUp's own skill.
- Published digests are worth verifying but are not a default vulnerability: all 6 matched, so
  the honest outcome was a clean integrity result and a "do not re-probe" note.

