cat > reports/gateway-hostedfields-cross-origin-messenger.md <<'REPORT_EOF'
# SumUp Hosted Fields gateway accepts unvalidated cross-origin `postMessage` commands

Asset: `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` (and the widget frames it mounts)
Class: CWE-346 Origin Validation Error -> CWE-862 Missing Authorization (CSRF-style)
Status: PoC confirmed client-side; server-side acceptance pending (Medium)

## Artifact under test (re-verified this session, byte-identical)

| File | Bytes | sha256 |
|---|---|---|
| `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` | 26839 | `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbe0281e4b977` |
| `https://gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` | 291877 | `0fae546ae34ee5e97cf0c3107d87adaa79129983d423bce6cf9a0fc19916c7bd` |

## Root cause (quoted from served bytes)

The messenger installs exactly one window listener:

    mount: () => { null == r || r.addEventListener("message", p) }

and the handler destructures **only `data` and `source`** — `event.origin` is never read anywhere
in the bundle:

    p = r => {
      const { data: n, source: i } = r;
      n && n.message && (
        d(n) && (m[n.message] || []).forEach(...),      // <-- onAny registry: NO source check
        t && i !== t || (c[n.message] || []).forEach(...)  // <-- variant registry: partial check
      )
    }

Operator precedence binds `&&` before `||`, so the expression is
`((t && i !== t) || (c[n.message] || []).forEach(...))`. Two consequences:

1. **`event.origin` is never validated.** There is no allowlist, no same-origin test, and no
   helper that performs one. Confirmed absent: `isTrustedOrigin`, `validOrigin`, `checkOrigin`,
   `sameOrigin`, `allowedOrigins` (0 occurrences each).
2. **The `onAny` registry (`m`) is dispatched unconditionally**, before and outside the only
   source check. The variant registry (`c`) is gated on `source === t`, and only when a target
   frame `t` is configured.

The money-handling command is registered in the **ungated** registry:

    return t.onAny($, (e => {
      let { payment: a, sessionId: n, product: s } = e;
      ...
      i.headers["Sumup-Product-Origin"] = t.origin
      ... i && (n.product_origin = i.origin, n.session_id = i.sessionId)
      s.endpoint("checkouts/".concat(r), { method: "PUT", headers: ... })

`$` is `form--submit` (`$="form--submit"`). The `payment`, `sessionId` and `product` values that
become the `PUT` body and the `Sumup-Product-Origin` / `session_id` fields are taken directly
from the untrusted message. The only shape validation is `d = e => e.type === "SumUpCard"`
(and `e.action === "message"`).

## Why this is reachable

The widget root sets no framing protection, so any third-party origin can embed it:

- `GET https://gateway.sumup.com/` -> `200`, no `X-Frame-Options`
- no `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors`
- no `Referrer-Policy`

An attacker page therefore obtains a live `Window` handle to the gateway frame and dispatches:

    frame.contentWindow.postMessage(
      { type: "SumUpCard", action: "message", variant: "hosted-fields",
        message: "form--submit", value: { payment: <...>, sessionId: <...>, product: { origin: "..." } } },
      "*")

Because `event.origin` is never inspected, the frame cannot tell this apart from its legitimate
parent. The SDK then issues:

    PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{checkoutId}

with attacker-chosen session and payment parameters.

Outbound leakage is secondary but real: `postMessage(t,"*")` and `postMessage(e,"*")` broadcast
widget state to `"*"`, and one messenger variant targets `document.referrer`'s full URL as
`targetOrigin` (invalid — the message is dropped) with a `"*"` fallback when referrer is empty.

## Impact

An attacker-controlled origin that can host or embed the widget can drive a checkout-write API
call through the victim's browser session, using attacker-supplied `sessionId` and `payment`
parameters. In a checkout/Shared-Checkout flow this is the primitive for forcing payment state on
someone else's order.

**Bounded, deliberately.** This report claims only the verified client-side behaviour plus the
observed outbound request. It does **not** claim:
- that the API accepts the emitted `PUT` (unproven; requires a human-operated valid checkout),
- any read access to victim payment data,
- any self-XSS-only path.

Because server-side acceptance is unproven, severity is **Medium**, not High. If a valid widget
session is accepted, this escalates — that test is the open item below.

## Open item / next step (human, ~10 minutes)

1. Load a throwaway SumUp checkout on a valid test/sandbox checkout ID.
2. From an unrelated origin, embed the gateway frame and send the `form--submit` message above
   using the checkout's own session ID.
3. Record whether the API returns 2xx for the resulting `PUT`.

I did not perform this step: it requires a valid checkout session and would write to a live
checkout, which the testing rules forbid without human authorization.

## Scope notes

- `gateway.sumup.com` is SumUp-owned and in scope.
- All interaction was passive/`GET`-only; no request was made that mutates customer data.
- Related but **not** claimed: framing alone is out of scope per program policy, and this report
  exists solely because of the missing origin validation plus attacker-controlled parameters.
