## Summary

The SumUp Hosted Fields script `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` (PCI card-entry iframe bundle) implements a postMessage messenger that **never validates `event.origin` on inbound messages**. The messenger also configures outbound postMessage targets with a fallback to `"*"` in multiple code paths, while the host page uses `document.referrer` as `targetOrigin` in one container path (which can throw on full URLs) and two other messenger sites use literal `"*`. These behaviors create an asymmetric origin enforcement posture where the receiver cannot distinguish messages from trusted SumUp origins vs any attacker-controlled origin able to frame or reach the hosted fields context in certain deployment patterns.

## Key evidence (served bytes, verified)

- Resource: `GET https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`
- Size: 26839 bytes (verified live)
- SHA256: `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873`
- Date verified: 2026-10-03 (current session)

Notes on verification: bytes are stable across cycles; verification performed via live GET in the current context per workspace rules (read-only).

## Code-level observations (from hosted.js)

1. **Inbound postMessage handler never checks `event.origin`**
- The bundle registers a postMessage listener for the hosted fields messenger. The handler destructures `{data, source}` only (no access to `event.origin` as part of an inbound validation branch) in the messenger implementation used by hosted.js. The receiver processes `data` based on message type without any origin guard.
- Impact: Any script running in any origin that can postMessage to the window containing the hosted fields (including cross-origin iframes or windows reached in certain topologies) can deliver messages to the hosted fields messenger receiver; the receiver has no basis to reject untrusted origins.

2. **Outbound targetOrigin applied only on send; fallback to `"*"` exists**
- When sending messages via `postMessage`, the implementation uses `t.postMessage(e, n || "*")` (outbound uses `n||"*"`) in the messenger paths observed in hosted.js. 
- One messenger site (container) uses `document.referrer` as the computed `targetOrigin` (`n=document.referrer`), which can be problematic (full URL forms may not be a valid targetOrigin string in some cases and throws in certain browsers in that code path), while two other messenger sites use literal `"*"`. 
- This is directionally asymmetric: trust is asserted outbound but never enforced inbound.

3. **Framing headers and deployment context**
- Hosted fields are served from `gateway.sumup.com` and embedded as PCI card-entry iframe in SumUp checkout contexts. Observed deployment: checkout pages PUT cross-origin in netlog traces (payment flow orchestration crosses origins). The absence of framing controls in the bundle surface combined with no inbound origin check is the core structural defect noted.
- The checkout/session validation path uses a static UUID v1–5 regex for `checkoutId` (`/^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i`) and the `payment_type !== "card"` branch skips the frame-access gate — these are separate gating details; the messenger origin-validation gap remains independent.

4. **Shared messenger code in sdk.js (generalizes concern)**
- `gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` (291,877 B, sha256 `0fae546ae34ee5e97cf0c3107d87adaa79129983d423bce6cf9a0fc19916c7bd`) shares the same messenger factory. It contains two `onAny` subscriptions (`confirmation-success`, `confirmation-fail`) that take the no-source-check branch. While not the same hosted fields receiver, this indicates the messenger pattern is reused without inbound origin validation across bundles.

## Reproduction table (per-claim, passive evidence)

| Claim | Evidence source | Method | Observation |
|---|---|---|---|
| hosted.js served bytes | `https://gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` (live GET) | passive byte fetch, sha256sum | 26839 bytes; sha256 `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873` |
| No inbound origin check in messenger | served hosted.js (static structure) | static analysis of messenger code paths (destructures `{data,source}` only; no `event.origin` guard) | inbound validation branch absent in the messenger used by hosted.js |
| Outbound uses `n||"*"`, asymmetric enforcement | served hosted.js | static analysis | `t.postMessage(e,n||"*")`; container uses `document.referrer` as targetOrigin (path-dependent); two other sites use literal `"*"`. |
| sdk.js shares pattern | `https://gateway.sumup.com/sdk.js` | passive byte inspection | same messenger factory; `onAny` subscriptions `confirmation-success`, `confirmation-fail` take no-source-check branch |
| Checkout flow origin-crossing observed | checkout PUT/netlog (passive observation context) | passive network log context | checkout orchestration crosses origins; hosted fields embedded as iframe in that context |

## Impact assessment

The absence of inbound `event.origin` validation on the hosted fields postMessage receiver means the receiver cannot authenticate the sender of messages in cross-origin scenarios. Combined with outbound fallback to `"*"`, the trust boundary is enforced only in one direction. In general, postMessage-based channel security requires validating `event.origin` on all inbound messages and using a strict `targetOrigin` on outbound sends; the observed pattern is structurally vulnerable to cross-origin message injection if an attacker can deliver crafted messages to the hosted fields context under a compatible embedding.

However, this is a **design-level origin-handling defect** in the messenger. Per the SumUp program rules, exploitation that requires mutating tests against live customer data or active exploitation of payment flows is not performed. This report documents the structural defect with concrete, passive, verifiable evidence (served bytes + static code structure).

## Methodology & compliance

- **Passive-first:** Only GET/HEAD/OPTIONS and static analysis performed. No active exploitation, no mutating requests, no auth-assisted active probing beyond passive documentation. 
- **Rate/verbs:** ≤1 rps, GET/HEAD/OPTIONS only. 
- **Evidence:** All claims traceable to concrete read-only evidence (file paths, URLs, byte counts, sha256s). 
- **Scope:** Gateway assets under `gateway.sumup.com` (SumUp-owned). 
- **Submission channel:** `bugs.olivermaicher.eu` (per `scope.yml:4`).

