712:- 2026-09-27 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `js.sumup.com/api`: the whole finding came from **recovering the call shape instead of guessing path shapes**. Six cycles closed this origin with a 6-shape prefix sweep; the real paths were sitting in `sdk.js` and the sweep was structurally incapable of hitting them, because the client only issues the request when the id matches a UUID regex. The same shape of error the KB already recorded for the legacy-callback sweep — *an exhaustive sweep is only exhaustive over the values actually sent* — had simply been repeated on a different asset, by me, and the general lesson had not transferred. Two disciplines that paid off here are both about checking a citation against the artifact: `hosted.js` was credited with an `apiBFF` reference it does not contain, and a `checkoutId` pattern three cycles had recorded as unrecoverable is a static literal in a different file on the same host.
726:- 2026-09-27 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`: the finding sharpened because I read the *parameter* rather than searching for the *check*. Three cycles framed this as "no `event.origin` validation" — true but flat, and it invites the response "just add a check." The stronger fact is that `origin` is a declared parameter, is supplied at the construction site, and is used on exactly one path: the outbound. The guard exists and is applied backwards, which is why it survived review and why the fix is non-obvious — a developer repairing the broken outbound `targetOrigin` will find `origin` in scope, already used, and conclude inbound is handled. Generalisable rule: when auditing a message channel, check which *direction* each credential parameter is applied in; presence of a credential argument is not evidence of enforcement.
727:- 2026-09-27 REJECTED OTHER @ `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`: the "unrecoverable runtime pattern" gate. Three cycles recorded the checkoutId check as `new RegExp(a.pattern)` and therefore unauditable, and one retracted it entirely as non-reproducing. Both were wrong in the same way — neither opened the file and read what the constructor was building. It is the card-IIN brand table (`627416`/`637202`/…). The actual gate is a static literal. The cost of that error was not merely an inaccurate note: carried into a report, it would have told a triager the remaining barrier is unrecoverable when it is in fact a public, well-known UUID v1–v5 regex. Reading the code around a citation is the only thing that catches this, and re-deriving a claim from a prior cycle's summary never will.
730:- 2026-09-27 ACCEPTED MISCONFIG @ `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js`: three prior cycles characterised the message channel from the container messenger alone and generalised from it, producing a claim ("outbound blocked by a bug") that was true of one of three call sites. Reading the *set* of construction sites — rather than the one already under discussion — falsified it, and simultaneously revealed the real shape: the guard is `n||"*"`, a predicate that an empty referrer defeats, and the value feeding it is supplied by the embedding page. Generalisable rule: when a control is described as broken or absent, enumerate every site that could carry it before characterising the channel; a single call site is a sample, not the surface. The same pass killed a second error — I had assumed `fe` was a base-URL constructor, and it is a host-pinned extractor whose hostname check is the only thing bounding the attacker's request body.
