# SumUp — unauthenticated RFC 7591 dynamic client registration on auth.sam-app.ro

- **Status:** ready to file
- **Report channel:** bugs.olivermaicher.eu (`scope.yml:4`)
- **Class:** AUTH / OAuth configuration
- **Severity:** Low–Medium (staging-only, blast radius bounded — see Impact)
- **Evidence basis:** discovery re-derived passively (GET) on 2026-10-06 UTC;
  registration behaviour verified live (POST) in the 2026-09-06 cycle

## Target

`https://auth.sam-app.ro` — SumUp's publicly reachable staging authorization
server (staging stack: `mcp`, `mcp-theta`, `api`, `api-theta`, `auth`,
`auth-theta`).

## The bug

RFC 7591 Dynamic Client Registration is enabled and reachable without
authentication, and it is advertised in the OIDC discovery document.

Re-derived this cycle (GET only):

```
GET https://auth.sam-app.ro/.well-known/openid-configuration   → 200
  registration_endpoint : https://auth.sam-app.ro/oauth2/register
  scopes_supported      : 22 entries
    offline_access, members.read/write, merchants.read/write,
    roles.read/write, checkouts.read/write, customers.read/write,
    api_keys.read/write, receipts.read, refunds.write,
    transactions.read, payouts.read, readers.read/write,
    sales.read, subscriptions.read/write
  authorization_endpoint: https://auth.sam-app.ro/oauth2/auth

GET https://auth.sumup.com/.well-known/openid-configuration     → 200
  registration_endpoint : (absent)
  scopes_supported      : 5 entries

GET .../oauth2/register → 404 on BOTH prod and staging (registration is
                          POST-only; 404 on GET is expected, not a mitigation)
```

The prod/staging divergence is the finding: `registration_endpoint` is present
only on the staging authorization server, and the staging server will actually
mint credentials for an anonymous requester.

## Verified behaviour (live POST, 2026-09-06 cycle)

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `POST /oauth2/register` unauthenticated | **201** with `client_id` + `client_secret` + attacker-chosen `redirect_uris` |
| Scope at registration | Forced to **empty** — requesting `openid` → `invalid_scope` "exceeds allowed scopes" |
| PKCE | Required (`code_challenge`) |
| Redirect allowlist | Enforced per client — unrelated `redirect_uri` → `401` |
| RFC 7592 management (GET/PUT/DELETE client) | **404** — registered clients cannot be inspected or revoked by the registrant |
| Sync to `api.sam-app.ro/authorize` | **Not synced** — `invalid_client`, so the dynamic client cannot pass the gateway |
| `POST /oauth2/token` (`client_credentials`, `client_secret_post`) | Mints a **real JWT** with empty `scp` and attacker-controlled `aud` |
| Gateway acceptance of that JWT | Accepted structurally (problem+json vs plain 404) but **empty scope blocks all resource access** |
| `token_endpoint_auth_method` | Stored, **not enforced** at the token endpoint (REJECTED as a separate finding) |

Discovery also declares 22 `scopes_supported` including `*.write` scopes —
those are never grantable to a dynamically registered client (registration
forces empty scope), so the escalation path stops there.

## Impact

- An anonymous party can create an unlimited number of real OAuth clients on a
  SumUp-hosted authorization server and mint valid, gateway-accepted JWTs at
  will, with no registration barrier and no per-client management endpoint.
- **Bounded blast radius (stated honestly):**
  - tokens carry empty `scp`, so no staging API resource is reachable;
  - clients are not synced to `api.sam-app.ro/authorize`, so they cannot pass
    the staging gateway's authorization endpoint;
  - PKCE and a per-client redirect allowlist are enforced;
  - no RFC 7592 endpoint exists, so a registrant cannot enumerate or delete
    what they created.
- **Why it is still worth reporting:** unauthenticated client registration on a
  company-hosted AS is a configuration defect regardless of current scope
  blocking; it is one config change (granting a non-empty default scope, or
  syncing clients to the gateway) away from mintable, resource-bearing tokens,
  and it gives an attacker an unbounded client-credential factory on SumUp
  infrastructure. Staging environments routinely share config with prod.

## Not claimed

- Prod `auth.sumup.com` does **not** expose registration (discovery omits
  `registration_endpoint`; `/register` 404 on GET/POST/OPTIONS in earlier
  cycles). This finding is staging-only.
- No scope escalation, no gateway resource access, and no token replay against
  production were demonstrated.
- `token_endpoint_auth_method` non-enforcement is recorded in the KB as
  REJECTED (server stores the preference but does not enforce it) and is not
  part of this report.

## Remediation

1. Disable dynamic client registration on `auth.sam-app.ro`, or gate it behind
   authentication/allowlisting, so staging mirrors prod
   (`registration_endpoint` absent).
2. If DCR must remain for testing, keep the empty-scope default, drop the
   `*.write` entries from staging `scopes_supported`, and never sync dynamic
   clients to `api.sam-app.ro/authorize`.
3. Add RFC 7592 management (or an operator-side revocation path) so registered
   clients are not orphaned.

## Scope note

In scope per `scope.yml`. `auth.sam-app.ro` is SumUp Group company-owned
staging infrastructure. Passive-only rule respected in this cycle: only
GET/HEAD/OPTIONS were issued; the 201 response cited above is prior-cycle live
evidence, not a request made now. No customer data was touched.

