# gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js — Cross-Origin postMessage Checkout Write

**Asset:** `gateway.sumup.com/hosted.js` (PCI card-entry iframe)  
**Class:** MISCONFIG — missing `event.origin` validation on inbound `postMessage`; no framing protection  
**Severity:** Medium (CSRF-like checkout manipulation via framable PCI component)  
**Confidence:** 90  
**Testability:** PASSIVE (static analysis + live iframe PoC); AUTH_HELPED for full payment flow validation  
**Reported:** 2026-10-06  
**Artifact hash:** `hosted.js` sha256 `1302f1d6a8fa330a71e50647be0281e4b977a198cbf27e0fd59cbecc9f220873` (26,839 B)

---

## 1. Summary

The SumUp hosted-fields iframe (`gateway.sumup.com/`) accepts cross-origin `postMessage` commands that drive a state-changing `PUT /v0.2/checkouts/{id}` on `api.sumup.com`. The inbound message handler **never reads `event.origin`**. The frame ships **no `X-Frame-Options`, no `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors`, no `Referrer-Policy`** — it is framable by any origin.

An attacker can embed the frame, send a crafted `form--submit` message with `payment_type !== "card"` (which bypasses the frame-access gate entirely), and cause the frame to issue a checkout PUT with an attacker-chosen `checkoutId` and payment payload.

